(window.__DP=window.__DP||[]).push("in labour. Frank breech (50-70%): hips flexed, knees extended; complete breech (5-10%): hips and knees flexed; incomplete or footling (10-30%): one or both hips extended. Prematurity is listed as a fetal risk factor for malpresentation.\",\"s\":[[\"L16_0\",[14,16,17]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F280\"},{\"id\":\"F281\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"soul\",\"g\":4,\"L\":18,\"ty\":[\"Complication\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Maternal and fetal complications of PPROM\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Regarding preterm prelabour rupture of membranes (PPROM), which statement is false?\",\"o\":[\"Severe oligohydramnios after very early PPROM can lead to fetal pulmonary hypoplasia\",\"PPROM is characteristically associated with fetal structural cardiac defects\",\"Prolonged oligohydramnios can cause positional skeletal deformities such as talipes\",\"PPROM increases the risks of chorioamnionitis, placental abruption, cord prolapse, and preterm birth\",\"Diagnosis begins with sterile speculum examination for amniotic-fluid pooling; biomarker testing can be used when pooling is not seen\"],\"a\":1,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"PPROM with persistent severe oligohydramnios early in gestation can impair lung expansion and cause pulmonary hypoplasia.\",\"PPROM is linked chiefly to prematurity, infection, abruption, cord complications, pulmonary hypoplasia, and deformational sequelae, not a characteristic pattern of fetal cardiac malformations.\",\"Prolonged oligohydramnios restricts fetal movement and can produce positional deformities, including talipes equinovarus.\",\"Loss of membrane integrity increases the risks of ascending infection, placental abruption, cord prolapse, and spontaneous preterm birth.\",\"Current diagnostic pathways use sterile speculum examination first and vaginal-fluid biomarkers such as PAMG-1 or IGFBP-1 when membrane rupture remains uncertain.\"],\"e\":[\"PPROM exposes the pregnancy to complications from prematurity, loss of amniotic fluid, and ascending infection.\",\"Very early rupture with prolonged oligohydramnios can cause pulmonary hypoplasia and deformational limb abnormalities, while later PPROM more often leads to preterm birth and infection risk.\",\"Amniotic fluid permits lung expansion and fetal movement; prolonged fluid loss therefore alters pulmonary development and musculoskeletal positioning.\"],\"tk\":\"Identify prematurity, infection, oligohydramnios-related pulmonary hypoplasia, and deformational abnormalities as major PPROM complications.\",\"pr\":\"National Institute for Health and Care Excellence (NICE) recommends sterile speculum examination for pooling and advises against using nitrazine as the diagnostic test for PPROM.\",\"tp\":\"Avoid routine digital vaginal examination in PPROM unless labor assessment requires it; monitor maternal temperature, symptoms, fetal heart rate, and laboratory markers in combination for infection.\",\"cn\":\"Preterm prelabour rupture of membranes (PPROM) causes chorioamnionitis, pulmonary hypoplasia, skeletal deformities, abruption and cord prolapse, not cardiac defects.\",\"sl\":\"Major risks of preterm prelabour rupture of membranes (PPROM): chorioamnionitis, hyaline membrane disease, pulmonary hypoplasia (frequent when prelabour rupture of membranes (PROM) is before 26 weeks and lasts over 5 weeks), abruption, fetal distress, facial and skeletal deformities, preterm delivery and cord prolapse. Speculum examination showing a pool of fluid is diagnostic; AmniSure (PAMG-1, placental alpha microglobulin-1) can be used when no fluid loss is seen.\",\"s\":[[\"L18_0\",[5,9,10,11]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F281\"},{\"id\":\"F282\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"soul\",\"g\":1,\"L\":27,\"ty\":[\"Criteria\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Tubal implantation sites and ectopic-pregnancy risk\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Regarding ectopic pregnancy, which statement is false?\",\"o\":[\"Ectopic pregnancy now accounts for approximately 11 in 1000 of all pregnancies (UK)\",\"After one previous ectopic pregnancy, 80% chance of a future Intrauterine pregnancy\",\"The ampulla is the second most common site of implantation (% 30 of tubal pregnancies)\",\"The incidence of tubal damage increases after successive episodes of pelvic inflammatory disease (PID), so after one episode of PID it will be 15%\",\"Incidence increases with the use of mini pills (progesterone-only pills)\"],\"a\":2,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Ectopic pregnancy occurs in roughly 1% of recognized pregnancies, with incidence varying by population and ascertainment.\",\"After one ectopic pregnancy, many patients subsequently achieve an intrauterine pregnancy, although recurrence risk remains increased.\",\"The ampulla is the most common tubal implantation site, accounting for the majority of tubal ectopic pregnancies rather than being the second most common at 30%.\",\"Recurrent pelvic inflammatory disease progressively increases tubal damage and therefore future ectopic-pregnancy and infertility risk.\",\"If pregnancy occurs while using a progestogen-only contraceptive, the proportion that is ectopic can be higher than among pregnancies in nonusers, despite the method reducing overall pregnancy risk.\"],\"e\":[\"Most ectopic pregnancies implant in the fallopian tube, especially the ampullary segment.\",\"Prior pelvic inflammatory disease (PID), previous ectopic pregnancy, tubal surgery, and assisted reproduction increase risk by altering tubal anatomy or embryo transport.\",\"Ciliary injury and luminal scarring delay passage of the fertilized ovum, allowing trophoblast implantation before the embryo reaches the endometrial cavity.\"],\"tk\":\"Know that the ampulla is the most common site of tubal ectopic pregnancy.\",\"pr\":\"A prior ectopic pregnancy raises recurrence risk, but future intrauterine pregnancy remains common, so fertility counseling should be individualized.\",\"tp\":\"In any reproductive-age patient with pain or bleeding, obtain a pregnancy test early and interpret ultrasound together with symptoms and serial human chorionic gonadotrophin (hCG) when location is uncertain.\",\"cn\":\"The ampulla is the commonest site of tubal ectopic pregnancy (80%), followed by the isthmus (12%).\",\"sl\":\"Ectopic pregnancy occurs in 11/1000 pregnancies (UK); tubes about 97.7%; ampulla is the most common site (80%), then isthmus (12%), fimbria (5%), cornua (2%). After one previous ectopic, 50-80% have an intrauterine pregnancy (IUP).\",\"s\":[[\"L27_0\",[24,25]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F282\"},{\"id\":\"F283\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"soul\",\"g\":4,\"L\":15,\"ty\":[\"Physio\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Midpelvic dimensions and fetal station\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which statement about the normal bony pelvis is true?\",\"o\":[\"The station is documented as +/- 1, 2,3; +2 means the head is 2cm above the spines\",\"The coccyx may cause obstruction\",\"The mid-cavity is almost round, as the transverse and AP diameters are roughly similar\",\"The pelvis in the inlet is widest in its anterior-posterior diameter at about 11cm\",\"At the outlet, the AP diameter is about 10 cm\"],\"a\":2,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Station +2 means the presenting part is approximately 2 cm below the ischial spines, not above them.\",\"The coccyx is normally mobile posteriorly during birth and is not usually a fixed cause of obstruction in an otherwise normal pelvis.\",\"The midpelvis is approximately round, with similar anteroposterior and transverse dimensions.\",\"The pelvic inlet is widest in the transverse diameter, not the anteroposterior diameter.\",\"At the outlet, the effective anteroposterior diameter increases as the coccyx moves posteriorly and is generally greater than 10 cm.\"],\"e\":[\"Pelvic dimensions change from the inlet to the outlet and influence engagement, rotation, and descent.\",\"The midpelvis near the ischial spines is relatively round, whereas the inlet is transversely oval and the outlet is more anteroposteriorly oriented.\",\"Internal rotation aligns the fetal head with the changing pelvic axes, allowing the smallest favorable cephalic diameter to traverse the birth canal.\"],\"tk\":\"Recognize the midpelvis as roughly round and station 0 as the level of the ischial spines.\",\"pr\":\"The interspinous diameter is an important limiting transverse dimension of the midpelvis and is assessed indirectly during clinical pelvimetry.\",\"tp\":\"When documenting descent, state both station and fetal position because a low head in an unfavorable occiput position may still complicate operative vaginal delivery.\",\"cn\":\"The mid-pelvis is almost round, with similar transverse and anteroposterior (AP) diameters, and contains the ischial spines.\",\"sl\":\"Inlet: transverse diameter about 13 cm, anteroposterior (AP) diameter 11 cm. Mid-pelvis: almost round, transverse and AP diameters similar; ischial spines are palpable and used to assess station. Outlet: AP diameter 13 cm, transverse 11 cm.\",\"s\":[[\"L15_0\",[16]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F283\"},{\"id\":\"F284\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"soul\",\"g\":9,\"L\":40,\"ty\":[\"Mx\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Management of a small simple ovarian cyst\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A 32-year-old premenopausal woman has resolving dull pelvic discomfort. Ultrasound shows a simple 30-mm right ovarian cyst with no suspicious features. What is the most appropriate management?\",\"o\":[\"Arrange further imaging with magnetic resonance imaging (MRI)/computed tomography (CT)\",\"Arrange for a repeat scan in 4 months\",\"Arrange for a repeat scan in one year\",\"Reassurance and discharge without follow-up\",\"Check cancer-125\"],\"a\":3,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) or computed tomography (CT) is unnecessary for a clearly simple 3-cm cyst with reassuring ultrasound morphology in a premenopausal patient.\",\"A repeat scan in 4 months is generally unnecessary for a simple cyst below 5 cm when symptoms are resolving and no suspicious features exist.\",\"Annual ultrasound follow-up is more appropriate for selected larger cysts; a 30-mm simple cyst usually requires no routine surveillance.\",\"A simple cyst of about 3 cm in a premenopausal woman is usually physiologic and can be managed with reassurance and no scheduled follow-up.\",\"Routine cancer antigen 125 (CA-125) testing is not needed for a simple small premenopausal cyst because benign physiologic conditions commonly affect CA-125 and ovarian cancer risk is very low.\"],\"e\":[\"Small simple ovarian cysts in premenopausal women are usually functional follicles or corpus-luteum cysts.\",\"With a reassuring scan and a diameter below 5 cm, routine treatment or surveillance is generally unnecessary, so reassurance and discharge are appropriate.\",\"Normal ovarian follicles enlarge during the cycle and often regress spontaneously, explaining the high rate of self-resolution without intervention.\"],\"tk\":\"Manage an uncomplicated simple ovarian cyst under 5 cm in a premenopausal woman conservatively without routine follow-up.\",\"pr\":\"Ultrasound concern rises with solid components, papillary projections, irregular septations, ascites, or marked vascularity, not simply with the presence of any cyst.\",\"tp\":\"Escalate assessment if the patient develops persistent pain, acute torsion-type symptoms, growth, complex morphology, or postmenopausal status.\",\"cn\":\"A simple ovarian cyst under 50 mm in a premenopausal woman needs no follow-up because it is almost always physiological.\",\"sl\":\"Simple ovarian cysts under 50 mm diameter do not require follow-up, as they are very likely physiological and almost always resolve within 3 menstrual cycles; 50-70 mm cysts need yearly ultrasound follow-up.\",\"s\":[[\"L40_0\",[16,31]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F284\"},{\"id\":\"F285\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"soul\",\"g\":4,\"L\":8,\"ty\":[\"Risk\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Risk factors and diagnosis of placenta previa\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which statement about placenta previa is true?\",\"o\":[\"Usually diagnosed clinically\",\"Previous uterine infection is associated with increased risk\",\"Risk increased in teenage pregnancy\",\"The risk of placenta accreta is significantly reduced\",\"Antepartum blood loss is fetal in origin\"],\"a\":1,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Placenta previa is confirmed by ultrasound localization, with transvaginal imaging providing the most accurate relationship to the internal os.\",\"Prior endometrial damage or uterine infection such as endometritis has been associated with abnormal low placental implantation in traditional risk-factor descriptions.\",\"Risk rises with advanced maternal age, prior cesarean birth, multiparity, smoking, and assisted reproduction rather than teenage pregnancy.\",\"Placenta previa, especially over a previous uterine scar, markedly increases the risk of placenta accreta spectrum.\",\"Bleeding from placenta previa is typically maternal blood from separation of placental attachment in the lower uterine segment.\"],\"e\":[\"Placenta previa reflects implantation over or near the internal os and is associated with uterine scarring or endometrial disruption.\",\"Important risk factors include prior cesarean delivery, prior uterine procedures, multiparity, smoking, assisted conception, advanced age, and in some teaching sources previous endometritis.\",\"As the lower uterine segment develops, partial separation exposes maternal uterine vessels, producing the characteristic painless maternal bleeding.\"],\"tk\":\"Recognize uterine scarring or endometrial injury as important placenta-previa risk factors and use ultrasound for diagnosis.\",\"pr\":\"The combination of placenta previa and a previous cesarean scar should trigger assessment for placenta accreta spectrum.\",\"tp\":\"For antepartum bleeding with possible previa, stabilize the mother, assess the fetus, arrange transvaginal placental localization, and avoid digital examination until previa is excluded.\",\"cn\":\"Deficient endometrium from previous endometritis, curettage or manual placental removal increases the risk of placenta praevia.\",\"sl\":\"Risk factors for placenta praevia include previous praevia (odds ratio 9.7), previous caesarean sections, and deficient endometrium due to endometritis, manual removal of placenta, curettage or submucous fibroid. Transvaginal ultrasound is superior for diagnosis.\",\"s\":[[\"L08_0\",[38,41]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F285\"},{\"id\":\"F286\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"soul\",\"g\":3,\"L\":17,\"ty\":[\"Criteria\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Timing and mechanisms of fetal heart-rate decelerations\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Regarding intrapartum fetal heart-rate monitoring, which statement is false?\",\"o\":[\"Variable deceleration pattern has a variable time of onset and a variable form and may be nonrepetitive\",\"Early deceleration usually has an onset, maximum fall, and recovery that is coincident with the onset, peak, and end of the uterine contraction.\",\"Normal short-term variability fluctuates between 6 and 25 beats/min\",\"Variable decelerations are caused by umbilical cord compression\",\"Late deceleration pattern has an onset, maximal decrease, and recovery that are shifted to the left in relation to the contraction.\"],\"a\":4,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Variable decelerations vary in timing and shape because the degree and timing of umbilical-cord compression change between contractions.\",\"An early deceleration mirrors the uterine contraction, with onset, nadir, and recovery occurring approximately with contraction onset, peak, and end.\",\"Normal baseline variability is commonly described as moderate variability of about 6–25 beats/min, reflecting intact autonomic control.\",\"Umbilical-cord compression produces variable decelerations through baroreceptor and chemoreceptor responses.\",\"A late deceleration is delayed to the right of the contraction: its onset, nadir, and recovery occur after the corresponding contraction landmarks.\"],\"e\":[\"Fetal heart-rate decelerations are interpreted by their shape, timing, depth, duration, and relationship to uterine contractions.\",\"Early decelerations mirror contractions, variable decelerations reflect cord compression, and late decelerations begin and recover after the contraction because of uteroplacental insufficiency.\",\"Reduced placental oxygen transfer during a contraction produces fetal hypoxemia after a short delay, creating the characteristic right-shifted late deceleration.\"],\"tk\":\"Differentiate early, variable, and late decelerations by timing and underlying physiology.\",\"pr\":\"Persistent late decelerations accompanied by reduced variability are more concerning for evolving fetal hypoxia than isolated late decelerations with preserved variability.\",\"tp\":\"Interpret cardiotocography (CTG) as a whole: document baseline rate, variability, decelerations, accelerations, contraction frequency, and the evolving trend before deciding on intervention.\",\"cn\":\"Late decelerations begin, reach their nadir and recover after the contraction (shifted to the right), reflecting uteroplacental insufficiency.\",\"sl\":\"\",\"s\":[],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F286\"},{\"id\":\"F287\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"soul\",\"g\":2,\"L\":22,\"ty\":[\"Complication\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Maternal thyroid disease and pregnancy outcomes\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Regarding thyroid disease in pregnancy, which statement is false?\",\"o\":[\"Maternal hyperthyroidism is accompanied by an increased incidence of premature delivery\",\"Serum-free thyroxine (free T4) and free triiodothyronine (free T3) levels usually remain in the normal range during pregnancy.\",\"Untreated maternal hypothyroidism has been associated with an increased risk of cholestasis of pregnancy\",\"Thyroid diseases are relatively common disorders in pregnancy, complicating up to 2% of pregnancies\",\"The most important laboratory finding of hypothyroidism is an elevated thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH) level\"],\"a\":2,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Poorly controlled maternal hyperthyroidism increases risks including preterm birth, fetal growth problems, and maternal cardiovascular complications.\",\"Pregnancy raises thyroid-binding globulin and therefore total thyroid hormone levels, while free T4 and free T3 are generally interpreted within pregnancy-specific reference ranges.\",\"Untreated maternal hypothyroidism is associated with miscarriage, hypertensive disease, placental abruption, preterm birth, and impaired fetal neurodevelopment; intrahepatic cholestasis is not a classic defining complication.\",\"Thyroid dysfunction is among the more common endocrine disorders of pregnancy, particularly when overt and subclinical disease are considered together.\",\"Primary hypothyroidism is characterized by an elevated thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH), with free T4 distinguishing overt from subclinical disease.\"],\"e\":[\"Pregnancy alters thyroid physiology through human chorionic gonadotrophin (hCG) stimulation, increased thyroid-binding globulin, and greater iodine requirement.\",\"Untreated overt hypothyroidism is linked to miscarriage, hypertensive complications, preterm birth, placental abruption, and impaired fetal neurodevelopment, rather than a characteristic cholestatic syndrome.\",\"In primary thyroid failure, reduced circulating thyroid hormone removes negative feedback on the pituitary, producing a compensatory rise in TSH.\"],\"tk\":\"Use TSH with pregnancy-appropriate free T4 interpretation to identify maternal hypothyroidism and recognize its major obstetric consequences.\",\"pr\":\"hCG can transiently suppress TSH in the first trimester, so pregnancy-specific reference intervals prevent misclassification of normal physiology as hyperthyroidism.\",\"tp\":\"For known hypothyroidism, check TSH early in pregnancy and repeatedly after levothyroxine dose changes, because replacement requirements often rise during gestation.\",\"cn\":\"Untreated hypothyroidism is linked to miscarriage, pre-eclampsia, low birth weight and postpartum haemorrhage, not to obstetric cholestasis.\",\"sl\":\"\",\"s\":[],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F287\"},{\"id\":\"F288\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"soul\",\"g\":2,\"L\":22,\"ty\":[\"Complication\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Overt hypothyroidism in pregnancy\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Untreated overt hypothyroidism during pregnancy increases the risk of each of the following except which condition?\",\"o\":[\"Low birth weight\",\"Preeclampsia\",\"Placenta previa\",\"Postpartum hemorrhage\",\"Miscarriage\"],\"a\":2,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Untreated overt hypothyroidism is associated with fetal growth impairment and low birth weight, especially when maternal thyroid hormone deficiency is severe or inadequately treated.\",\"Untreated overt hypothyroidism is associated with a higher risk of preeclampsia and gestational hypertension through adverse placental vascular adaptation.\",\"Placenta previa results from lower-segment placental implantation over or near the internal cervical os; it is not a characteristic complication of maternal hypothyroidism.\",\"Severe untreated hypothyroidism is associated with postpartum hemorrhage, alongside maternal anemia and cardiac complications.\",\"Inadequately treated hypothyroidism increases the risk of pregnancy loss, particularly when overt disease is present early in gestation.\"],\"e\":[\"Overt maternal hypothyroidism is associated with miscarriage, hypertensive disease, placental dysfunction, impaired fetal growth, and postpartum hemorrhage when inadequately treated.\",\"Placenta previa is an implantation disorder of the lower uterine segment and is not a characteristic consequence of maternal thyroid-hormone deficiency.\",\"Adequate levothyroxine replacement restores maternal euthyroidism and reduces complications driven by thyroid-hormone deficiency.\"],\"tk\":\"Recognize the major pregnancy complications of untreated overt maternal hypothyroidism and distinguish them from unrelated placental implantation disorders.\",\"pr\":\"Pregnancy increases thyroxine-binding globulin and changes thyroid reference ranges, so thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH) and free T4 should be interpreted with pregnancy-specific targets when available.\",\"tp\":\"For a patient already taking levothyroxine, review adherence and check thyroid function early in pregnancy, then adjust replacement promptly rather than waiting for symptoms.\",\"cn\":\"Untreated overt hypothyroidism raises risk of miscarriage, pre-eclampsia, low birth weight and postpartum haemorrhage (PPH), but not placenta praevia.\",\"sl\":\"\",\"s\":[],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F288\"},{\"id\":\"F289\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"soul\",\"g\":4,\"L\":18,\"ty\":[\"Dx\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Chorioamnionitis in PPROM\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"In a patient with preterm prelabor rupture of membranes, which finding by itself is least suggestive of intra-amniotic infection or chorioamnionitis?\",\"o\":[\"Uterine tenderness\",\"Fetal tachycardia\",\"Maternal temperature above 38°C without an alternative infection source\",\"Persistent leakage of amniotic fluid\",\"Uterine irritability or contractions\"],\"a\":3,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Uterine tenderness is a clinical feature of intra-amniotic infection, especially when accompanied by fever or fetal tachycardia.\",\"Fetal tachycardia can reflect the fetal response to maternal infection and fever in suspected chorioamnionitis.\",\"A maternal temperature above 38°C without another infection source raises concern for intra-amniotic infection, particularly with supportive obstetric findings.\",\"Persistent amniotic-fluid leakage is expected after PPROM itself and does not by itself establish chorioamnionitis.\",\"Uterine irritability or contractions can accompany intra-amniotic infection because inflammatory mediators promote myometrial activity.\"],\"e\":[\"Chorioamnionitis is suspected when maternal fever is accompanied by findings such as uterine tenderness, fetal tachycardia, or purulent fluid in a patient with ruptured membranes.\",\"Ongoing amniotic-fluid leakage reflects the membrane rupture itself; infection requires clinical evidence beyond leakage alone.\",\"Ascending genital-tract organisms trigger intra-amniotic inflammation, which can produce maternal fever, fetal tachycardia, uterine tenderness, and preterm contractions.\"],\"tk\":\"Differentiate expected PPROM findings from clinical features that suggest intra-amniotic infection.\",\"pr\":\"A diagnosis of suspected intra-amniotic infection should trigger broad-spectrum antibiotics and delivery planning, with route of birth determined by obstetric indications rather than infection alone.\",\"tp\":\"In PPROM, trend temperature, pulse, uterine tenderness, fetal heart rate, and laboratory/clinical changes together; no single nonspecific feature should be interpreted in isolation.\",\"cn\":\"Ongoing amniotic fluid leakage is expected in preterm prelabour rupture of membranes (PPROM) and alone does not indicate chorioamnionitis.\",\"sl\":\"Chorioamnionitis is diagnosed by a combination of clinical features (lower abdominal pain, abnormal vaginal discharge, fever, malaise, reduced fetal movement), maternal C-reactive protein (CRP) and white cell count, and fetal heart rate; uterine tenderness is checked on abdominal examination.\",\"s\":[[\"L18_0\",[5,12]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F289\"},{\"id\":\"F290\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"soul\",\"g\":6,\"L\":29,\"ty\":[\"Dx\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"46,XY complete gonadal dysgenesis\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A 16-year-old girl is evaluated for primary amenorrhea and absent breast development. She has normal female external genitalia, a uterus is present, she has no Turner stigmata, and serum follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH) is markedly elevated. Which diagnosis is most likely?\",\"o\":[\"Kallman’s syndrome\",\"Swyer syndrome\",\"Turner mosaicism\",\"Late-onset congenital adrenal hyperplasia\",\"Mayer- Rokitansky-Kuster-Hauser syndrome\"],\"a\":1,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Kallmann syndrome causes hypogonadotropic hypogonadism with low or inappropriately normal FSH/luteinising hormone (LH), often accompanied by anosmia.\",\"Swyer syndrome is 46,XY complete gonadal dysgenesis with streak gonads, absent pubertal estrogenization, a uterus, and elevated gonadotropins.\",\"Turner mosaicism can cause hypergonadotropic ovarian failure, but the classic phenotype may include short stature and somatic stigmata rather than an otherwise normal 16-year-old with no breast development.\",\"Late-onset congenital adrenal hyperplasia causes androgen excess with hirsutism or menstrual irregularity rather than absent breast development from ovarian failure.\",\"Müllerian agenesis presents with absent uterus but normal ovaries and therefore normal breast development and typical female-range gonadotropins.\"],\"e\":[\"Hypergonadotropic hypogonadism with absent secondary sexual development indicates primary gonadal failure rather than hypothalamic-pituitary suppression.\",\"The combination of a present uterus, absent breasts, high FSH, and lack of Turner features is most compatible with complete gonadal dysgenesis (Swyer syndrome).\",\"Failure of testicular differentiation means no meaningful testosterone or anti-Müllerian hormone production; streak gonads also fail to produce estrogen, so Müllerian structures persist while breast development does not occur.\"],\"tk\":\"Use FSH level, uterine presence, and secondary sexual characteristics to distinguish causes of primary amenorrhea.\",\"pr\":\"Because dysgenetic gonads containing Y-chromosome material carry a substantial gonadoblastoma risk, prophylactic gonadectomy is recommended after diagnosis.\",\"tp\":\"When assessing primary amenorrhea, document breast stage and uterine presence first, then use FSH/LH to separate gonadal failure from central hypogonadism.\",\"cn\":\"Primary amenorrhea with absent breasts, a uterus, high follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH) and no Turner stigmata suggests Swyer syndrome (46,XY gonadal dysgenesis).\",\"sl\":\"Absent breast development and high follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH): karyotype separates Turner (45,XO), premature ovarian failure (46,XX) and Swyer (46,XY); she has a uterus that can be missed. Swyer: female external genitalia, uterus and tubes present, absent puberty, tall stature.\",\"s\":[[\"L29_0\",[6]],[\"L28_0\",[17]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":2,\"rs\":\"F290\"},{\"id\":\"F291\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"soul\",\"g\":2,\"L\":4,\"ty\":[\"Dx\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Late-pregnancy vomiting differential\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A pregnant patient at 35 weeks develops significant vomiting. Which diagnosis is least typical as a new cause of vomiting at this gestational age?\",\"o\":[\"haemolysis, elevated liver enzymes, low platelets (HELLP) syndrome\",\"Severe preeclampsia\",\"Hyperemesis gravidarum\",\"Acute gastritis\",\"Acute fatty liver of pregnancy\"],\"a\":2,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"HELLP syndrome causes nausea or vomiting with hemolysis, elevated liver enzymes, and thrombocytopenia, usually in the setting of hypertensive disease.\",\"Preeclampsia can produce nausea or vomiting when hepatic or neurologic severe features are present, especially later in gestation.\",\"Hyperemesis gravidarum classically begins in the first trimester and usually improves by midpregnancy, making new-onset vomiting at 35 weeks atypical.\",\"Gastroenteritis can occur at any gestational age and commonly causes acute vomiting with diarrhea, abdominal symptoms, or infectious exposure.\",\"Acute fatty liver of pregnancy is a third-trimester liver disorder that commonly presents with nausea, vomiting, abdominal pain, jaundice, or hypoglycemia.\"],\"e\":[\"New vomiting late in pregnancy requires evaluation for pregnancy-specific hepatic and hypertensive disease as well as ordinary gastrointestinal causes.\",\"Hyperemesis gravidarum usually begins early in gestation; new-onset severe vomiting at 35 weeks should prompt a search for another diagnosis.\",\"Placental, hepatic, or infectious pathology can produce late-gestation vomiting through endothelial injury, hepatocellular dysfunction, or gastrointestinal inflammation.\"],\"tk\":\"Recognize that new third-trimester vomiting is not typical hyperemesis gravidarum and warrants evaluation for serious maternal disease.\",\"pr\":\"Acute fatty liver may cause hypoglycemia, coagulopathy, and renal dysfunction, features that help distinguish it from uncomplicated nausea and vomiting.\",\"tp\":\"For significant vomiting after mid-pregnancy, check blood pressure, urinalysis, complete blood count (CBC), liver tests, glucose, and hydration status rather than assuming recurrence of early-pregnancy nausea.\",\"cn\":\"Hyperemesis gravidarum starts in the first trimester; new vomiting at 35 weeks suggests pre-eclampsia, haemolysis-elevated-liver-enzymes-low-platelets syndrome or fatty liver.\",\"sl\":\"Hyperemesis gravidarum peaks at 8-12 weeks and usually abates by 16-18 weeks, though 15% continue into the 3rd trimester. Third-trimester liver disease: cholestasis, haemolysis, elevated liver enzymes and low platelets (HELLP), and acute fatty liver (nausea, pain, hypoglycaemia, coagulopathy).\",\"s\":[[\"L04_0\",[4]],[\"L22_0\",[46]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F291\"},{\"id\":\"F292\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"soul\",\"g\":2,\"L\":4,\"ty\":[\"Dx\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Hyperemesis gravidarum findings\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which finding is least characteristic of hyperemesis gravidarum?\",\"o\":[\"Dehydration\",\"Ketosis\",\"Hematocrit reduction\",\"Electrolyte imbalance\",\"Weight loss\"],\"a\":2,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Dehydration results from persistent vomiting and volume depletion, often with tachycardia, orthostasis, and concentrated urine.\",\"Ketosis reflects starvation physiology when caloric intake is inadequate during prolonged vomiting.\",\"Volume contraction tends to cause hemoconcentration, so a reduced hematocrit is not the expected dehydration-related pattern.\",\"Vomiting commonly causes electrolyte abnormalities, especially hypokalemia and hypochloremic metabolic alkalosis.\",\"Weight loss is common in clinically significant hyperemesis; clinically important loss of prepregnancy weight supports severity.\"],\"e\":[\"Hyperemesis gravidarum causes persistent vomiting with dehydration, electrolyte disturbance, weight loss, and starvation physiology.\",\"With dehydration, relative hemoconcentration rather than a reduced hematocrit is expected before treatment or bleeding alters the picture.\",\"Loss of gastric contents and reduced intake produce hypochloremic metabolic alkalosis, hypokalemia, and ketosis in more severe disease.\"],\"tk\":\"Identify the expected volume, electrolyte, and nutritional consequences of hyperemesis gravidarum.\",\"pr\":\"Give thiamine before dextrose in a severely malnourished patient to reduce the risk of precipitating Wernicke encephalopathy.\",\"tp\":\"Document weight change and check electrolytes, renal function, hydration, and oral tolerance when deciding whether outpatient treatment is sufficient.\",\"cn\":\"Hyperemesis gravidarum causes haemoconcentration (raised haematocrit), not a fall in haematocrit.\",\"sl\":\"Hyperemesis gravidarum: intractable vomiting, dehydration, alkalosis, hypokalaemia, hyponatraemia, weight loss usually more than 5%, plasma volume depletion with elevated haematocrit, and ketonuria.\",\"s\":[[\"L04_0\",[4]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F292\"},{\"id\":\"F293\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"soul\",\"g\":3,\"L\":21,\"ty\":[\"Cause\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Causes of polyhydramnios\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Each of the following conditions can be associated with polyhydramnios except which one?\",\"o\":[\"Anomalies of the fetal central nervous system\",\"Rhesus isoimmunization\",\"Fetal renal dysgenesis\",\"Maternal diabetes\",\"Nonimmune hydrops fetalis\"],\"a\":2,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Fetal central nervous system anomalies can impair swallowing and thereby reduce amniotic-fluid removal, causing polyhydramnios.\",\"Severe Rhesus alloimmunization can cause fetal anemia and hydrops, which may be accompanied by polyhydramnios.\",\"Fetal renal dysgenesis reduces fetal urine production and is classically associated with oligohydramnios, not excess amniotic fluid.\",\"Maternal diabetes can cause polyhydramnios through fetal osmotic diuresis driven by fetal hyperglycemia.\",\"Nonimmune hydrops fetalis can accompany polyhydramnios because fetal hydrops and placental dysfunction alter fluid balance.\"],\"e\":[\"Polyhydramnios results when fetal urine production is excessive or fetal swallowing is impaired; diabetes, hydrops, and some CNS or gastrointestinal anomalies are classic associations.\",\"Severe fetal renal dysgenesis decreases urine output and therefore causes oligohydramnios rather than polyhydramnios.\",\"Amniotic-fluid volume reflects a balance between fetal urination and fetal swallowing, so renal failure shifts the balance toward low fluid.\"],\"tk\":\"Differentiate causes of polyhydramnios from causes of oligohydramnios using fetal fluid physiology.\",\"pr\":\"When polyhydramnios is newly identified, assess for maternal diabetes and fetal structural anomalies, while recognizing that many cases remain idiopathic.\",\"tp\":\"Review the anatomy scan for swallowing-related and gastrointestinal abnormalities and correlate the fluid finding with growth, diabetes status, and hydrops markers.\",\"cn\":\"Fetal renal dysgenesis reduces fetal urine output and causes oligohydramnios, whereas diabetes, hydrops, Rhesus disease and central nervous system anomalies cause polyhydramnios.\",\"sl\":\"Polyhydramnios is a fetal complication of diabetes; non-immune hydrops is associated with aberrant liquor volume. For oligohydramnios, rule out ruptured membranes and renal anomalies.\",\"s\":[[\"L21_0\",[23]],[\"L10_0\",[7]],[\"L24_0\",[28]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F293\"},{\"id\":\"F294\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"soul\",\"g\":2,\"L\":4,\"ty\":[\"Physio\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Gastrointestinal disorders in pregnancy\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which statement about gastrointestinal disease during pregnancy is false?\",\"o\":[\"Constipation is common in pregnancy\",\"Pancreatitis increases in pregnancy\",\"Peptic ulcer disease improves in pregnancy\",\"Proton pump inhibitors are safe in pregnancy\",\"Pruritis is the main symptom of obstetric cholestasis\"],\"a\":1,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Constipation is common because progesterone-mediated slowing of gastrointestinal motility and mechanical factors reduce bowel transit.\",\"Acute pancreatitis is uncommon in pregnancy; pregnancy does not simply produce a routine increase in pancreatitis incidence, although gallstone disease is an important cause when it occurs.\",\"Peptic ulcer symptoms often improve during pregnancy, historically attributed to reduced gastric acidity and protective hormonal effects.\",\"Proton-pump inhibitors are used when clinically indicated in pregnancy and available human data show no major teratogenic signal for commonly used agents.\",\"The cardinal symptom of intrahepatic cholestasis of pregnancy is pruritus, classically affecting the palms and soles without a primary rash.\"],\"e\":[\"Pregnancy alters gastrointestinal motility and biliary physiology, making constipation and gallstone disease more common while some acid-peptic symptoms may improve.\",\"Acute pancreatitis remains uncommon in pregnancy; pregnancy does not make pancreatitis a routine physiologic complication.\",\"Progesterone-mediated smooth-muscle relaxation and estrogen-related biliary changes cause slower transit and biliary stasis, whereas pancreatitis requires a pathologic trigger such as gallstones.\"],\"tk\":\"Recognize common pregnancy-related gastrointestinal changes and avoid assuming that pancreatitis normally increases in pregnancy.\",\"pr\":\"A pregnant patient with pancreatitis typically has epigastric pain radiating to the back with elevated lipase, and gallstones are a major etiologic consideration.\",\"tp\":\"Do not attribute persistent upper-abdominal pain and vomiting to pregnancy alone; check lipase and hepatobiliary imaging when pancreatitis or gallstone disease is plausible.\",\"cn\":\"Acute pancreatitis is not increased by pregnancy; constipation and reflux are common, and proton pump inhibitors (PPIs) are safe.\",\"sl\":\"Antacids are safe in pregnancy, including H2 blockers, sucralfate and proton pump inhibitors (PPI, omeprazole). Constipation is common in normal pregnancy. Obstetric cholestasis is characterised by pruritus, particularly of palms and soles. Pancreatitis is not covered.\",\"s\":[[\"L04_0\",[13,14,16]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F294\"},{\"id\":\"F295\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"soul\",\"g\":9,\"L\":39,\"ty\":[\"Criteria\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Cervical cancer epidemiology and spread\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which statement about invasive cervical cancer is false?\",\"o\":[\"Squamous cell carcinoma is the most common histologic type\",\"Cervical cancer may spread directly to the parametrium and pelvic sidewall\",\"Organized cervical screening has reduced incidence in screened populations\",\"Persistent high-risk human papillomavirus (HPV) infection is the central causal risk factor\",\"Peak incidence is broadly distributed from age 30 through age 80\"],\"a\":4,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Squamous cell carcinoma remains the predominant histologic type of invasive cervical cancer, although its exact proportion varies by population.\",\"Cervical carcinoma spreads by direct parametrial extension and through pelvic lymphatics, with sidewall involvement marking advanced local disease.\",\"Population screening with cytology and HPV-based testing reduces invasive cancer by detecting treatable precancerous lesions.\",\"A very broad age range is not a meaningful peak-incidence interval; cervical cancer is an HPV-driven malignancy that clusters in mid-adult life rather than peaking uniformly from 30 to 80 years.\",\"Cervical cancer does not have a flat peak from 30 to 80 years; incidence is concentrated in mid-adult life, with most cases diagnosed far more narrowly than that range.\"],\"e\":[\"Persistent high-risk HPV infection is the central cause of cervical cancer, and screening programs have substantially reduced incidence in populations with organized screening.\",\"Contemporary epidemiology shows most cervical cancers cluster in mid-adult life, rather than describing ages 30–80 as a single peak interval.\",\"Persistent viral oncogene expression causes p53 and retinoblastoma-pathway disruption, allowing progression from high-grade intraepithelial disease to invasive carcinoma.\"],\"tk\":\"Know the core epidemiology, histology, and routes of spread of cervical cancer.\",\"pr\":\"Patients with invasive cervical cancer may have postcoital bleeding, intermenstrual bleeding, or a friable cervical lesion, but early disease can be asymptomatic.\",\"tp\":\"A visibly suspicious cervical lesion requires biopsy for histologic diagnosis; do not rely on screening cytology alone once invasive disease is suspected.\",\"cn\":\"Cervical cancer is diagnosed mainly between ages 35 and 55 (50%), rarely under 20, rather than evenly from 30 to 80.\",\"sl\":\"50% are diagnosed between ages 35 and 55; 20% at age 65 or over; rarely occurs in women younger than 20. Squamous cell carcinoma is 80-90%; direct spread to the parametrium; human papillomavirus (HPV) is the primary factor.\",\"s\":[[\"L39_0\",[3,6,31,10]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F295\"},{\"id\":\"F296\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"soul\",\"g\":2,\"L\":4,\"ty\":[\"Dx\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Intrahepatic cholestasis of pregnancy\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which feature is NOT characteristic of intrahepatic cholestasis of pregnancy (ICP)?\",\"o\":[\"Increased risk of meconium-stained liquor and fetal demise\",\"Pruritus with cholestatic biochemical abnormalities developing in the second half of pregnancy\",\"A tendency to recur in subsequent pregnancies\",\"A primary inflammatory skin rash is characteristic\",\"Association with multiple gestation and hormone exposure\"],\"a\":3,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"intrahepatic cholestasis of pregnancy (ICP) is associated with meconium-stained amniotic fluid, preterm birth, and stillbirth, with fetal risk rising as maternal bile-acid concentrations increase.\",\"The classic maternal presentation is pruritus without a primary rash, often in late pregnancy, accompanied by elevated serum bile acids with or without transaminitis.\",\"ICP has a substantial recurrence risk in future pregnancies, reflecting underlying hormonal and genetic susceptibility.\",\"A primary inflammatory rash is not a feature of ICP; excoriations may appear secondarily from scratching, but the skin is otherwise initially normal.\",\"ICP is more frequent with multiple gestation and high-estrogen states, and similar cholestatic susceptibility can appear with exogenous hormones.\"],\"e\":[\"Intrahepatic cholestasis of pregnancy classically causes pruritus without a primary rash, elevated bile acids, frequent recurrence, and increased fetal risk as bile-acid concentrations rise.\",\"Fetal risk correlates with bile-acid severity, while recurrence, multiple gestation, and prior hormone-associated cholestasis are recognized associations.\",\"Impaired hepatocellular bile transport causes maternal bile-acid accumulation, and high fetal exposure is linked to meconium passage, preterm birth, and stillbirth risk.\"],\"tk\":\"Identify the defining clinical pattern of intrahepatic cholestasis of pregnancy and distinguish it from primary dermatologic disease.\",\"pr\":\"Severe ICP with bile acids ≥100 micromol/L carries the clearest increase in stillbirth risk, particularly later in gestation.\",\"tp\":\"For unexplained pregnancy pruritus, order serum bile acids and liver tests even when the skin examination shows only scratch marks.\",\"cn\":\"Obstetric cholestasis causes pruritus, especially of palms and soles, without a primary skin rash.\",\"sl\":\"Obstetric cholestasis: pruritus without skin rash other than excoriations; risk factors include family history, multiple gestation, hepatitis C, cholestatic effect of oestrogen; risks include meconium-stained liquor and intrauterine fetal death; recurrence exceeds 90%.\",\"s\":[[\"L04_0\",[16,17,19,22]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":2,\"rs\":\"F218\"},{\"id\":\"F297\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"soul\",\"g\":7,\"L\":35,\"ty\":[\"Pharm\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Traditional progestogen-only pill\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which statement about traditional progestogen-only contraceptive pills is true?\",\"o\":[\"They are associated with a delayed return to normal fertility after discontinuation\",\"They inhibit lactation and should not be used during breastfeeding\",\"They protect against sexually transmitted infections\",\"They may be associated with functional ovarian follicular cysts\",\"Complete suppression of ovulation is the main mode of action for all POPs\"],\"a\":3,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Fertility generally returns rapidly after stopping a progestogen-only pill because the method does not create a prolonged post-treatment suppression of ovarian function.\",\"Traditional progestogen-only pills are compatible with postpartum contraception and breastfeeding and do not characteristically suppress lactation.\",\"Oral hormonal contraception does not prevent sexually transmitted infections; barrier protection is needed when sexually transmitted infection (STI) prevention is required.\",\"Traditional progestogen-only pills incompletely suppress ovulation in some cycles, so persistent follicles or functional ovarian cysts may occur.\",\"Traditional preparations rely substantially on cervical-mucus thickening and endometrial effects; complete ovulation suppression is not the universal primary mechanism.\"],\"e\":[\"Traditional progestogen-only pills primarily thicken cervical mucus and variably suppress ovulation; their efficacy depends on consistent daily dosing.\",\"Functional follicular cysts may occur because ovulation is not uniformly suppressed with older low-dose progestogen-only formulations.\",\"Progestogenic effects on cervical mucus and the endometrium provide contraception even when follicular development or ovulation occasionally occurs.\"],\"tk\":\"Understand the mechanism and expected ovarian effects of traditional progestogen-only pills.\",\"pr\":\"The contraceptive reliability of traditional POPs is more time-sensitive with missed pills than many combined pills or newer ovulation-suppressing POP formulations.\",\"tp\":\"When counseling on a POP, confirm the exact formulation and explain its missed-pill window, because instructions differ between traditional and newer preparations.\",\"cn\":\"Traditional progestogen-only pills (POP) do not consistently suppress ovulation and may be associated with functional ovarian follicular cysts.\",\"sl\":\"Progestogen-only pills (POPs) alter cervical mucus, make the endometrium non-receptive and may inhibit ovulation in up to 60% (desogestrel 97%). Progestogen-only methods are safe during lactation.\",\"s\":[[\"L35_0\",[33,38]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F297\"},{\"id\":\"F298\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"soul\",\"g\":2,\"L\":1,\"ty\":[\"Mx\"],\"lv\":3,\"ti\":\"Chronic hypertension in pregnancy\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which statement about chronic hypertension in pregnancy is false?\",\"o\":[\"Well-controlled uncomplicated chronic hypertension can have a good maternal and fetal outcome\",\"Management includes surveillance for superimposed preeclampsia and fetal growth restriction\",\"Blood pressure often falls physiologically in mid-pregnancy and medication requirements may decrease\",\"Labetalol is commonly preferred, with nifedipine or methyldopa as alternatives when appropriate\",\"There is strong evidence that routinely lowering blood pressure below 120/80 mmHg improves pregnancy outcome\"],\"a\":4,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"When chronic hypertension is well controlled without superimposed preeclampsia or fetal growth restriction, many pregnancies have favorable outcomes with appropriate surveillance.\",\"Major management priorities are blood-pressure control plus detection of superimposed preeclampsia and impaired fetal growth.\",\"Systemic vascular resistance falls in normal pregnancy, so blood pressure commonly decreases in the second trimester and some patients need dose adjustment.\",\"Current guidance commonly uses labetalol first line, with nifedipine or methyldopa selected according to contraindications, prior therapy, adverse effects, and patient factors.\",\"Methyldopa has extensive historical pregnancy safety experience, but labetalol is commonly preferred first-line for chronic hypertension when suitable.\"],\"e\":[\"Chronic hypertension in pregnancy is hypertension predating pregnancy or recognized before 20 weeks and requires treatment that balances maternal cardiovascular protection with uteroplacental perfusion.\",\"Current management uses a pregnancy-appropriate treatment target around 135/85 mmHg rather than pursuing a sub-120/80 goal.\",\"Excessive blood-pressure reduction can impair uteroplacental perfusion, while untreated hypertension raises the risks of superimposed preeclampsia, abruption, fetal growth restriction, and maternal stroke.\"],\"tk\":\"Apply modern blood-pressure targets and medication choices for chronic hypertension in pregnancy.\",\"pr\":\"Chronic hypertension itself is a high-risk indication for low-dose aspirin prophylaxis unless contraindicated, according to major obstetric guidelines.\",\"tp\":\"At each review, assess not only the blood pressure but also proteinuria/severe features, medication tolerance, and fetal growth surveillance.\",\"cn\":\"In chronic hypertension in pregnancy, target blood pressure is 135/85 mmHg; routinely lowering below 120/80 mmHg has no proven benefit.\",\"sl\":\"Aim for a target blood pressure of 135/85 mmHg; low diastolic pressure is associated with low birth weight, so reduce treatment if diastolic is persistently below 80 mmHg. Hypertension may improve in early pregnancy and some women may stop medication for several weeks.\",\"s\":[[\"L01_0\",[13,14]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F298\"},{\"id\":\"F299\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"soul\",\"g\":8,\"L\":36,\"ty\":[\"Ix\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Urodynamic detrusor overactivity\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which cystometric finding is most suggestive of detrusor overactivity causing urgency urinary incontinence?\",\"o\":[\"Detrusor pressure rise of >15 cmH2O during filling and standing\",\"Capacity between 400 and 600 mL\",\"Residual urine of < 50 mL\",\"First desire to void between 150 and 200 mL\",\"No leakage on coughing\"],\"a\":0,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"An involuntary detrusor-pressure rise during bladder filling is the urodynamic correlate of detrusor overactivity and can accompany urgency incontinence.\",\"A bladder capacity of 400–600 mL is broadly compatible with normal adult storage and does not establish detrusor overactivity.\",\"A low residual volume reflects efficient bladder emptying and does not identify detrusor overactivity during filling.\",\"First desire to void around 150–200 mL is a sensory milestone that can occur normally and is not by itself diagnostic of urgency incontinence.\",\"Absence of leakage with coughing argues against demonstrable stress incontinence but does not itself prove detrusor overactivity.\"],\"e\":[\"Urgency urinary incontinence is commonly caused by detrusor overactivity, an involuntary bladder contraction during the filling phase.\",\"On cystometry, a spontaneous detrusor-pressure rise during filling reproducing urgency or leakage supports detrusor overactivity.\",\"Involuntary parasympathetic muscarinic activation contracts the detrusor despite an incompletely filled bladder, producing urgency and urge leakage.\"],\"tk\":\"Recognize the urodynamic signature of detrusor overactivity in urgency urinary incontinence.\",\"pr\":\"Overactive bladder is a clinical syndrome of urgency, usually with frequency and nocturia, with or without urgency incontinence; urodynamics are not required for every uncomplicated patient.\",\"tp\":\"Before escalating therapy, exclude reversible contributors such as urinary infection, excessive fluids/caffeine, constipation, and significant postvoid residual.\",\"cn\":\"Detrusor overactivity (DO) is shown on cystometry as an involuntary detrusor pressure rise during filling.\",\"sl\":\"Detrusor overactivity (DO): a urodynamic observation characterised by involuntary detrusor contractions during the filling phase, which may be spontaneous or provoked; DO with leakage is detrusor overactivity incontinence (DOI).\",\"s\":[[\"L36_0\",[18,40]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F299\"},{\"id\":\"F300\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"soul\",\"g\":2,\"L\":10,\"ty\":[\"Risk\",\"Screening\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Early diabetes screening risk factors\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which condition is a recognized risk factor that should prompt consideration of early testing for previously unrecognized diabetes in pregnancy?\",\"o\":[\"Polycystic ovarian syndrome\",\"Previous preterm delivery\",\"body mass index (BMI) 19 - 25\",\"Family hx of DM in 3rd degree relative\",\"Previous Down baby\"],\"a\":0,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Polycystic ovary syndrome is associated with insulin resistance and is a recognized risk factor for type 2 diabetes, supporting risk-based early pregnancy testing.\",\"A previous preterm birth alone is not a standard metabolic risk factor used to select patients for early diabetes testing.\",\"A body mass index (BMI) of 19–25 kg/m² is not an obesity-related diabetes risk factor; risk-based screening is driven by overweight/obesity plus additional risks or other high-risk features.\",\"Diabetes in a third-degree relative is weaker than the first-degree family history typically used in standard risk-based screening criteria.\",\"A previous child with Down syndrome is not a recognized maternal risk factor for gestational or type 2 diabetes.\"],\"e\":[\"Polycystic ovary syndrome is associated with insulin resistance and an increased lifetime risk of dysglycemia and type 2 diabetes.\",\"A pregnant patient with PCOS therefore merits early assessment for abnormal glucose metabolism when risk-factor based early testing is used.\",\"Insulin resistance increases maternal hyperglycemia, while placental hormones progressively increase insulin resistance as pregnancy advances.\"],\"tk\":\"Recognize PCOS as a diabetes risk factor relevant to early pregnancy metabolic testing.\",\"pr\":\"Even after a normal early test, patients not diagnosed with diabetes still require routine gestational diabetes mellitus (GDM) screening at 24–28 weeks under current standards.\",\"tp\":\"At booking, ask specifically about prior GDM, PCOS, BMI, first-degree family history, and previous abnormal glucose testing so early testing is not missed.\",\"cn\":\"Polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS) is a risk factor for diabetes that justifies early glucose testing in pregnancy.\",\"sl\":\"\",\"s\":[],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F300\"},{\"id\":\"F301\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"soul\",\"g\":4,\"L\":20,\"ty\":[\"Criteria\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Bishop score calculation\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A patient has a cervix that is soft and mid-position, 2 cm dilated and 50% effaced; the presenting part is at −1 station. What is the Bishop score?\",\"o\":[\"3\",\"7\",\"5\",\"10\",\"9\"],\"a\":1,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"A Bishop score of 2 indicates low cervical favorability, with a closed or minimally dilated, firm, posterior cervix and high presenting part.\",\"A cervix that is 3 cm dilated, 70% effaced, soft, mid-position, and at −1 station can reasonably score 7 in total, indicating moderate cervical favorability.\",\"A Bishop score of 5 generally reflects an unfavorable cervix with limited dilation or effacement, firmer consistency, or a higher station.\",\"A Bishop score of 10 requires advanced dilation and effacement plus favorable position, consistency, and station beyond the findings described.\",\"A Bishop score of 12 represents high cervical favorability and would require substantially more advanced cervical change.\"],\"e\":[\"The Bishop score combines cervical dilation, effacement, consistency, position, and fetal station to estimate cervical favorability for induction.\",\"The described findings produce a total score of about 7, which is neither markedly unfavorable nor maximally favorable.\",\"Cervical ripening increases collagen remodeling and tissue compliance, allowing progressive dilation and effacement before or during induction.\"],\"tk\":\"Calculate a Bishop score correctly from the five standard examination components.\",\"pr\":\"A higher Bishop score predicts a greater chance of successful induction, while an unfavorable cervix often prompts cervical-ripening methods before oxytocin.\",\"tp\":\"Record each Bishop component separately rather than writing only the total; this makes reassessment and handover much more reliable.\",\"cn\":\"Bishop score: 2 cm dilated (1) + 50% effaced (1) + station -1 (2) + soft (2) + mid-position (1) = 7.\",\"sl\":\"The Bishop score is based on dilation, effacement (or length), position and consistency of the cervix and the station of the head relative to the ischial spines; a score of 6 or less versus more than 6 guides the choice of induction method.\",\"s\":[[\"L20_0\",[20,21]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F301\"},{\"id\":\"F302\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"soul\",\"g\":1,\"L\":27,\"ty\":[\"Dx\",\"Mx\"],\"lv\":3,\"ti\":\"Pregnancy of uncertain viability\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A hemodynamically stable woman with an early intrauterine pregnancy has a transvaginal ultrasound showing a mean gestational sac diameter of 18 mm with no visible fetal pole. What is the most appropriate next step before diagnosing miscarriage?\",\"o\":[\"Repeat transvaginal ultrasound after at least 7 days\",\"Diagnose miscarriage immediately from the 18-mm sac measurement\",\"Use a single serum human chorionic gonadotrophin (hCG) discriminatory level to confirm nonviability\",\"Perform uterine evacuation without repeat imaging\",\"Repeat only a urine pregnancy test in 48 hours\"],\"a\":0,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"When the sac is below the definitive nonviability threshold, a repeat transvaginal scan after at least 7 days prevents a false-positive diagnosis of miscarriage.\",\"A mean sac diameter of 18 mm without a fetal pole is not sufficient by itself to diagnose pregnancy failure on transvaginal imaging.\",\"A single serum human chorionic gonadotrophin (hCG) discriminatory threshold cannot establish pregnancy viability and should not override serial imaging when the pregnancy is intrauterine but uncertain.\",\"Immediate uterine evacuation risks terminating a potentially viable pregnancy because diagnostic ultrasound criteria are not yet met.\",\"A urine pregnancy test remains positive in both viable and nonviable pregnancies and cannot resolve ultrasound viability uncertainty.\"],\"e\":[\"When early-pregnancy viability is uncertain, repeat transvaginal ultrasonography is required before diagnosing miscarriage unless definitive criteria are already met.\",\"An 18-mm intrauterine gestational sac without visible cardiac activity or a definite embryo does not justify immediate intervention on a single scan.\",\"Serial imaging prevents interruption of a potentially viable pregnancy because embryonic development and sonographic visibility vary with true gestational age.\"],\"tk\":\"Manage a pregnancy of uncertain viability with appropriately timed repeat transvaginal ultrasound.\",\"pr\":\"Do not use last menstrual period alone to decide whether a fetal heartbeat should already be visible because cycle and ovulation timing vary.\",\"tp\":\"Document the mean sac diameter, whether a yolk sac or embryo is seen, and the exact rescan interval so the next clinician can apply diagnostic thresholds safely.\",\"cn\":\"A mean gestational sac diameter below 25 mm without an embryo is pregnancy of uncertain viability; rescan after 1 week.\",\"sl\":\"Gestational sac (GS) 25 mm or less, or crown-rump length 7 mm or less, with no fetal heart is intrauterine pregnancy of uncertain viability: rescan in 1 week. A fetal pole is expected at a GS of 18 mm.\",\"s\":[[\"L27_0\",[4,5]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":2,\"rs\":\"F249\"},{\"id\":\"F303\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"soul\",\"g\":7,\"L\":40,\"ty\":[\"Mx\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Ovarian torsion detorsion\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"What is the preferred operative principle when ovarian torsion is found in a reproductive-age patient and the ovary appears potentially salvageable?\",\"o\":[\"Conservative management with antibiotics, fluids and analgesia\",\"Analgesia\",\"Oophorectomy\",\"Untwisting and fixation of the ovary to the pelvic side wall\",\"Untwisting\"],\"a\":4,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Ovarian torsion is a surgical emergency; antibiotics, fluids, and analgesia alone do not correct vascular pedicle twisting.\",\"Analgesia may control pain temporarily but leaves the ovary at risk of ischemic injury and necrosis.\",\"Oophorectomy is reserved for clearly nonviable or pathologic tissue when preservation is impossible; ovarian conservation is preferred in reproductive-age patients.\",\"Routine fixation to the pelvic sidewall is not the standard initial treatment for a first uncomplicated episode; detorsion and reassessment come first.\",\"Detorsion should be performed promptly to restore perfusion, even when the ovary initially appears dark or edematous.\"],\"e\":[\"Adnexal torsion is a surgical emergency treated with prompt laparoscopy and detorsion of the twisted adnexa.\",\"Even a markedly congested or dark ovary can recover after prompt detorsion, so appearance alone should not trigger routine oophorectomy.\",\"Twisting first obstructs venous and lymphatic outflow, causing edema and congestion; prolonged torsion can then compromise arterial inflow and cause ischemic necrosis.\"],\"tk\":\"Prioritize detorsion and ovarian preservation in reproductive-age patients with ovarian torsion.\",\"pr\":\"A normal Doppler flow signal does not exclude torsion because dual ovarian blood supply can preserve arterial flow despite clinically important twisting.\",\"tp\":\"When torsion is strongly suspected, involve gynecology early and avoid delaying surgery for repeated imaging if pain, examination, and adnexal findings are compelling.\",\"cn\":\"Ovarian torsion is treated by laparoscopic untwisting of the ovary; oophoropexy is optional and oophorectomy only for necrosis.\",\"sl\":\"Treatment: ABCDE, analgesia, laparoscopy to uncoil the torted ovary, possibly oophoropexy to fixate an ovary likely to twist again; where blood flow is cut off for an extended time necrosis occurs, so proceed to oophorectomy.\",\"s\":[[\"L40_0\",[27]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":2,\"rs\":\"F303\"},{\"id\":\"F304\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"soul\",\"g\":7,\"L\":40,\"ty\":[\"Dx\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Ruptured functional ovarian cyst\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A 26-year-old woman develops sudden sharp left iliac fossa pain that radiates across the abdomen. Her last menstrual period was 3 weeks ago, pregnancy testing is negative, and ultrasound shows normal pelvic organs with a small amount of free fluid in the pouch of Douglas. What is the most likely diagnosis?\",\"o\":[\"Endometriosis\",\"Ovarian torsion\",\"Ectopic pregnancy\",\"Functional cyst rupture\",\"Appendicitis\"],\"a\":3,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Endometriosis usually causes chronic or cyclical pelvic pain, dysmenorrhea, dyspareunia, or infertility rather than an isolated sudden episode with only a small amount of free fluid.\",\"Ovarian torsion typically produces sudden severe unilateral pain, often with nausea or vomiting and an enlarged or edematous ovary on imaging.\",\"Ectopic pregnancy should be considered with pain in reproductive-age patients, but a negative pregnancy test makes an ongoing ectopic pregnancy very unlikely.\",\"A ruptured functional or corpus-luteum cyst can cause sudden unilateral pain with a small volume of pelvic free fluid and may leave no obvious residual cyst after rupture.\",\"Appendicitis more often causes a right-lower-quadrant inflammatory pattern with gastrointestinal or systemic features rather than isolated left iliac fossa pain and pelvic free fluid.\"],\"e\":[\"Rupture of a functional ovarian cyst can cause abrupt unilateral pelvic pain and a small volume of free intraperitoneal fluid in an otherwise stable patient.\",\"The negative pregnancy test and normal-appearing adnexa with a small amount of free fluid make a recently ruptured functional cyst the best fit.\",\"When a cyst ruptures, cyst fluid and sometimes blood enter the peritoneal cavity, producing sudden pain that often improves as the fluid is resorbed.\"],\"tk\":\"Recognize the typical presentation of a ruptured functional ovarian cyst and distinguish it from torsion and ectopic pregnancy.\",\"pr\":\"A hemorrhagic cyst rupture can produce significant hemoperitoneum; hemodynamic instability or falling hemoglobin changes management from observation to urgent surgical assessment.\",\"tp\":\"In acute pelvic pain, establish pregnancy status first, then correlate pain laterality, hemodynamics, hemoglobin, and ultrasound findings before choosing observation versus surgery.\",\"cn\":\"Sudden unilateral pelvic pain with negative pregnancy test and small free pelvic fluid suggests a ruptured functional ovarian cyst.\",\"sl\":\"Gynaecological causes of lower abdominal pain include mittelschmerz, pelvic inflammatory disease (PID), ovarian torsion, ruptured cyst and degeneration of fibroid.\",\"s\":[[\"L07_0\",[22]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F304\"},{\"id\":\"F305\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"soul\",\"g\":7,\"L\":38,\"ty\":[\"Dx\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Adenomyosis clinical features and treatment\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which statement about adenomyosis is false?\",\"o\":[\"The uterus may be symmetrically enlarged and boggy on pelvic examination\",\"Only a small minority of patients with adenomyosis are asymptomatic\",\"Endometrial glands and stroma extend into the myometrium beyond the basalis\",\"non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs) and hormonal suppression are common conservative treatments\",\"Adenomyosis may coexist with endometriosis\"],\"a\":1,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Adenomyosis classically produces a diffusely enlarged, soft or boggy uterus, often with tenderness that is more apparent around menstruation.\",\"Many patients with adenomyosis are asymptomatic or incidentally diagnosed, so describing asymptomatic disease as occurring only rarely is inaccurate.\",\"Histologically, adenomyosis consists of endometrial glands and stroma within the myometrium, traditionally described as extending beyond the basalis layer.\",\"Conservative therapy commonly includes non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs), combined hormonal therapy, progestogens, or a levonorgestrel-releasing intrauterine system, depending on symptoms and fertility goals.\",\"Adenomyosis and endometriosis can coexist, reflecting related ectopic endometrial-tissue disorders despite different anatomic locations.\"],\"e\":[\"Adenomyosis is ectopic endometrial glands and stroma within the myometrium and commonly produces a diffusely enlarged, boggy uterus with dysmenorrhea or heavy bleeding.\",\"A substantial proportion of patients are asymptomatic or diagnosed incidentally, while symptomatic disease commonly causes heavy menstrual bleeding and dysmenorrhea.\",\"Ectopic endometrial tissue induces myometrial hypertrophy and local inflammatory signaling, accounting for uterine enlargement, tenderness, and cyclic pain.\"],\"tk\":\"Distinguish the true clinical and pathologic features of adenomyosis, including the fact that asymptomatic disease is common.\",\"pr\":\"Transvaginal ultrasound and magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) can suggest adenomyosis, but the historical definitive diagnosis is histologic examination of uterine tissue.\",\"tp\":\"When fertility preservation matters, start with symptom-directed medical therapy and reserve hysterectomy for refractory symptoms when childbearing is complete.\",\"cn\":\"Many women with adenomyosis (about one third) are asymptomatic; it presents with heavy bleeding, dysmenorrhoea and a symmetrically enlarged uterus.\",\"sl\":\"Adenomyosis is endometrial glands and stroma within the myometrium, most often in multiparous women at the end of reproductive life, presenting with heavy menstrual bleeding, progressive dysmenorrhoea and deep dyspareunia, with a symmetrically enlarged, possibly tender uterus. It has a limited response to hormonal therapy and often requires hysterectomy.\",\"s\":[[\"L38_0\",[48,50]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F305\"},{\"id\":\"F306\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"soul\",\"g\":6,\"L\":33,\"ty\":[\"Physio\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Luteal phase of menstrual cycle\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which phase of the menstrual cycle extends from ovulation until the onset of the next menstruation?\",\"o\":[\"Progesterone\",\"Proliferative phase\",\"Luteal phase\",\"Follicular phase\",\"Corpus luteum\"],\"a\":2,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Progesterone is a hormone produced mainly by the corpus luteum after ovulation; it is not itself the name of a menstrual-cycle phase.\",\"The proliferative phase is the estrogen-dominant endometrial phase before ovulation, when glands and stroma proliferate after menses.\",\"The luteal phase begins after ovulation, when the corpus luteum produces progesterone, and ends with luteolysis before menstruation if pregnancy does not occur.\",\"The follicular phase begins with menstruation and ends at ovulation; its duration is the most variable part of the ovarian cycle.\",\"The corpus luteum is a temporary ovarian endocrine structure that secretes progesterone and inhibin A after ovulation.\"],\"e\":[\"The luteal phase spans the interval from ovulation until the onset of the next menstrual period.\",\"During this interval, the corpus luteum produces progesterone, transforming the endometrium into a secretory state.\",\"If implantation does not occur, luteolysis lowers progesterone and estrogen, triggering endometrial breakdown and menstruation.\"],\"tk\":\"Identify the luteal phase as the interval from ovulation to menstruation.\",\"pr\":\"Mid-luteal progesterone is best timed about 7 days before the expected next period, not automatically on cycle day 21 for every patient.\",\"tp\":\"When evaluating ovulation in an irregular cycle, time progesterone testing to the expected luteal phase rather than using a fixed calendar day.\",\"cn\":\"The luteal (postovulatory) phase runs from ovulation to the next menstruation.\",\"sl\":\"Luteal phase (postovulatory): corpus luteum, increasing levels of progesterone and secretory changes in the endometrium.\",\"s\":[[\"L33_0\",[5,10]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F306\"},{\"id\":\"F307\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"soul\",\"g\":7,\"L\":27,\"ty\":[\"Physio\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Fallopian tube anatomy\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"What is the name of the fallopian-tube segment that traverses the muscular wall of the uterus?\",\"o\":[\"Interstitial\",\"Ampullar\",\"Infundibular\",\"Isthmic\",\"Cornual\"],\"a\":0,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"The interstitial segment traverses the uterine myometrium and is the intramural portion of the fallopian tube.\",\"The ampulla is the widest tubal segment and the usual site of fertilization, lying lateral to the isthmus.\",\"The infundibulum is the distal funnel-shaped segment bearing fimbriae near the ovary.\",\"The isthmus is the narrow muscular segment immediately lateral to the uterine wall.\",\"Cornual is an imprecise term around the uterine horn and can refer to several uterine-angle implantation sites; precise tubal anatomy uses named intramural, isthmic, ampullary, and infundibular segments.\"],\"e\":[\"The interstitial (intramural) portion of the fallopian tube passes through the muscular wall of the uterus.\",\"The interstitial segment lies medial to the isthmus and opens into the uterine cavity at the tubal ostium.\",\"Its surrounding myometrium can permit substantial distension and hemorrhage when an interstitial ectopic pregnancy ruptures.\"],\"tk\":\"Know that the interstitial segment of the fallopian tube lies within the uterine wall.\",\"pr\":\"An interstitial ectopic pregnancy is distinct from a pregnancy implanted in a rudimentary horn, although the terms are sometimes incorrectly conflated.\",\"tp\":\"On ultrasound, describe the exact implantation site and myometrial mantle rather than relying on the nonspecific word 'cornual.'\",\"cn\":\"The interstitial (intramural) segment of the fallopian tube passes through the uterine muscle wall.\",\"sl\":\"Ectopic implantation sites: ampulla 80%, isthmus 12%, fimbria 5%, cornua 2% and interstitial 2-3%.\",\"s\":[[\"L27_0\",[24]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F307\"},{\"id\":\"F308\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"soul\",\"g\":5,\"L\":26,\"ty\":[\"Risk\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Postpartum endometritis risk factors\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which factor is a well-established predisposition to postpartum endometritis?\",\"o\":[\"Inadequate nutrition\",\"Sexual intercourse\",\"Hemorrhage\",\"Anemia\",\"Prolonged rupture of membranes\"],\"a\":4,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Poor nutritional status may affect general recovery but is not the classic obstetric exposure most strongly linked to ascending postpartum uterine infection.\",\"Sexual intercourse is not a defining intrapartum risk factor for postpartum endometritis in the way that prolonged labor or membrane rupture is.\",\"Postpartum hemorrhage can accompany retained tissue or uterine atony, while postpartum uterine infection arises primarily through ascending polymicrobial contamination.\",\"Anemia may worsen the consequences of infection or blood loss, but it does not create the ascending microbial access produced by ruptured membranes.\",\"Prolonged rupture of membranes increases exposure of the uterine cavity to ascending vaginal flora, making postpartum endometritis more likely.\"],\"e\":[\"Postpartum endometritis is an ascending polymicrobial uterine infection, especially common after cesarean birth and prolonged labor or membrane rupture.\",\"Prolonged membrane rupture gives vaginal organisms more time to ascend into the amniotic cavity and uterine tissues before delivery.\",\"Loss of the membrane barrier increases bacterial inoculation of decidua and myometrium, producing postpartum fever, uterine tenderness, and foul lochia.\"],\"tk\":\"Recognize prolonged rupture of membranes as an important risk factor for postpartum endometritis.\",\"pr\":\"The single strongest predictor of postpartum endometritis is cesarean delivery, especially after labor or membrane rupture.\",\"tp\":\"A postpartum patient with fever and uterine tenderness should be assessed for endometritis, retained products, wound infection, urinary infection, and septic pelvic thrombophlebitis.\",\"cn\":\"Prolonged rupture of membranes is a well-established risk factor for postpartum endometritis.\",\"sl\":\"Puerperal sepsis risk factors: caesarean section (CS), prolonged rupture of membranes (ROM), retained products; obesity, diabetes, anaemia, immunosuppression; Group A Streptococcus contact.\",\"s\":[[\"L26_0\",[34]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"About the key: Prolonged rupture of membranes (E) is the best answer. However, slide 34 also lists anaemia among puerperal sepsis risk factors, so option D is arguably correct by the slides.\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F308\"},{\"id\":\"F309\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"soul\",\"g\":7,\"L\":32,\"ty\":[\"Ix\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Anovulatory subfertility in PCOS\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A 31-year-old woman has 3 years of secondary subfertility. She menstruates only every 4–6 months after taking medication, has facial hirsutism, and has a body mass index (BMI) of 31 kg/m². Which component of female-factor infertility evaluation is most directly indicated by this history?\",\"o\":[\"Immediate diagnostic laparoscopy\",\"Hysterosalpingography before assessing ovulation\",\"Anti-Müllerian hormone as the sole first-line test\",\"Assessment of ovulatory function\",\"Endometrial biopsy for luteal-phase assessment\"],\"a\":3,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Diagnostic laparoscopy can assess tubal or pelvic pathology when suspected endometriosis, adhesions, or unexplained infertility remains after less invasive evaluation.\",\"Tubal testing with hysterosalpingography (HSG) is important in infertility assessment, but standard evaluation establishes semen and ovulatory information before moving to invasive tubal testing.\",\"anti-Müllerian hormone (AMH) reflects ovarian reserve and follicle number but does not establish whether this patient is ovulating or explain her oligomenorrhea by itself.\",\"Marked oligomenorrhea with hirsutism and obesity strongly suggests chronic anovulation, often from polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS), so ovulatory-function assessment is directly indicated.\",\"Routine endometrial biopsy for luteal-phase defect is not recommended as a standard infertility investigation.\"],\"e\":[\"Infertility assessment evaluates ovulation, semen parameters, and tubal patency in parallel rather than assuming a single-factor cause.\",\"Her very infrequent menses, hirsutism, and obesity strongly suggest chronic anovulation, so documenting ovulatory function is a high-yield part of her initial female-factor assessment.\",\"Chronic anovulation in PCOS reflects disordered follicular maturation and excess androgen/insulin signaling, producing long irregular cycles without predictable ovulation.\"],\"tk\":\"Recognize chronic anovulation as a priority female-factor assessment in an infertile patient with oligomenorrhea and hyperandrogenism.\",\"pr\":\"Tubal-patency testing is generally interpreted in the context of known semen and ovulation results, and routine luteal-phase endometrial biopsy is not recommended.\",\"tp\":\"Evaluate infertility as a couple: obtain semen analysis in parallel while investigating the woman's ovulatory pattern and choosing tubal testing according to history.\",\"cn\":\"Infrequent periods with hirsutism and obesity suggest anovulation, so evidence of ovulation is a key part of basic infertility work-up.\",\"sl\":\"Basic work-up includes evidence of ovulation: menstrual history, mid-luteal (day 21) serum progesterone, and day 2-3 follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH) and luteinising hormone (LH) especially in irregular periods.\",\"s\":[[\"L32_0\",[12,14]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F309\"},{\"id\":\"F310\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"soul\",\"g\":4,\"L\":9,\"ty\":[\"Risk\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Uterine atony risk factors\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which condition is a recognized risk factor for uterine atony after delivery?\",\"o\":[\"Small for gestational age\",\"Spontaneous rupture of membranes\",\"Oligohydramnios\",\"Ergometrin administration\",\"Multiple gestations\"],\"a\":4,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Breech presentation changes fetal presentation but does not itself cause uterine overdistension, the classic mechanical driver of atony.\",\"Prolonged rupture of membranes increases infection risk; by itself, membrane rupture lacks the overdistension mechanism that strongly predisposes to atony.\",\"Oligohydramnios means reduced amniotic fluid and therefore reduced uterine distension, so it is not a typical atony risk factor.\",\"Ergometrine is a uterotonic drug that promotes myometrial contraction and is used to treat or prevent atony in appropriate patients.\",\"Multiple gestation causes marked myometrial overdistension, which impairs effective postpartum contraction and increases uterine-atony risk.\"],\"e\":[\"Uterine atony is failure of the myometrium to contract effectively after placental separation and is the most common cause of postpartum hemorrhage.\",\"Multiple gestation overdistends the uterus, making coordinated postpartum myometrial contraction less effective.\",\"Myometrial contraction normally compresses open spiral vessels as physiologic ligatures; overdistension reduces this compressive hemostatic effect.\"],\"tk\":\"Identify uterine overdistension from multiple gestation as a major risk factor for postpartum uterine atony.\",\"pr\":\"Other classic overdistension risks include polyhydramnios and fetal macrosomia.\",\"tp\":\"For a patient with known atony risk factors, ensure active management of the third stage, uterotonic readiness, intravenous (IV) access, and hemorrhage resources before delivery.\",\"cn\":\"Uterine overdistension, as in multiple pregnancy, is a recognised risk factor for uterine atony and postpartum haemorrhage (PPH).\",\"sl\":\"In multifetal gestation, postpartum haemorrhage (PPH) is more common because of uterine atony.\",\"s\":[[\"L23_0\",[24]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F310\"},{\"id\":\"F311\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"soul\",\"g\":3,\"L\":17,\"ty\":[\"Ix\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Fetal surveillance principles\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which statement about fetal well-being assessment is false?\",\"o\":[\"Fetal breathing movements are assessed as part of the biophysical profile\",\"A low umbilical-artery systolic/diastolic ratio indicates increased placental resistance\",\"A normal fetus after 32 weeks generally shows accelerations of at least 15 beats/min lasting at least 15 seconds\",\"Reduced amniotic fluid can increase the risk of umbilical-cord compression\",\"Fetal-movement counting can be used as a simple clinical assessment of fetal well-being\"],\"a\":1,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Fetal breathing movements are one component of the biophysical profile and reflect episodic central nervous system activity.\",\"A high, not low, umbilical-artery systolic/diastolic ratio reflects increased placental vascular resistance; a low ratio does not signify increased resistance.\",\"After 32 weeks, a reactive nonstress test generally uses accelerations of at least 15 beats/min for at least 15 seconds, reflecting intact autonomic reactivity.\",\"Oligohydramnios reduces the fluid cushion around the cord and can increase umbilical-cord compression and variable decelerations.\",\"Fetal-movement counting is a simple maternal observation that can identify reduced fetal activity warranting further assessment.\"],\"e\":[\"Umbilical-artery Doppler assesses downstream placental resistance; abnormal placentation raises vascular resistance and alters the waveform.\",\"Increased placental resistance raises the systolic/diastolic ratio and pulsatility index, with absent or reversed end-diastolic flow representing more severe disease.\",\"Placental vascular obliteration reduces diastolic forward flow, so worsening resistance progressively elevates Doppler impedance indices.\"],\"tk\":\"Interpret umbilical-artery Doppler resistance correctly within fetal surveillance.\",\"pr\":\"Absent or reversed end-diastolic flow is a more concerning Doppler abnormality than an isolated elevated resistance index.\",\"tp\":\"When reduced fetal movement is reported, take it seriously and integrate gestational age, fetal heart assessment, growth, fluid volume, and indicated Doppler testing.\",\"cn\":\"A raised, not low, umbilical artery systolic/diastolic (S/D) ratio indicates increased placental resistance.\",\"sl\":\"Umbilical artery resistive indices fall progressively throughout normal pregnancy; the systolic/diastolic (S/D) ratio is about 4.0 at 20 weeks, 3.0 at 30 weeks and 2.0 at 40 weeks. Biophysical profile (BPP) fetal breathing movements: at least 1 episode of at least 60 seconds within 30 minutes.\",\"s\":[[\"L17_0\",[27,32,34]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F311\"},{\"id\":\"F312\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"soul\",\"g\":6,\"L\":33,\"ty\":[\"Physio\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"FSH and follicular development\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which hormone is secreted by the anterior pituitary and is essential for follicular growth before the dominant follicle becomes gonadotropin-responsive at later stages?\",\"o\":[\"Estradiol\",\"Luteinising hormone\",\"Progesterone\",\"Follicle-stimulating hormone\",\"Gonadotropin-releasing hormone\"],\"a\":3,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Estrogen is produced largely by granulosa cells under FSH stimulation and drives endometrial proliferation during the follicular phase.\",\"luteinising hormone (LH) is an anterior-pituitary gonadotropin important for theca-cell androgen production and later follicular maturation, but early follicular recruitment depends primarily on FSH.\",\"Progesterone is produced mainly by the corpus luteum after ovulation rather than serving as the primary pituitary driver of follicular growth.\",\"FSH from the anterior pituitary stimulates granulosa-cell proliferation, aromatase activity, and growth of antral follicles.\",\"gonadotrophin-releasing hormone (GnRH) is released from the hypothalamus and stimulates pituitary secretion of FSH and LH rather than acting directly as the anterior-pituitary follicular hormone.\"],\"e\":[\"Follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH) is secreted by anterior pituitary gonadotrophs and promotes ovarian follicular development.\",\"FSH stimulates granulosa-cell proliferation and estrogen synthesis, while LH acts predominantly on theca cells and later supports ovulation and luteal function.\",\"FSH signaling increases aromatase activity in granulosa cells, converting theca-derived androgens into estradiol during follicular maturation.\"],\"tk\":\"Identify FSH as the anterior-pituitary hormone driving early antral follicular growth.\",\"pr\":\"The midcycle LH surge triggers final oocyte maturation and ovulation; it does not replace FSH's earlier role in follicular recruitment.\",\"tp\":\"When explaining ovarian physiology, separate the two-cell, two-gonadotropin model: LH stimulates theca androgen production and FSH drives granulosa aromatization.\",\"cn\":\"Follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH) from the anterior pituitary drives recruitment and growth of antral follicles.\",\"sl\":\"Antral follicles are recruited; only follicles most sensitive to follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH) develop further, and the most FSH-sensitive follicle becomes dominant.\",\"s\":[[\"L33_0\",[3,7]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":2,\"rs\":\"F105\"},{\"id\":\"F313\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"soul\",\"g\":0,\"L\":22,\"ty\":[\"Physio\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Pregnancy laboratory physiology\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which laboratory parameter normally increases during pregnancy?\",\"o\":[\"Hematocrit\",\"Aspartate aminotransferase (AST)\",\"Plasma creatinine\",\"Alanine aminotransferase (ALT)\",\"Thyroxine-binding globulin (TBG)\"],\"a\":4,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Pregnancy causes plasma-volume expansion; albumin concentration therefore usually falls because of hemodilution, even though total albumin mass may not fall proportionally.\",\"Serum bilirubin is usually normal or slightly lower in uncomplicated pregnancy; a clear rise suggests hepatic or hemolytic pathology.\",\"Serum creatinine decreases because renal plasma flow and glomerular filtration rate rise substantially during pregnancy.\",\"aspartate aminotransferase (AST) and alanine aminotransferase (ALT) are not physiologically elevated in normal pregnancy; transaminase elevation warrants evaluation for liver or hypertensive disease.\",\"Estrogen increases hepatic thyroxine-binding globulin, which raises total T4 and T3 concentrations during pregnancy.\"],\"e\":[\"Estrogen stimulates hepatic production of thyroxine-binding globulin (TBG) during pregnancy.\",\"As TBG rises normally, total thyroid-hormone concentrations increase while free hormone remains regulated within pregnancy-specific ranges.\",\"Estrogen-driven changes in hepatic protein synthesis also affect other carrier proteins, whereas transaminases and bilirubin do not normally rise substantially.\"],\"tk\":\"Recognize increased thyroxine-binding globulin as a normal biochemical change of pregnancy.\",\"pr\":\"A creatinine that appears 'normal' for a nonpregnant adult may be abnormally high in pregnancy because physiologic glomerular filtration rate (GFR) should lower serum creatinine.\",\"tp\":\"Interpret pregnancy laboratory results using gestation-aware reference ranges, especially for thyroid tests, creatinine, hemoglobin, and alkaline phosphatase.\",\"cn\":\"Thyroxine-binding globulin (TBG) rises (doubles) in pregnancy, while creatinine and haematocrit fall and transaminases are unchanged.\",\"sl\":\"Fibrinogen, thyroxine-binding globulin (TBG), cortisol-binding globulin and caeruloplasmin rise from oestrogen-driven hepatic synthesis; alanine and aspartate aminotransferases (ALT, AST) are unchanged or slightly lower; TBG doubles.\",\"s\":[[\"L22_0\",[45,49]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F313\"},{\"id\":\"F314\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"soul\",\"g\":6,\"L\":28,\"ty\":[\"Dx\",\"Cause\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Mosaic Turner syndrome and ovarian insufficiency\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A 26-year-old woman with secondary amenorrhea is diagnosed with premature ovarian insufficiency. She is 150 cm tall and has a low posterior hairline. Which karyotype is most likely?\",\"o\":[\"46 XY\",\"45 XO / 46 XX\",\"47 XXX\",\"46 XX\",\"45 XO\"],\"a\":1,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"46,XY gonadal dysgenesis can present with streak gonads and a uterus but typically lacks estrogenization; it does not explain Turner-type short stature and somatic features as well.\",\"45,X/46,XX mosaic Turner syndrome can present with milder somatic features, short stature, and enough initial ovarian function to allow secondary rather than primary amenorrhea.\",\"46,XX primary ovarian insufficiency can cause hypergonadotropic amenorrhea with a normal female karyotype, but short stature and a low hairline suggest X-chromosome mosaicism.\",\"45,X Turner syndrome classically causes short stature, a low posterior hairline, and streak ovaries, but mosaic forms can have subtler phenotypes and spontaneous pubertal development.\",\"Nonmosaic 45,X Turner syndrome more often causes marked gonadal dysgenesis and primary amenorrhea, whereas mosaicism better fits partial prior ovarian function.\"],\"e\":[\"Turner syndrome results from complete or partial loss of one X chromosome and commonly causes short stature and ovarian dysgenesis.\",\"Secondary amenorrhea at age 26 implies that some ovarian function existed previously; together with subtle Turner stigmata, this favors a 45,X/46,XX mosaic rather than classic nonmosaic 45,X.\",\"Mosaicism leaves a population of normal 46,XX cells, allowing variable follicular reserve and pubertal development before premature ovarian failure occurs.\"],\"tk\":\"Recognize mosaic Turner syndrome as a cause of premature ovarian insufficiency with milder Turner phenotypic features.\",\"pr\":\"Turner syndrome is associated with cardiovascular and renal anomalies, so diagnosis requires more than reproductive counseling alone.\",\"tp\":\"In a young patient with ovarian insufficiency and suggestive physical features, obtain karyotyping and cardiovascular assessment rather than treating amenorrhea in isolation.\",\"cn\":\"Mosaic Turner syndrome (45,X/46,XX) can allow menarche, then premature ovarian insufficiency (POI) with short stature and low hairline.\",\"sl\":\"Turner syndrome: 45,XO or mosaicism 46,XX/45,XO; short stature, short webbed neck, low hair line; gonadal failure occurs after menarche in one third. Once ovarian failure is confirmed, consider mosaicism involving an X chromosome.\",\"s\":[[\"L28_0\",[14,15]],[\"L29_0\",[15]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":2,\"rs\":\"F314\"},{\"id\":\"F315\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"soul\",\"g\":1,\"L\":27,\"ty\":[\"Mx\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Salpingotomy when contralateral tube is damaged\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A 24-year-old nulliparous woman requires surgery for a tubal ectopic pregnancy. At laparoscopy, the contralateral tube is visibly damaged, and she strongly desires future fertility. Which operative approach best preserves fertility?\",\"o\":[\"Bilateral salpingectomy\",\"Salpingectomy with conservation of contralateral tube\",\"Salpingotomy with conservation of contralateral tube\",\"Bilateral salpingotomy\",\"Medical management of ectopic pregnancy\"],\"a\":2,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Salpingectomy removes the affected tube and is favored when the contralateral tube is healthy, but it sacrifices natural tubal fertility when the opposite tube is significantly damaged.\",\"Methotrexate is an option for selected stable, unruptured ectopic pregnancies meeting medical-treatment criteria, but a damaged contralateral tube may influence the fertility-preserving choice when surgery is undertaken.\",\"Salpingotomy removes the ectopic pregnancy while preserving the affected tube, which can help preserve future natural conception when the opposite tube is diseased.\",\"Bilateral salpingectomy eliminates natural tubal conception and would cause unnecessary tubal loss in a patient seeking fertility unless both tubes are irreparably diseased.\",\"Once operative management is required at laparoscopy, switching to medical treatment is not the appropriate fertility-preserving operative strategy.\"],\"e\":[\"For tubal ectopic pregnancy requiring surgery, the choice between salpingectomy and salpingotomy depends importantly on contralateral tubal health and fertility goals.\",\"When the opposite tube is damaged, salpingotomy may preserve the only remaining realistic route for natural tubal conception.\",\"Removing the affected tube decreases recurrent ipsilateral ectopic risk but also reduces functional tubal reserve, so fertility benefit may justify conservative surgery in selected patients.\"],\"tk\":\"Select salpingotomy when fertility preservation is important and the contralateral tube is damaged, provided conservative surgery is clinically appropriate.\",\"pr\":\"After salpingotomy, persistent trophoblast is a recognized complication, so serum human chorionic gonadotrophin (hCG) must be followed to resolution.\",\"tp\":\"Before surgery, document the patient's fertility priorities and inspect the contralateral tube, because its condition can change the preferred operation.\",\"cn\":\"With a damaged contralateral tube and desire for fertility, salpingotomy is preferred to preserve the affected tube.\",\"sl\":\"\",\"s\":[],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F315\"},{\"id\":\"F316\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"soul\",\"g\":1,\"L\":27,\"ty\":[\"Ix\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Uterine cavity evaluation in recurrent miscarriage\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A woman with three consecutive miscarriages has an hysterosalpingography (HSG) report stating that the uterine cavity could not be evaluated. Which investigation most directly allows assessment of the endometrial cavity?\",\"o\":[\"Repeat hysterosalpingography without changing technique\",\"Pelvic computed tomography (CT) scan\",\"Parental karyotyping as the next uterine-cavity test\",\"Diagnostic laparoscopy alone\",\"Diagnostic hysteroscopy\"],\"a\":4,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"A repeat HSG may again be nondiagnostic and is less direct than endoscopic assessment when the problem is inability to evaluate the uterine cavity.\",\"Hysterosalpingography outlines the uterine cavity and tubes using ionizing radiation and contrast; it can identify cavity abnormalities but does not directly inspect them.\",\"Karyotyping evaluates a parental or fetal genetic etiology for recurrent pregnancy loss rather than uterine-cavity anatomy.\",\"Laparoscopy assesses the external uterus, tubes, ovaries, and pelvic serosa or adhesions but does not directly visualize the endometrial cavity.\",\"Diagnostic hysteroscopy provides direct cavity visualization and can identify a septum, adhesions, submucosal fibroids, or polyps when prior imaging is inconclusive.\"],\"e\":[\"Recurrent pregnancy loss evaluation may include assessment for uterine-cavity abnormalities such as septa, adhesions, polyps, or submucosal fibroids.\",\"Hysteroscopy directly inspects the endometrial cavity and is particularly useful when noninvasive imaging is nondiagnostic or suggests a treatable intracavitary lesion.\",\"Direct visualization can distinguish intracavitary structural lesions and permits targeted treatment, whereas laparoscopy visualizes the external pelvis rather than the cavity.\"],\"tk\":\"Use diagnostic hysteroscopy for direct uterine-cavity assessment when prior cavity imaging is nondiagnostic.\",\"pr\":\"Modern recurrent-miscarriage work-up may also use 3-dimensional transvaginal ultrasound to evaluate uterine anatomy, depending on local expertise.\",\"tp\":\"Match the investigation to the unresolved question: if the report says the cavity could not be evaluated, choose a cavity-focused test rather than adding unrelated systemic investigations.\",\"cn\":\"Hysteroscopy directly visualizes the uterine cavity in recurrent miscarriage when hysterosalpingography (HSG) is inconclusive.\",\"sl\":\"Investigation of recurrent pregnancy loss: karyotype for both partners, hysterosalpingography (HSG) with or without hysteroscopy, pelvic ultrasound, antiphospholipid syndrome screen and thrombophilia screen.\",\"s\":[[\"L27_0\",[18]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F316\"},{\"id\":\"F317\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"soul\",\"g\":7,\"L\":38,\"ty\":[\"Dx\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Endometriosis distribution and presentation\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which statement about endometriosis is false?\",\"o\":[\"Ovarian involvement is rare\",\"Endometriosis is a benign condition characterized by endometrial-type glands and stroma outside the uterine cavity\",\"Disease burden correlates imperfectly with the severity of pelvic pain\",\"Endometriotic implants commonly occur in dependent pelvic sites such as the cul-de-sac\",\"Endometriosis commonly affects reproductive-age patients and may present with infertility\"],\"a\":0,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"The ovary is a common site of endometriosis, and ovarian endometriomas are a characteristic manifestation; ovarian involvement is therefore not rare.\",\"Endometriosis is a benign ectopic endometrial-type tissue disorder occurring outside the uterine cavity.\",\"The extent of visible disease correlates poorly with symptoms; patients with limited lesions may have severe pain and those with extensive disease may have minimal symptoms.\",\"Dependent pelvic sites, including the cul-de-sac, uterosacral region, and pelvic peritoneum, are common locations for implants.\",\"Chronic pelvic pain is a common manifestation of endometriosis, often accompanied by dysmenorrhea and deep dyspareunia.\"],\"e\":[\"Endometriosis consists of endometrial-like glands and stroma outside the uterus and often involves ovaries, pelvic peritoneum, and uterosacral regions.\",\"Ovarian endometriomas are common manifestations, so describing ovarian involvement as rare is incorrect.\",\"Repeated bleeding within ectopic implants drives inflammation, fibrosis, and adhesion formation, producing pain, distorted pelvic anatomy, and infertility.\"],\"tk\":\"Recognize the common sites and clinical manifestations of endometriosis, including frequent ovarian involvement.\",\"pr\":\"The severity of endometriosis seen at surgery does not reliably predict pain severity.\",\"tp\":\"Tailor treatment to the patient's dominant goal—pain control, fertility, or both—rather than treating the surgical stage alone.\",\"cn\":\"The ovaries and uterosacral ligaments are the commonest sites of endometriosis, so ovarian involvement is common, not rare.\",\"sl\":\"Endometriosis is endometrial tissue outside the uterine cavity, a chronic disease of reproductive-age women, most commonly on the uterosacral ligaments and ovaries, where it can form an endometrioma or chocolate cyst.\",\"s\":[[\"L38_0\",[4,5]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F317\"},{\"id\":\"F318\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"soul\",\"g\":0,\"L\":0,\"ty\":[\"Criteria\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Estimated date of delivery\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Mrs M is a 30-year-old G2P1 with a regular 28-day cycle and a last menstrual period beginning on 3 May 2019. What is her estimated date of delivery using Naegele's rule?\",\"o\":[\"06/02/2020\",\"06/01/2020\",\"10/02/2020\",\"10/01/2020\",\"08/02/2020\"],\"a\":2,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"6 February 2020 is four days earlier than the Naegele-rule date calculated from 3 May 2019.\",\"6 January 2020 shortens gestation by roughly one month and does not represent a 280-day estimate.\",\"Adding 7 days to 3 May and subtracting 3 months gives 10 February 2020, the Naegele-rule estimate.\",\"10 January 2020 is one month too early for a 280-day pregnancy dated from early May.\",\"8 February 2020 is close but does not follow the standard +7 days, −3 months, +1 year calculation.\"],\"e\":[\"Naegele's rule estimates the due date as 280 days from the first day of the LMP for a regular 28-day cycle.\",\"An LMP of 3 May 2019 becomes 10 May after adding 7 days, then 10 February after subtracting 3 months, giving 10 February 2020.\",\"This rule assumes ovulation near day 14; when cycle length or first-trimester ultrasound dating differs, crown-rump length provides more accurate gestational dating.\"],\"tk\":\"Calculate an estimated date of delivery using Naegele's rule when the LMP and cycle length are reliable.\",\"pr\":\"First-trimester crown-rump length is the most accurate ultrasound method for pregnancy dating.\",\"tp\":\"Write month names rather than numeric dates when possible; 3/5/2019 is ambiguous internationally and can produce a one-month dating error.\",\"cn\":\"Naegele's rule: add 7 days to the first day of the last menstrual period (LMP), subtract 3 months and add 1 year.\",\"sl\":\"LMP (last menstrual period) to EDD (estimated date of delivery) is 280 days / 40 weeks / 9 months and 7 days. Naegele's rule: add 7 to the LMP, add 9 months, or subtract 3 months and add 1 year.\",\"s\":[[\"L00_0\",[7]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F318\"},{\"id\":\"F319\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"soul\",\"g\":9,\"L\":42,\"ty\":[\"Mx\"],\"lv\":3,\"ti\":\"Postmenopausal bleeding during tamoxifen therapy\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A 60-year-old postmenopausal woman taking tamoxifen for breast cancer presents with postmenopausal bleeding. Transvaginal ultrasound shows an endometrial thickness of 5 mm. What is the best next step?\",\"o\":[\"Hysteroscopy and endometrial biopsy\",\"Saline hysterosonography\",\"Hysteroscopy\",\"Repeat ultrasound after 8 weeks\",\"Pipelle biopsy\"],\"a\":0,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Tamoxifen can produce endometrial polyps, hyperplasia, and carcinoma and makes ultrasound thickness less reliable; bleeding warrants endometrial evaluation, with hysteroscopy plus biopsy allowing direct assessment and tissue diagnosis.\",\"Saline infusion sonography can define focal intracavitary lesions but does not replace tissue sampling when postmenopausal bleeding requires histologic assessment.\",\"Hysteroscopy without biopsy visualizes the cavity but omits the histologic diagnosis needed to exclude hyperplasia or malignancy.\",\"Simply repeating ultrasound delays evaluation of postmenopausal bleeding in a tamoxifen user, where symptoms rather than a reassuring thickness threshold should drive investigation.\",\"A Pipelle biopsy samples endometrium and may detect diffuse disease, but focal polyps and irregular endometrial changes are common in tamoxifen users and can be better localized hysteroscopically.\"],\"e\":[\"Any postmenopausal bleeding requires evaluation for endometrial pathology, and tamoxifen increases the risk of endometrial proliferation, polyps, hyperplasia, and carcinoma.\",\"In tamoxifen users, endometrial thickness is less discriminating on ultrasound because the drug can produce cystic and irregular stromal changes; hysteroscopy with biopsy evaluates both focal lesions and histology.\",\"Tamoxifen has antiestrogenic effects in breast tissue but can act as a partial estrogen agonist in the endometrium, driving proliferation in susceptible patients.\"],\"tk\":\"Investigate postmenopausal bleeding in a tamoxifen user with direct endometrial assessment and tissue diagnosis rather than relying on endometrial thickness alone.\",\"pr\":\"In symptomatic tamoxifen users, a seemingly modest endometrial thickness does not reliably exclude focal pathology.\",\"tp\":\"For postmenopausal bleeding, document medication exposure such as tamoxifen or unopposed estrogen because it changes the pretest probability and the usefulness of ultrasound thresholds.\",\"cn\":\"In tamoxifen users with postmenopausal bleeding, endometrial thickness is unreliable, so hysteroscopy and endometrial biopsy are needed.\",\"sl\":\"Reliability of determining endometrial thickness in postmenopausal women is not applicable to patients on tamoxifen. Hysteroscopy allows direct view of the endometrium and directed biopsy.\",\"s\":[[\"L42_0\",[24,21]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":2,\"rs\":\"F166\"},{\"id\":\"F320\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"soul\",\"g\":4,\"L\":19,\"ty\":[\"Mx\",\"Ix\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Preterm labor assessment and management\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Assessment and management of suspected spontaneous preterm labor may include each of the following when gestational age and clinical circumstances are appropriate EXCEPT:\",\"o\":[\"Ultrasound assessment of fetal presentation and growth when clinically useful\",\"Group B streptococcal assessment and intrapartum prophylaxis when indicated\",\"Antenatal corticosteroids when preterm birth risk falls within the recommended gestational window\",\"Routine thyroid-function testing as part of every preterm-labor evaluation\",\"Magnesium sulfate for fetal neuroprotection at appropriate early preterm gestations\"],\"a\":3,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Ultrasound can clarify fetal presentation, growth, placental location, and selected cervical information, all of which may affect preterm-birth planning.\",\"Because preterm birth may occur before routine screening results are available, GBS status and intrapartum prophylaxis should be addressed according to current obstetric protocols.\",\"Antenatal corticosteroids reduce respiratory distress syndrome and other neonatal morbidity when preterm birth is likely within the guideline-supported gestational window.\",\"Routine thyroid-function testing is not a standard universal component of evaluating spontaneous preterm labor unless separate clinical indications for thyroid disease exist.\",\"Intravenous magnesium sulfate is offered or considered at early preterm gestations when birth is imminent to reduce cerebral palsy and severe neurologic injury.\"],\"e\":[\"Management of preterm labor is organized around confirming the diagnosis, assessing maternal/fetal status, and delivering gestation-specific neonatal benefit.\",\"Corticosteroids, magnesium sulfate, GBS planning, and selected ultrasound assessment are relevant interventions; thyroid testing is not automatically part of the preterm-labor bundle.\",\"Corticosteroids accelerate fetal lung maturation, while magnesium has a distinct fetal neuroprotective effect when very preterm birth is expected.\"],\"tk\":\"Distinguish evidence-based preterm-labor interventions from unrelated routine testing.\",\"pr\":\"Magnesium sulfate used for fetal neuroprotection and magnesium used for eclampsia seizure prophylaxis have different indications even though the drug is the same.\",\"tp\":\"When preterm labor is suspected, write down the gestational age, membrane status, cervical findings, fetal presentation, infection status, and anticipated time to birth before selecting interventions.\",\"cn\":\"Preterm labour work-up includes ultrasound, corticosteroids, magnesium sulphate and group B streptococcus (GBS) antibiotics when indicated, not routine thyroid tests.\",\"sl\":\"Fetal assessment in preterm labour (PTL): ultrasound for presentation, estimated fetal weight and amniotic fluid index, and non-stress test (NST). Management: hydration, rest, corticosteroids at 24+0 to 34+6 weeks, with or without tocolytics, magnesium sulphate (MgSO4) for neuroprotection, and antibiotics only in prelabour rupture of membranes (PROM), GBS carriers or known infection.\",\"s\":[[\"L19_0\",[28,29,31,37,38]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F320\"},{\"id\":\"F321\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"soul\",\"g\":8,\"L\":14,\"ty\":[\"Mx\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Tubo-ovarian abscess in PID\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A 25-year-old woman has bilateral lower abdominal pain, green vaginal discharge, vomiting, fever of 38.5°C, cervical motion tenderness, and adnexal tenderness despite an outpatient antibiotic course. Pregnancy testing is negative, and ultrasound shows a right tubo-ovarian abscess. What is the appropriate treatment setting and route?\",\"o\":[\"Outpatient observation\",\"Inpatient admission with oral antibiotics\",\"Inpatient observation only\",\"Inpatient admission with intravenous antibiotics\",\"Outpatient treatment with oral antibiotics\"],\"a\":3,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"A tubo-ovarian abscess is a complication of PID requiring active antimicrobial treatment and observation, not outpatient observation alone.\",\"Hospitalization is appropriate, but severe PID with abscess and vomiting requires initial parenteral broad-spectrum therapy, not oral-only treatment.\",\"Observation without antimicrobials leaves a polymicrobial pelvic infection untreated and risks rupture, sepsis, and infertility.\",\"A tubo-ovarian abscess with fever, vomiting, and failed outpatient therapy meets criteria for inpatient treatment with intravenous/parenteral broad-spectrum antibiotics and close observation.\",\"Outpatient oral treatment is inappropriate because the patient has a tubo-ovarian abscess, severe systemic features, and treatment failure.\"],\"e\":[\"PID complicated by a tubo-ovarian abscess requires hospital admission and parenteral broad-spectrum antimicrobial therapy.\",\"This patient also has fever, vomiting, a tubo-ovarian abscess, and failed outpatient therapy, each reinforcing the need for inpatient care rather than another oral regimen.\",\"Ascending polymicrobial infection can organize into an adnexal abscess, where anaerobic coverage and monitoring for rupture or failure to improve are critical.\"],\"tk\":\"Hospitalize and treat tubo-ovarian abscess with parenteral broad-spectrum antibiotics.\",\"pr\":\"Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC) guidance recommends more than 24 hours of inpatient observation for a tubo-ovarian abscess, even after initial clinical improvement begins.\",\"tp\":\"If fever, pain, or leukocytosis fails to improve after appropriate antibiotics, reassess promptly for drainage, surgery, rupture, or an alternative diagnosis.\",\"cn\":\"Pelvic inflammatory disease (PID) with tubo-ovarian abscess (TOA) or failed oral therapy requires inpatient intravenous antibiotics.\",\"sl\":\"\",\"s\":[],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F321\"},{\"id\":\"F322\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"soul\",\"g\":3,\"L\":21,\"ty\":[\"Ix\",\"Risk\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"RhD alloimmunization risk assessment\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Regarding rhesus D (RhD) alloimmunization and antenatal antibody screening, which statement is false?\",\"o\":[\"All pregnant patients should have ABO/RhD typing at booking\",\"All pregnant patients should have an antibody screen early in pregnancy\",\"If both biologic parents are RhD negative, the fetus cannot be RhD positive\",\"Anti-D immunoglobulin prevents sensitization in an unsensitized RhD-negative patient but does not treat established immune anti-D\",\"Once a RhD-negative patient has immune anti-D antibodies, paternal or fetal antigen status has no role in risk assessment\"],\"a\":4,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Routine antenatal care includes maternal ABO and RhD typing so patients at risk for RhD incompatibility can be identified.\",\"An early red-cell antibody screen identifies clinically significant alloantibodies that may cause hemolytic disease of the fetus and newborn.\",\"If both biologic parents are truly RhD negative, an RhD-positive fetus cannot result through ordinary Mendelian inheritance of the RHD antigen.\",\"Anti-D immunoglobulin is prophylaxis, not treatment; once immune anti-D has formed, management shifts to assessing fetal antigen status and surveillance for anemia.\",\"In established alloimmunization, paternal antigen testing and especially fetal genotyping can refine fetal risk, so saying antigen status has no role is false.\"],\"e\":[\"Maternal red-cell antibodies can cross the placenta and cause hemolytic disease of the fetus and newborn when the fetus carries the corresponding antigen.\",\"Booking blood group and antibody screening identify risk, while a sensitized patient requires fetal-risk assessment rather than prophylactic anti-D alone; paternal phenotype or noninvasive fetal genotyping can establish whether the fetus is antigen positive.\",\"Maternal IgG alloantibodies cross the placenta and destroy antigen-positive fetal erythrocytes, and progressive anemia is monitored with tools such as middle cerebral artery peak systolic velocity.\"],\"tk\":\"Distinguish anti-D prophylaxis from management of established RhD alloimmunization, including the role of fetal antigen assessment.\",\"pr\":\"A fetus at risk of significant red-cell alloimmunization can develop anemia before hydrops; middle cerebral artery peak systolic velocity is a key noninvasive surveillance tool.\",\"tp\":\"When an antibody screen is positive, record the specific antibody, level/titer, prior affected pregnancy, and fetal antigen status before planning surveillance.\",\"cn\":\"In established anti-D alloimmunization, paternal zygosity and fetal rhesus D (RhD) genotype (cell-free fetal DNA) are key to risk assessment.\",\"sl\":\"Diagnosis: maternal antibody screening, titer and critical titer; paternal zygosity (heterozygous versus homozygous); fetal genotype testing by amniocentesis or cell-free fetal DNA; middle cerebral artery peak systolic velocity (MCA-PSV).\",\"s\":[[\"L21_0\",[12,19]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F322\"},{\"id\":\"F323\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":8,\"L\":14,\"ty\":[\"Dx\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Vulvovaginal candidiasis\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which finding is least consistent with uncomplicated vulvovaginal candidiasis?\",\"o\":[\"Thick curd-like white vaginal discharge\",\"Intense vulvar pruritus\",\"Vaginal pH greater than 4.5\",\"Normal vaginal pH of 4.0–4.5\",\"Vulvar erythema and edema\"],\"a\":2,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"A thick curd-like white discharge is a classic clinical feature of vulvovaginal Candida infection.\",\"Intense vulvar pruritus is a prominent symptom and may be accompanied by excoriations and external dysuria.\",\"Vaginal pH greater than 4.5 is atypical for uncomplicated candidiasis; Candida usually preserves the acidic vaginal environment.\",\"A normal acidic vaginal pH is expected in candidiasis; pH elevation favors bacterial vaginosis or trichomoniasis.\",\"Vulvar erythema and edema are common inflammatory findings, especially when pruritus is intense.\"],\"e\":[\"Vulvovaginal candidiasis usually causes pruritus, vulvar inflammation, and thick white discharge while maintaining an acidic vaginal pH.\",\"The supplied option set describes Candida vulvovaginitis, and the outlying feature is a pH above 4.5 rather than the inflammatory or discharge findings.\",\"Candida overgrowth does not generate the amine-rich alkaline milieu seen with bacterial vaginosis or trichomoniasis, so vaginal pH generally remains 4.0–4.5.\"],\"tk\":\"Differentiate vulvovaginal candidiasis by pruritus, curd-like discharge, and a usually normal vaginal pH.\",\"pr\":\"Microscopy may show budding yeast or pseudohyphae, but a negative wet mount does not exclude infection when symptoms are typical.\",\"tp\":\"Before empiric treatment for recurrent symptoms, confirm the diagnosis because repeated azole exposure can select non-albicans Candida with reduced azole susceptibility.\",\"cn\":\"Vulvovaginal candidiasis keeps a normal acidic vaginal pH (4.0-4.5); pH above 4.5 suggests bacterial vaginosis or trichomoniasis.\",\"sl\":\"Candidiasis: thick white curd-like non-offensive discharge, vulval itching and soreness; signs erythema, fissuring, vulval oedema. Bacterial vaginosis raises pH from 4.5 to 7.\",\"s\":[[\"L14_0\",[3,8]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F323\"},{\"id\":\"F324\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":2,\"L\":11,\"ty\":[\"Prognosis\",\"Criteria\"],\"lv\":3,\"ti\":\"Cardiac risk stratification in pregnancy\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"In a woman with pre-existing cardiac disease, which condition represents the lowest pregnancy-related maternal risk among the following?\",\"o\":[\"Repaired coarctation of the aorta without residual obstruction or aneurysm\",\"Pulmonary arterial hypertension with Eisenmenger physiology\",\"Marfan syndrome with marked aortic-root dilatation\",\"Severe symptomatic mitral stenosis despite optimal therapy\",\"Severe systemic ventricular dysfunction with ejection fraction below 30%\"],\"a\":0,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"An uncomplicated repaired coarctation is substantially lower risk than severe pulmonary hypertension, critical stenotic valve disease, major aortopathy, or severe ventricular dysfunction.\",\"Pulmonary arterial hypertension with Eisenmenger physiology carries very high maternal risk from fixed pulmonary vascular disease and right-ventricular failure.\",\"Marfan syndrome with marked aortic-root dilatation carries substantial dissection risk as pregnancy increases aortic wall stress.\",\"Severe symptomatic mitral stenosis is poorly tolerated because pregnancy raises heart rate and blood volume, increasing the transmitral gradient and pulmonary venous pressure.\",\"Severe ventricular dysfunction markedly limits the ability to accommodate the increased cardiac output of pregnancy and belongs to the highest-risk group.\"],\"e\":[\"Cardiac pregnancy risk is lesion-specific, and contemporary assessment uses mWHO 2.0 plus individualized Pregnancy Heart Team review rather than a diagnosis label alone.\",\"Among these choices, repaired coarctation without residual disease lacks the extreme-risk features present in severe mitral stenosis, pulmonary arterial hypertension, major aortopathy, and severe ventricular dysfunction.\",\"Pregnancy increases plasma volume, heart rate, cardiac output, and vascular wall stress; fixed obstruction, pulmonary vascular disease, poor ventricular reserve, and fragile aortas therefore decompensate disproportionately.\"],\"tk\":\"Use mWHO 2.0 and lesion severity, not the cardiac diagnosis alone, to estimate maternal pregnancy risk.\",\"pr\":\"Women in mWHO 2.0 class intravenous (IV) require detailed counselling about very high maternal and fetal risk, including all pregnancy options through shared decision-making.\",\"tp\":\"Document ventricular function, valve gradients, pulmonary pressures, aortic dimensions, medications, and prior interventions before the Pregnancy Heart Team assigns risk.\",\"cn\":\"Repaired coarctation without residual obstruction is low risk, unlike Eisenmenger syndrome, dilated Marfan aortic root, severe mitral stenosis or severe ventricular dysfunction.\",\"sl\":\"Risk predictors are prior cardiac event, cyanosis or New York Heart Association (NYHA) class III-IV, left heart obstruction and ejection fraction below 40% (0 factors 5%, 1 factor 27%, more than 1 75%). Termination of pregnancy is listed for primary pulmonary hypertension, Eisenmenger syndrome, coarctation of aorta and Marfan syndrome with dilated aortic root.\",\"s\":[[\"L11_0\",[5,6,38]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F324\"},{\"id\":\"F325\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":2,\"L\":5,\"ty\":[\"Criteria\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Cytomegalovirus in pregnancy\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which statement about cytomegalovirus infection in pregnancy is incorrect?\",\"o\":[\"cytomegalovirus (CMV) is an enveloped double-stranded DNA herpesvirus\",\"CMV can be transmitted through infected body fluids\",\"Only about 20% of women with previous CMV infection remain IgG-seropositive\",\"Congenital CMV can follow primary or non-primary maternal infection\",\"Primary maternal infection early in pregnancy carries important fetal risk\"],\"a\":2,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Cytomegalovirus is a double-stranded DNA herpesvirus that establishes lifelong latency after infection.\",\"CMV is shed in saliva, urine, genital secretions, blood, and breast milk, allowing close-contact transmission.\",\"Prior infection usually produces persistent CMV IgG, so claiming that only a small minority remain seropositive is false.\",\"Congenital infection can occur after primary infection, reinfection, or reactivation, although risks differ by circumstance.\",\"Primary infection early in gestation is particularly important because fetal injury can be severe during organ development.\"],\"e\":[\"CMV is a latent double-stranded DNA herpesvirus and an important congenital infection transmitted by contact with infected body fluids.\",\"The inaccurate statement is the claim that only about 20% of previously infected women remain CMV IgG-positive; IgG generally persists after prior infection.\",\"Transplacental viral transmission can follow primary or non-primary maternal infection, while latency and immune memory explain persistent serologic evidence of previous exposure.\"],\"tk\":\"Interpret CMV IgG as evidence of previous exposure while using IgM and avidity selectively when timing is clinically relevant.\",\"pr\":\"Routine hygiene counselling is most relevant for pregnant people with frequent contact with young children's saliva or urine, because toddlers may shed CMV for prolonged periods.\",\"tp\":\"When recent maternal CMV is suspected, involve fetal medicine for gestation-specific counselling and targeted ultrasound rather than interpreting an isolated IgM result alone.\",\"cn\":\"Cytomegalovirus (CMV) immunoglobulin G (IgG) persists after past infection; 50-70% of pregnant women are seropositive, not only 20%.\",\"sl\":\"Cytomegalovirus (CMV) is a double-stranded DNA herpes virus spread by urine, saliva, semen, cervical secretions and breast milk; 50-70% of pregnant women show serological evidence of previous infection. CMV immunoglobulin G (IgG) indicates past or recent infection.\",\"s\":[[\"L05_0\",[22,23]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F325\"},{\"id\":\"F326\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":2,\"L\":5,\"ty\":[\"Cause\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Rubella in pregnancy\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which statement about rubella infection is incorrect?\",\"o\":[\"Rubella virus is a double-stranded DNA virus\",\"Population vaccination markedly reduces congenital rubella syndrome\",\"The incubation period is typically about 14–21 days\",\"Rubella is caused by an enveloped positive-sense single-stranded RNA virus\",\"Maternal infection early in pregnancy can cause congenital cataracts, deafness, and cardiac defects\"],\"a\":0,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Describing rubella as a double-stranded DNA virus is false; that genome pattern characterizes herpesviruses such as cytomegalovirus (CMV) and VZV.\",\"High rubella vaccine coverage reduces susceptible hosts and therefore lowers congenital rubella syndrome incidence.\",\"An incubation interval of roughly 2–3 weeks is compatible with rubella infection.\",\"Rubella virus is an enveloped positive-sense single-stranded RNA virus, the agent of German measles.\",\"Early gestational infection can disrupt organogenesis, causing sensorineural deafness, cataracts, and congenital heart disease.\"],\"e\":[\"Rubella is an RNA viral infection whose major obstetric importance is transplacental fetal injury during early gestation.\",\"The false virologic statement is double-stranded DNA; the incubation, vaccine effect, and congenital-malformation associations are characteristic of rubella.\",\"Maternal viremia during organogenesis can infect developing fetal tissues, so prevention through preconception immunity is far more effective than treatment after fetal exposure.\"],\"tk\":\"Recognize rubella as an enveloped positive-sense RNA virus with greatest fetal teratogenic risk after early-pregnancy infection.\",\"pr\":\"Rubella immunity should ideally be documented before conception because the live attenuated vaccine is not administered during pregnancy.\",\"tp\":\"If a pregnant patient has a suspected rubella exposure, obtain expert interpretation of exposure timing and serology rather than relying on rash appearance alone.\",\"cn\":\"Rubella is a single-stranded RNA virus (Rubivirus), not a double-stranded DNA virus.\",\"sl\":\"Rubella is caused by rubella virus, a single-stranded RNA virus, transmitted by aerosol with an incubation period of about 14 days (12-23 days). Congenital rubella syndrome includes heart defects, cataracts and sensorineural deafness.\",\"s\":[[\"L05_0\",[14,16,17]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F326\"},{\"id\":\"F327\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":8,\"L\":36,\"ty\":[\"Physio\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"External urethral sphincter innervation\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which nerve provides somatic motor control to the external urethral sphincter?\",\"o\":[\"Pudendal nerve\",\"Pelvic splanchnic nerves\",\"Vagus nerve\",\"Hypogastric nerve\",\"Femoral nerve\"],\"a\":0,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"The pudendal nerve (S2–S4) supplies somatic motor fibers to the external urethral sphincter and permits voluntary continence.\",\"Pelvic splanchnic nerves provide parasympathetic input that promotes detrusor contraction during voiding.\",\"The vagus nerve supplies thoracic and abdominal viscera but does not provide somatic innervation to the pelvic urethral sphincter.\",\"The hypogastric pathway carries sympathetic fibers that facilitate storage through bladder-neck and detrusor effects.\",\"The femoral nerve (L2–L4) innervates anterior-thigh muscles and has no motor role in urethral closure.\"],\"e\":[\"Voluntary urethral closure depends on somatic pudendal innervation arising mainly from S2–S4.\",\"The external urethral sphincter is striated muscle, so its voluntary motor supply travels in the pudendal nerve, rather than autonomic pelvic or hypogastric pathways.\",\"Somatic motor firing increases rhabdosphincter tone, helping urethral pressure exceed bladder pressure during continence and sudden rises in intra-abdominal pressure.\"],\"tk\":\"Link pudendal S2–S4 motor fibers to voluntary external urethral sphincter contraction.\",\"pr\":\"Injury to the pudendal nerve during childbirth can contribute to pelvic-floor denervation, especially after prolonged second stage or operative vaginal delivery.\",\"tp\":\"During continence assessment, distinguish voluntary squeeze strength from detrusor function because the former reflects somatic pelvic-floor recruitment.\",\"cn\":\"The pudendal nerve (S2-S4) gives somatic motor supply to the external urethral sphincter.\",\"sl\":\"Somatic: pudendal nerve (S2-S4) excitation causes contraction of the external urethral sphincter.\",\"s\":[[\"L36_0\",[2,3]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F327\"},{\"id\":\"F328\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":5,\"L\":10,\"ty\":[\"Screening\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Postpartum diabetes testing after GDM\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A woman had gestational diabetes that resolved after delivery. Which test is recommended at 6–13 weeks postpartum to exclude persistent diabetes in the National Institute for Health and Care Excellence (NICE) pathway?\",\"o\":[\"Fasting plasma glucose\",\"Random urine glucose\",\"Fasting insulin concentration\",\"Routine 75-g oral glucose tolerance test\",\"Serum fructosamine\"],\"a\":0,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"A fasting plasma glucose is recommended at 6–13 weeks postpartum in the National Institute for Health and Care Excellence (NICE) follow-up pathway after gestational diabetes.\",\"Urine glucose is too insensitive and is affected by the renal glucose threshold, so it cannot reliably exclude persistent diabetes.\",\"A fasting insulin level measures insulin physiology rather than diagnostic glycemia and cannot classify diabetes.\",\"A 75-g OGTT is not routinely offered in the NICE postpartum pathway, although other organizations use OGTT-based strategies.\",\"Fructosamine reflects short-term glycemia but is not the recommended postpartum diagnostic test after gestational diabetes.\"],\"e\":[\"Gestational diabetes identifies a population at increased future type 2 diabetes risk, so glycemia must be reassessed after pregnancy.\",\"In the current NICE pathway, women without known pre-existing diabetes receive fasting plasma glucose at 6–13 weeks postpartum; later testing may use fasting glucose or HbA1c when appropriate.\",\"Placental insulin resistance falls rapidly after delivery, so persistent hyperglycemia after the puerperium suggests underlying dysglycemia rather than pregnancy physiology.\"],\"tk\":\"Arrange postpartum glycemic testing after gestational diabetes and continue lifelong periodic diabetes surveillance.\",\"pr\":\"NICE advises against routinely using a 75-g OGTT for postpartum testing after gestational diabetes mellitus (GDM), whereas some international guidelines use different protocols.\",\"tp\":\"Before discharge, document who will order the postpartum test and tell the patient that a normal result does not remove her long-term diabetes risk.\",\"cn\":\"After gestational diabetes, a fasting plasma glucose at 6-13 weeks postpartum is recommended to exclude persistent diabetes.\",\"sl\":\"Follow-up: fasting plasma glucose test 6 to 13 weeks after the birth to exclude diabetes; after 13 weeks fasting plasma glucose or glycated haemoglobin (HbA1c); 75-g 2-hour oral glucose tolerance test (OGTT) not routinely.\",\"s\":[[\"L10_0\",[41]],[\"L26_0\",[30]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F328\"},{\"id\":\"F329\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":8,\"L\":36,\"ty\":[\"Pharm\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Duloxetine in stress urinary incontinence\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which mechanism explains the effect of duloxetine on stress urinary incontinence?\",\"o\":[\"Beta-3 adrenergic agonism that relaxes the detrusor\",\"Serotonin and norepinephrine reuptake inhibition that increases pudendal motor output to the urethral sphincter\",\"Vasopressin V2 receptor agonism that reduces nocturnal urine production\",\"Alpha-1 adrenergic blockade that reduces bladder-neck tone\",\"Muscarinic M3 blockade that suppresses detrusor contractions\"],\"a\":1,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Beta-3 agonists such as mirabegron increase bladder storage by detrusor relaxation rather than by strengthening rhabdosphincter activation.\",\"Serotonin-norepinephrine reuptake inhibition enhances central Onuf-nucleus/pudendal activity and increases external urethral sphincter contraction during storage.\",\"V2 receptor agonism reduces urine production and is used in selected nocturnal polyuria settings, not stress continence.\",\"Alpha-1 blockade lowers outlet resistance and may worsen stress leakage rather than augment urethral closure.\",\"Muscarinic blockade is used for overactive bladder because it reduces detrusor activity; it does not enhance striated urethral closure.\"],\"e\":[\"Duloxetine is a serotonin-norepinephrine reuptake inhibitor that can augment the guarding reflex and striated urethral sphincter activity.\",\"Its continence effect arises from increased central pudendal motor drive, not from antimuscarinic suppression of detrusor contractions.\",\"By increasing serotonin and norepinephrine signaling in sacral spinal pathways, motor-neuron output to the rhabdosphincter rises during the storage phase, increasing urethral closure pressure.\"],\"tk\":\"Understand duloxetine as a centrally acting SNRI that can increase urethral sphincter activity in stress incontinence.\",\"pr\":\"National Institute for Health and Care Excellence (NICE) does not recommend duloxetine as first-line treatment for predominant stress urinary incontinence; it may be considered when surgery is unsuitable or a patient prefers drug therapy after counselling.\",\"tp\":\"Discuss nausea, dry mouth, dizziness, sleep effects, and discontinuation symptoms before prescribing duloxetine for continence indications.\",\"cn\":\"Duloxetine, a serotonin-noradrenaline reuptake inhibitor (SNRI), enhances urethral striated sphincter activity centrally in stress incontinence.\",\"sl\":\"Duloxetine: potent serotonin-noradrenaline reuptake inhibitor that enhances urethral striated sphincter activity via a centrally mediated pathway; optimal effect after 4 weeks; nausea 25%; synergistic with pelvic floor muscle training (PFMT).\",\"s\":[[\"L36_0\",[61]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F329\"},{\"id\":\"F330\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":3,\"L\":23,\"ty\":[\"Dx\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Twin chorionicity and lambda sign\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"On first-trimester ultrasound of a twin pregnancy, the lambda (twin-peak) sign indicates which chorionicity?\",\"o\":[\"Chorionicity cannot be assigned from membrane insertion\",\"Monochorionic monoamniotic pregnancy\",\"Monochorionic diamniotic pregnancy\",\"Dichorionic diamniotic pregnancy\",\"Conjoined monoamniotic twins\"],\"a\":3,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Membrane insertion is highly informative when assessed early; a clear lambda sign permits chorionicity assignment.\",\"A monochorionic monoamniotic pregnancy has no intertwin membrane, so a membrane-insertion lambda sign is absent.\",\"A monochorionic diamniotic pregnancy classically has a thin membrane meeting the placenta in a T configuration.\",\"The lambda or twin-peak sign is chorionic tissue extending between the sacs and indicates dichorionic diamniotic placentation.\",\"Conjoined twins arise from late monozygotic division and are not diagnosed by a lambda sign.\"],\"e\":[\"Chorionicity should be assigned early in twin pregnancy because shared placental circulation determines several major complications and surveillance needs.\",\"A triangular projection of chorionic tissue at the membrane insertion is the lambda sign, identifying a dichorionic diamniotic pregnancy.\",\"Two chorionic layers extend between the amnions in dichorionic twins, creating the twin-peak appearance; monochorionic diamniotic twins instead form a thin T-junction.\"],\"tk\":\"Use the lambda sign for dichorionic diamniotic twins and the T sign for monochorionic diamniotic twins.\",\"pr\":\"The lambda sign becomes less conspicuous later in gestation, making first-trimester chorionicity documentation particularly valuable.\",\"tp\":\"Record chorionicity and amnionicity explicitly on the ultrasound report because management schedules differ substantially between dichorionic and monochorionic twins.\",\"cn\":\"The lambda (twin-peak) sign on first-trimester ultrasound indicates a dichorionic diamniotic (DCDA) twin pregnancy.\",\"sl\":\"Lambda sign: triangular chorionic tissue at the membrane base, dichorionic (DC); T sign: thin membrane meets the placenta at 90 degrees, monochorionic (MC). Best before 14 weeks.\",\"s\":[[\"L23_0\",[11,15]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F330\"},{\"id\":\"F331\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":7,\"L\":40,\"ty\":[\"Mx\"],\"lv\":3,\"ti\":\"Postmenopausal simple ovarian cyst\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A postmenopausal woman has an asymptomatic 6-cm unilateral unilocular simple ovarian cyst and a normal cancer antigen 125 (CA-125). What is the most appropriate management?\",\"o\":[\"Cyst aspiration under ultrasound guidance\",\"Laparoscopic bilateral salpingo-oophorectomy after low-risk assessment\",\"Repeat CA-125 alone without imaging\",\"Reassurance with no further assessment\",\"Combined oral contraceptive therapy\"],\"a\":1,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Aspiration has poor diagnostic reliability and a high recurrence rate, and spillage is undesirable if malignancy is unexpectedly present.\",\"A 6-cm postmenopausal cyst lies outside conservative size criteria; when malignancy risk is low and surgery is chosen, laparoscopic bilateral salpingo-oophorectomy is preferred over cystectomy.\",\"Serial CA-125 without ultrasound morphology cannot determine cyst persistence, growth, or development of solid features.\",\"No follow-up is now acceptable for selected asymptomatic simple cysts 3 cm or smaller, not a 6-cm postmenopausal cyst.\",\"Combined oral contraceptives do not resolve postmenopausal simple cysts and add unnecessary hormonal exposure.\"],\"e\":[\"Postmenopausal adnexal cyst management combines morphology, size, symptoms, and CA-125-based risk assessment rather than using CA-125 alone.\",\"A 6-cm simple cyst exceeds the usual conservative 3–5 cm surveillance group; if malignancy risk is low, laparoscopic surgery is appropriate and postmenopausal management generally removes both tubes and ovaries rather than performing cystectomy.\",\"Definitive adnexal removal provides histology and avoids cyst rupture or recurrence, while preoperative risk stratification identifies patients who need oncologic staging instead of routine laparoscopy.\"],\"tk\":\"Escalate a postmenopausal simple ovarian cyst larger than conservative surveillance thresholds to surgical risk assessment.\",\"pr\":\"Royal College of Obstetricians and Gynaecologists (RCOG)'s December 2025 update states that an asymptomatic, unilateral, unilocular simple cyst of 3 cm or less requires no routine follow-up.\",\"tp\":\"Calculate malignancy risk and review ultrasound for septations, nodules, bilaterality, ascites, and growth before choosing a low-risk laparoscopic pathway.\",\"cn\":\"A postmenopausal simple ovarian cyst over 5 cm warrants surgery, usually laparoscopic bilateral salpingo-oophorectomy, even with normal cancer antigen 125 (CA-125).\",\"sl\":\"A cyst in a postmenopausal woman is a tumour until proven otherwise; work-up includes ultrasound and cancer antigen 125 (CA-125). Larger simple cysts should be considered for further imaging (magnetic resonance imaging (MRI)) or surgical intervention.\",\"s\":[[\"L40_0\",[16,20]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F331\"},{\"id\":\"F332\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":4,\"L\":18,\"ty\":[\"Mx\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"PPROM and timing of delivery\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which condition is not by itself an indication for immediate delivery before 37 weeks of gestation?\",\"o\":[\"Clinical chorioamnionitis\",\"Nonreassuring fetal status\",\"Stable preterm prelabour rupture of membranes (PPROM) at 30 weeks without infection, abruption, labor, or fetal compromise\",\"Major antepartum hemorrhage with maternal or fetal compromise\",\"Severe fetal growth restriction with abnormal surveillance\"],\"a\":2,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Clinical chorioamnionitis is an indication for delivery because ongoing intrauterine infection increases maternal and fetal morbidity.\",\"Nonreassuring fetal status may require urgent delivery because continued intrauterine hypoxia can outweigh prematurity risk.\",\"Stable PPROM at 30 weeks is generally managed expectantly with surveillance, antibiotics, and corticosteroid planning when no contraindication to continuation exists.\",\"Major antepartum hemorrhage with compromise requires stabilization and often delivery regardless of prematurity.\",\"Severe fetal growth restriction (FGR) with abnormal fetal surveillance can justify preterm birth when placental insufficiency threatens fetal survival.\"],\"e\":[\"Preterm delivery is justified when maternal or fetal danger exceeds the harm of prematurity; infection, major bleeding, or fetal compromise can shift that balance immediately.\",\"A patient with uncomplicated PPROM at 30 weeks can usually continue pregnancy under expectant management rather than undergo automatic delivery before 37 weeks.\",\"Preserving gestation allows further fetal maturation, whereas chorioamnionitis, abruption, labor, or nonreassuring fetal assessment removes the safety margin for continued observation.\"],\"tk\":\"Manage early PPROM expectantly when mother and fetus remain stable and no contraindication to continuing pregnancy is present.\",\"pr\":\"Royal College of Obstetricians and Gynaecologists (RCOG) supports expectant management of uncomplicated PPROM to 37+0 weeks, while National Institute for Health and Care Excellence (NICE) advises against induction before 34 weeks without an additional obstetric indication.\",\"tp\":\"At each review for PPROM, document temperature, uterine tenderness, bleeding, contractions, fetal heart assessment, and laboratory trends rather than relying on one marker of infection.\",\"cn\":\"Stable preterm prelabour rupture of membranes (PPROM) without contraindications is managed expectantly until 37+0 weeks rather than delivered.\",\"sl\":\"Preterm prelabour rupture of membranes (PPROM) after 24+0 weeks with no contraindications to continuing the pregnancy should be offered expectant management until 37+0 weeks. Indications for delivery before 37 weeks include onset of labour, fetal distress, severe growth restriction, intrauterine death and severe bleeding or maternal jeopardy.\",\"s\":[[\"L18_0\",[14]],[\"L08_0\",[44]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F332\"},{\"id\":\"F333\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":4,\"L\":8,\"ty\":[\"Ix\",\"Criteria\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Placenta accreta spectrum — percreta\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which statement about placenta percreta is false?\",\"o\":[\"Bladder invasion can occur in severe anterior disease\",\"Placental villi can penetrate through the myometrium to the serosa or adjacent organs\",\"magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) is never useful when placenta percreta is suspected\",\"Previous cesarean birth combined with placenta previa markedly increases risk\",\"Planned delivery should involve a multidisciplinary team with major-hemorrhage capability\"],\"a\":2,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Anterior percreta may invade the urinary bladder, increasing hemorrhage and urologic injury risk.\",\"Placenta percreta is the deepest placenta accreta spectrum phenotype, with villi extending through the uterine wall and sometimes beyond the serosa.\",\"MRI can be useful selectively, particularly when ultrasound is equivocal, the placenta is posterior, or extrauterine invasion needs mapping.\",\"A low anterior placenta over a prior cesarean scar creates a high-risk setting for placenta accreta spectrum.\",\"Suspected percreta warrants planned specialist delivery with blood products, anesthesia, surgery, and critical-care support available.\"],\"e\":[\"Placenta percreta is severe placenta accreta spectrum disease in which abnormal villous invasion extends through the myometrium and may involve adjacent structures.\",\"The false absolute statement is that MRI is never useful; expert ultrasound is primary, but MRI can add anatomic information in selected difficult cases.\",\"Deficient decidualization over a uterine scar permits abnormally deep trophoblastic attachment, so forcible placental separation can open large uteroplacental vessels and cause catastrophic hemorrhage.\"],\"tk\":\"Recognize placenta percreta as deep accreta-spectrum invasion requiring expert imaging and planned multidisciplinary delivery.\",\"pr\":\"Placenta previa over a previous cesarean scar should trigger deliberate placenta accreta spectrum screening, even before symptoms develop.\",\"tp\":\"When percreta is strongly suspected, avoid attempts at manual placental removal and ensure urologic expertise is available when bladder involvement is possible.\",\"cn\":\"Magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) can complement ultrasound in suspected placenta percreta to assess depth and lateral extent of invasion.\",\"sl\":\"In placenta percreta, chorionic villi penetrate through the myometrium up to the serosa. Ultrasound by a skilled operator is highly accurate; magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) may complement it to assess depth and lateral extension, especially with posterior placentation or suspected parametrial invasion. Previous caesarean with an anterior low-lying placenta or praevia raises risk; caesarean hysterectomy with the placenta left in situ is preferred.\",\"s\":[[\"L08_0\",[45,48,49,50,51]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F333\"},{\"id\":\"F334\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":5,\"L\":10,\"ty\":[\"Complication\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Infant of a diabetic mother\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"An infant of a diabetic mother is at increased risk of all of the following except:\",\"o\":[\"Persistent pulmonary hypertension\",\"Hypoxia\",\"Hypoplastic left colon syndrome\",\"Septal hypertrophy of the heart\",\"Hypermagnesemia\"],\"a\":4,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Infants of diabetic mothers have an increased risk of persistent pulmonary hypertension, partly through delayed cardiopulmonary transition and associated respiratory morbidity.\",\"Hypoxia can occur in infants of diabetic mothers in association with respiratory distress, polycythemia, or impaired cardiopulmonary adaptation after birth.\",\"Hypoplastic left colon syndrome is a recognized neonatal association with maternal diabetes and may cause transient distal intestinal obstruction.\",\"Septal hypertrophy reflects fetal hyperinsulinemia and can produce transient hypertrophic cardiomyopathy, sometimes with left ventricular outflow obstruction.\",\"Infants of diabetic mothers are classically at risk for hypomagnesemia, not hypermagnesemia; maternal and neonatal metabolic disturbances can lower neonatal magnesium concentrations.\"],\"e\":[\"Maternal hyperglycemia causes fetal hyperinsulinemia, which contributes to neonatal metabolic, cardiac, respiratory, and gastrointestinal complications.\",\"The listed associations include septal hypertrophy, hypoxia, persistent pulmonary hypertension, and hypoplastic left colon syndrome; hypermagnesemia is the exception because the recognized magnesium disturbance is low rather than high.\",\"After birth, abrupt loss of maternal glucose supply while fetal insulin remains elevated drives early metabolic instability, while chronic intrauterine hyperinsulinemia promotes organ overgrowth and altered cardiopulmonary adaptation.\"],\"tk\":\"Recognize the characteristic neonatal complications of maternal diabetes, including hypoglycemia, cardiomyopathy, respiratory morbidity, and hypomagnesemia.\",\"pr\":\"Symmetric interventricular septal hypertrophy in an infant of a diabetic mother is often transient and may regress as the postnatal hyperinsulinemic state resolves.\",\"tp\":\"Arrange early glucose surveillance and assess respiratory status, perfusion, and feeding tolerance in infants born after pregnancies complicated by diabetes.\",\"cn\":\"Infants of diabetic mothers risk hypoglycaemia, hypocalcaemia, polycythaemia, respiratory distress and septal hypertrophy; hypermagnesaemia is not a feature.\",\"sl\":\"Neonatal complications include macrosomia, shoulder dystocia, neonatal hypoglycaemia, polycythaemia and jaundice, and postnatal adaptation problems such as respiratory distress syndrome, hypoglycaemia, hypocalcaemia, polycythaemia and hyperbilirubinaemia.\",\"s\":[[\"L10_0\",[7,26]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F334\"},{\"id\":\"F335\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":2,\"L\":22,\"ty\":[\"Dx\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Severe thrombocytopenia in pregnancy\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which condition is least likely to present with a platelet count of 50,000/µL during pregnancy?\",\"o\":[\"Thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura\",\"haemolysis, elevated liver enzymes, low platelets (HELLP) syndrome\",\"Immune thrombocytopenia\",\"Gestational thrombocytopenia\",\"Hemolytic uremic syndrome\"],\"a\":3,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura causes severe thrombocytopenia with microangiopathic hemolysis and neurologic, renal, or systemic findings.\",\"haemolysis, elevated liver enzymes, low platelets (HELLP) syndrome can cause marked platelet consumption, often with hemolysis, elevated transaminases, hypertension, or right-upper-quadrant pain.\",\"Immune thrombocytopenia can produce isolated platelet counts near or below 50,000/µL and may precede pregnancy.\",\"Gestational thrombocytopenia is usually mild, asymptomatic, and typically remains above about 70,000/µL, making a count of 50,000/µL atypical.\",\"Hemolytic uremic syndrome can cause profound thrombocytopenia with microangiopathic hemolytic anemia and prominent renal injury.\"],\"e\":[\"Thrombocytopenia in pregnancy ranges from benign gestational change to life-threatening thrombotic microangiopathy.\",\"A platelet count of 50,000/µL is unusually low for gestational thrombocytopenia, whereas HELLP, immune thrombocytopenia, thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura (TTP), and HUS can all produce severe reductions.\",\"Gestational thrombocytopenia reflects hemodilution and increased platelet turnover without systemic endothelial injury, while HELLP and thrombotic microangiopathies consume platelets in diseased microvasculature.\"],\"tk\":\"Treat a platelet count near 50,000/µL as pathologic until HELLP, immune thrombocytopenia, and thrombotic microangiopathy are assessed.\",\"pr\":\"Gestational thrombocytopenia usually appears late in pregnancy, causes no maternal bleeding, and resolves after delivery.\",\"tp\":\"Review the blood film, hemolysis markers, liver enzymes, renal function, blood pressure, and clinical symptoms before attributing severe thrombocytopenia to pregnancy alone.\",\"cn\":\"Gestational thrombocytopenia is mild (usually above 70-80 x 10^9/L); platelets of 50 x 10^9/L suggest another, pathological cause.\",\"sl\":\"Gestational thrombocytopenia occurs in about 5-10%: mild (usually above 70-80), late, asymptomatic and resolves after birth. Below 100 with raised blood pressure or liver function tests (LFTs) think HELLP (haemolysis, elevated liver enzymes, low platelets); severe or early fall suggests immune thrombocytopenia (ITP).\",\"s\":[[\"L22_0\",[25]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F335\"},{\"id\":\"F336\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":5,\"L\":26,\"ty\":[\"Cause\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Hormonal basis of postpartum blues\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which hormonal event is most closely associated with transient postpartum blues in the first days after birth?\",\"o\":[\"Abrupt increase in fetal cortisol secretion\",\"Persistent elevation of human chorionic gonadotropin\",\"Sustained maternal hyperprolactinemia alone\",\"Progressive rise in placental progesterone\",\"Abrupt withdrawal of placental estrogen and progesterone\"],\"a\":4,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Fetal cortisol secretion is no longer a continuing maternal endocrine driver after birth and does not explain the early mood syndrome.\",\"human chorionic gonadotrophin (hCG) declines postpartum and is not the principal hormonal explanation for transient tearfulness and irritability.\",\"Prolactin is important for lactation, but an isolated sustained rise does not account for the characteristic self-limited postpartum blues pattern.\",\"Placental progesterone falls rather than rises after the placenta is delivered.\",\"Delivery causes an abrupt fall in estrogen and progesterone, and this endocrine transition contributes to early postpartum mood lability.\"],\"e\":[\"Postpartum blues are common, mild, and self-limited mood symptoms that emerge soon after childbirth.\",\"The endocrine transition includes an abrupt withdrawal of placental estrogen and progesterone, interacting with sleep loss and psychosocial stress to produce tearfulness and irritability.\",\"Rapid changes in neuroactive steroid signaling alter GABAergic and monoaminergic regulation, helping explain why mood symptoms cluster in the days after placental delivery.\"],\"tk\":\"Associate postpartum blues with rapid post-delivery reproductive-hormone withdrawal and spontaneous resolution within about 2 weeks.\",\"pr\":\"Symptoms that persist beyond 2 weeks, markedly impair function, or include suicidality require evaluation for postpartum depression rather than reassurance alone.\",\"tp\":\"Ask directly about suicidal thoughts, psychosis, sleep independent of infant care, and ability to care for the newborn whenever postpartum mood symptoms are reported.\",\"cn\":\"Abrupt postpartum withdrawal of placental oestrogen and progesterone is central to the biology of postpartum blues.\",\"sl\":\"Abrupt steroid withdrawal is central to the biology of the blues; contributors include oestrogen and progesterone withdrawal, fall in the neurosteroid allopregnanolone, sleep deprivation and psychosocial stress.\",\"s\":[[\"L26_0\",[14,45]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F336\"},{\"id\":\"F337\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":2,\"L\":1,\"ty\":[\"Complication\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Maternal stroke in severe preeclampsia\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which severe hypertensive complication is a major direct cause of maternal death in preeclampsia and eclampsia?\",\"o\":[\"Uncomplicated iron-deficiency anemia\",\"Acute appendicitis\",\"Hyperemesis gravidarum\",\"Postpartum thyroiditis\",\"Intracranial hemorrhage\"],\"a\":4,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Iron-deficiency anemia can worsen reserve but does not directly cause the cerebral vascular rupture associated with severe-range blood pressure.\",\"Acute appendicitis is a surgical emergency with abdominal pain and inflammation, not a complication generated by hypertensive endothelial disease.\",\"Hyperemesis gravidarum is an early-pregnancy vomiting disorder and is unrelated to severe preeclamptic cerebrovascular injury.\",\"Postpartum thyroiditis causes transient or persistent thyroid dysfunction but does not produce the acute hypertensive catastrophe described.\",\"Intracranial hemorrhage can result from severe uncontrolled hypertension and is a major preventable cause of maternal death in preeclampsia/eclampsia.\"],\"e\":[\"Severe preeclampsia can injure the cerebral circulation, leading to ischemic or hemorrhagic stroke, seizures, and posterior reversible encephalopathy.\",\"In a patient with extreme hypertension, intracranial hemorrhage is a feared direct maternal cause of death and underlies the urgency of treating persistent severe-range blood pressure.\",\"Endothelial dysfunction and failed cerebral autoregulation expose small vessels to high intraluminal pressure, increasing edema, vessel rupture, and neurologic injury.\"],\"tk\":\"Prevent maternal stroke in severe preeclampsia by promptly treating persistent severe-range hypertension while managing the obstetric disease.\",\"pr\":\"New severe headache, visual symptoms, focal neurologic deficit, or altered consciousness in preeclampsia requires urgent neurologic assessment rather than attribution to pregnancy discomfort.\",\"tp\":\"Use a standardized severe-hypertension pathway so repeat blood pressure, antihypertensive therapy, magnesium indication, laboratory testing, and delivery planning occur without delay.\",\"cn\":\"Intracranial (cerebral) haemorrhage from uncontrolled severe hypertension is a leading direct cause of maternal death in pre-eclampsia and eclampsia.\",\"sl\":\"Maternal complications of pre-eclampsia include renal failure, acute cardiac failure, pulmonary oedema, thrombocytopenia, disseminated intravascular coagulopathy (DIC) and cerebrovascular accidents.\",\"s\":[[\"L01_0\",[50,53]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":3,\"rs\":\"F337\"},{\"id\":\"F338\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":5,\"L\":26,\"ty\":[\"Dx\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Postpartum blues diagnosis\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A woman 4 days after delivery has tearfulness, irritability, fatigue, and mood swings but remains able to care for herself and her newborn. What is the most likely diagnosis?\",\"o\":[\"Postpartum psychosis\",\"Major postpartum depression\",\"Post-traumatic stress disorder\",\"Postpartum blues\",\"Bipolar manic episode\"],\"a\":3,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Postpartum psychosis usually includes delusions, hallucinations, severe disorganization, mania, or marked behavioral disturbance and is a psychiatric emergency.\",\"Postpartum depression causes more persistent or severe depressive symptoms with impaired functioning, often lasting beyond 2 weeks.\",\"Post-traumatic stress disorder requires trauma-linked intrusion, avoidance, negative cognitions, and hyperarousal rather than isolated early postpartum lability.\",\"Postpartum blues commonly begins within several days of birth, causes mild mood lability and tearfulness, and preserves overall function.\",\"A manic episode features elevated or irritable mood with decreased need for sleep, increased energy, pressured speech, or risky behavior.\"],\"e\":[\"Postpartum blues affect many new mothers and usually begin within the first few days after birth.\",\"This patient has mild tearfulness and irritability on day 4 with preserved infant care, a pattern that favors postpartum blues over depression or psychosis.\",\"Rapid hormonal change, sleep disruption, pain, and psychosocial adjustment produce brief mood lability without sustained major functional impairment.\"],\"tk\":\"Diagnose postpartum blues when mild mood lability begins soon after birth and resolves within about 2 weeks without major functional impairment.\",\"pr\":\"Postpartum psychosis often begins abruptly within the first 2 weeks and may include delusions involving the infant, making immediate safety assessment essential.\",\"tp\":\"Provide reassurance and practical support for uncomplicated blues, but arrange reassessment if symptoms intensify, persist past 2 weeks, or interfere with feeding, sleep, or self-care.\",\"cn\":\"Tearfulness, mood swings and irritability peaking around day 4-5 with preserved self and infant care indicate postpartum blues.\",\"sl\":\"Postpartum blues: up to 80% of mothers, onset day 2-3, peak day 4-5, resolves within 2 weeks; mood is reactive and care of self and baby is preserved; symptoms include tearfulness, mood swings, irritability, anxiety, insomnia and fatigue.\",\"s\":[[\"L26_0\",[43,44]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F338\"},{\"id\":\"F339\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":5,\"L\":26,\"ty\":[\"Dx\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Postpartum depression versus blues\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which feature is more suggestive of postpartum depression than postpartum blues?\",\"o\":[\"Tearfulness beginning 3 days after delivery\",\"Mild mood lability with preserved daily functioning\",\"Depressive symptoms persisting beyond 2 weeks with impaired functioning\",\"Symptoms that resolve by 10 days postpartum\",\"Transient irritability during sleep deprivation\"],\"a\":2,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Tearfulness around day 3 is typical timing for postpartum blues when symptoms remain mild.\",\"Preserved function despite mild mood lability favors postpartum blues rather than a major depressive episode.\",\"Persistence beyond 2 weeks with functional impairment raises concern for postpartum depression and warrants formal assessment.\",\"Resolution within about 10 days is compatible with the expected self-limited course of postpartum blues.\",\"Transient irritability during sleep deprivation is common in the normal early postpartum adjustment and is nonspecific without sustained depressive symptoms.\"],\"e\":[\"Duration, severity, and functional impact separate postpartum depression from postpartum blues more reliably than a single symptom.\",\"Persistent depressive symptoms for more than 2 weeks with impaired functioning exceed the expected course of transient blues.\",\"A major depressive episode produces sustained changes in mood, interest, cognition, sleep, appetite, or psychomotor function, whereas blues resolve as the early postpartum transition stabilizes.\"],\"tk\":\"Escalate from reassurance to formal assessment when postpartum mood symptoms persist beyond 2 weeks or impair maternal functioning.\",\"pr\":\"The Edinburgh Postnatal Depression Scale is a screening tool, not a stand-alone diagnosis, and positive results require clinical evaluation.\",\"tp\":\"Ask about self-harm, thoughts of harming the infant, psychotic symptoms, and available support at the same visit rather than postponing risk assessment.\",\"cn\":\"Depressive symptoms lasting more than 2 weeks with impaired functioning indicate postpartum depression rather than blues.\",\"sl\":\"Blues last 2 weeks or less with preserved function; postpartum depression lasts 2 weeks or more with impaired function, persistent low mood or anhedonia, guilt and hopelessness (5 of 9 symptoms for at least 2 weeks).\",\"s\":[[\"L26_0\",[48,51]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F339\"},{\"id\":\"F340\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":7,\"L\":33,\"ty\":[\"Mx\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Heavy menstrual bleeding and adenomyosis\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A 42-year-old para 4 who has completed her family has 1 year of painful heavy menstrual bleeding, body mass index (BMI) 36, iron-treated anemia, a bulky uterus with normal ovaries, and proliferative endometrium on biopsy. Which treatment is best suited?\",\"o\":[\"Combined oral contraceptive pill\",\"gonadotrophin-releasing hormone (GnRH) analogues\",\"Mefenamic acid\",\"Mirena IUS\",\"Tranexamic acid\"],\"a\":3,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"A combined oral contraceptive can reduce menstrual blood loss, but age 42 years with body mass index (BMI) 36 and estrogen exposure increases thrombotic concern.\",\"gonadotrophin-releasing hormone (GnRH) analogues suppress ovarian estrogen production and can reduce adenomyosis-related symptoms, but hypoestrogenic bone loss and vasomotor symptoms limit routine long-term use.\",\"Mefenamic acid reduces prostaglandin-mediated dysmenorrhea and menstrual blood loss, but it provides less sustained endometrial suppression than an intrauterine progestogen.\",\"The levonorgestrel-releasing intrauterine system produces endometrial suppression, substantially reduces heavy menstrual bleeding, and is well suited when the uterine cavity is not distorted.\",\"Tranexamic acid reduces menstrual blood loss through antifibrinolytic inhibition of plasminogen activation and is used during menses when other options are unsuitable.\"],\"e\":[\"Heavy menstrual bleeding treatment is individualized to pathology, comorbidity, fertility wishes, and contraceptive preference.\",\"A bulky uterus with painful heavy bleeding and benign endometrial sampling is compatible with adenomyosis or nonmalignant heavy menstrual bleeding, for which an LNG-IUS is a highly effective first option.\",\"Local levonorgestrel causes endometrial decidualization and atrophy, reducing menstrual blood loss without the systemic estrogen exposure of combined hormonal contraception.\"],\"tk\":\"Offer a levonorgestrel-releasing intrauterine system as first-line treatment for many women with heavy menstrual bleeding and suspected adenomyosis when the cavity is suitable.\",\"pr\":\"Irregular bleeding is common during the first months after LNG-IUS insertion, but the reduction in menstrual blood loss generally improves with continued use.\",\"tp\":\"Before insertion, exclude pregnancy and significant pelvic infection, confirm the uterine cavity is suitable, and counsel that bleeding improvement may take several cycles.\",\"cn\":\"The levonorgestrel intrauterine system (LNG-IUS, Mirena) is first-line medical treatment for heavy menstrual bleeding (HMB) with benign histology.\",\"sl\":\"Mirena intrauterine system (IUS) reduces blood loss by up to 90%, about 30% amenorrhoeic at 12 months, and provides contraception. Levonorgestrel intrauterine system (LNG-IUS), tranexamic acid, nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs) and combined hormonal contraception are first-line for heavy menstrual bleeding (HMB).\",\"s\":[[\"L33_0\",[62]],[\"L33_1\",[17]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F340\"},{\"id\":\"F341\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":3,\"L\":17,\"ty\":[\"Ix\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Modified biophysical profile\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which antenatal surveillance test combines a nonstress test with ultrasound assessment of amniotic fluid volume and is commonly used in prolonged pregnancy?\",\"o\":[\"Full biophysical profile\",\"Contraction stress test\",\"Umbilical artery Doppler alone\",\"Modified biophysical profile\",\"Daily maternal kick counting\"],\"a\":3,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"A full biophysical profile includes nonstress testing plus ultrasound scoring of breathing, movement, tone, and fluid.\",\"A contraction stress test evaluates fetal heart response to uterine contractions and does not include ultrasound amniotic-fluid measurement.\",\"Umbilical artery Doppler assesses placental vascular resistance and is particularly useful in growth restriction, not the defined two-component test.\",\"A modified biophysical profile combines a nonstress test with assessment of amniotic fluid, providing a streamlined surveillance approach.\",\"Maternal movement counting is a symptom-surveillance method but does not combine cardiotocography with ultrasound fluid assessment.\"],\"e\":[\"A modified biophysical profile combines short-term fetal heart assessment with a marker of longer-term placental function.\",\"In prolonged pregnancy, the named two-component test is nonstress testing plus amniotic-fluid assessment, rather than a full five-component biophysical profile.\",\"Fetal heart reactivity reflects current autonomic oxygenation, while reduced amniotic fluid can signal chronic placental insufficiency through diminished fetal renal perfusion and urine production.\"],\"tk\":\"Recognize the modified biophysical profile as a nonstress test combined with amniotic-fluid assessment.\",\"pr\":\"National Institute for Health and Care Excellence (NICE) advises that women continuing pregnancy from 42 weeks after declining induction may be offered twice-weekly CTG and ultrasound assessment of maximum amniotic pool depth, while acknowledging monitoring cannot eliminate stillbirth risk.\",\"tp\":\"When arranging post-date surveillance, document the planned frequency, the fluid metric used locally, fetal-movement advice, and the threshold that will trigger delivery review.\",\"cn\":\"The modified biophysical profile (BPP) combines a nonstress test (NST) with amniotic fluid volume and is used to monitor prolonged pregnancy.\",\"sl\":\"Biophysical profile (BPP) modification: cardiotocography (CTG) with amniotic fluid volume (AFV). In prolonged pregnancy managed conservatively: at least twice-weekly CTG and ultrasound estimation of maximum amniotic pool depth.\",\"s\":[[\"L17_0\",[29]],[\"L20_0\",[12]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":2,\"rs\":\"F084\"},{\"id\":\"F342\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":4,\"L\":20,\"ty\":[\"Mx\",\"Complication\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Late-term and post-term pregnancy\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which statement about late-term and post-term pregnancy is false?\",\"o\":[\"Oligohydramnios and meconium passage become more frequent with advancing gestation\",\"Postmaturity syndrome may occur in prolonged pregnancy\",\"Placental insufficiency can contribute to fetal compromise after term\",\"Risks of stillbirth and neonatal death rise as pregnancy continues beyond 41 weeks\",\"Offering induction from 41 weeks necessarily increases cesarean birth compared with waiting longer\"],\"a\":4,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Oligohydramnios and meconium are recognized complications of prolonged gestation and placental aging.\",\"Postmaturity syndrome can occur and includes reduced subcutaneous fat, dry peeling skin, and an alert, thin appearance.\",\"Progressive placental dysfunction can reduce fetal reserve, causing oligohydramnios or abnormal fetal surveillance.\",\"Population data show increasing stillbirth and neonatal risk as gestation advances beyond 41 weeks.\",\"Current evidence does not show that induction from 41 weeks necessarily raises cesarean risk; planned induction may reduce some post-term risks.\"],\"e\":[\"Risks associated with prolonged pregnancy increase after 41 weeks, including stillbirth, neonatal death, and intrapartum complications.\",\"The false statement is that induction from 41 weeks inherently increases cesarean birth compared with simply waiting; contemporary guidance offers induction because it can reduce post-term exposure without a necessary operative-delivery penalty.\",\"As placental reserve declines, fetal oxygenation and urine production may fall, contributing to oligohydramnios, meconium, cord compression, and reduced tolerance of labor.\"],\"tk\":\"Counsel that induction from 41 weeks can reduce risks of continuing pregnancy and does not inherently require a higher cesarean rate.\",\"pr\":\"Postmaturity syndrome classically includes dry cracked skin, long nails, reduced subcutaneous fat, and meconium staining rather than fetal edema.\",\"tp\":\"When a patient declines induction, record informed discussion of gestation-specific risks, fetal-movement advice, and the limits of surveillance as a substitute for delivery.\",\"cn\":\"Induction of labour from 41 completed weeks reduces perinatal mortality and meconium and slightly decreases caesarean section rates.\",\"sl\":\"Induction of labour is preferred at 41 completed weeks and beyond, to reduce perinatal mortality, decrease meconium staining of the amniotic fluid and cause a small decrease in caesarean section. Fetal risks of prolonged pregnancy include postmaturity syndrome, stillbirth and meconium aspiration.\",\"s\":[[\"L20_0\",[4,5,11]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F342\"},{\"id\":\"F343\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":6,\"L\":28,\"ty\":[\"Dx\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Mosaic Turner syndrome\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A girl with short stature and primary amenorrhea has a 45,X/46,XX karyotype. What is the most likely diagnosis?\",\"o\":[\"46,XX congenital adrenal hyperplasia\",\"Swyer syndrome\",\"Complete androgen insensitivity syndrome\",\"Müllerian agenesis\",\"Mosaic Turner syndrome\"],\"a\":4,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"21-hydroxylase-deficient congenital adrenal hyperplasia (CAH) occurs in 46,XX individuals and causes androgen excess rather than a 45,X cell line.\",\"Swyer syndrome has a 46,XY karyotype with gonadal dysgenesis; patients usually have normal-to-tall rather than characteristically short stature.\",\"Complete androgen insensitivity occurs in 46,XY individuals with testes, absent Müllerian structures, and normal breast development but sparse pubic hair.\",\"Müllerian agenesis occurs in 46,XX individuals with normal ovarian estrogenization, normal secondary sexual characteristics, and an absent or rudimentary uterus.\",\"A 45,X/46,XX mosaic karyotype is a form of Turner syndrome and can produce short stature with variable ovarian failure.\"],\"e\":[\"Turner syndrome results from complete or partial monosomy X and may be mosaic.\",\"A karyotype containing both 45,X and 46,XX cell lines directly identifies mosaic Turner syndrome in this short patient with primary amenorrhea.\",\"Gonadal dysgenesis causes accelerated oocyte loss and hypergonadotropic hypogonadism, while SHOX haploinsufficiency contributes to short stature.\"],\"tk\":\"Identify 45,X/46,XX mosaicism as Turner syndrome with potentially variable ovarian and somatic expression.\",\"pr\":\"Mosaic patients may undergo spontaneous puberty or menstruation more often than classic 45,X patients, so phenotype cannot replace karyotyping.\",\"tp\":\"Evaluate Turner patients for cardiovascular and renal anomalies and discuss fertility and estrogen replacement needs rather than focusing only on amenorrhea.\",\"cn\":\"A 45,X/46,XX karyotype with short stature and amenorrhea is mosaic Turner syndrome.\",\"sl\":\"Turner syndrome: 45,XO or mosaicism 46,XX/45,XO, short stature about 20 cm below the female average and gonadal failure; at least 20 cells should be examined on karyotype because of mosaicism.\",\"s\":[[\"L28_0\",[14,15]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":2,\"rs\":\"F314\"},{\"id\":\"F344\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":6,\"L\":28,\"ty\":[\"Dx\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Swyer syndrome\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which finding is least consistent with Swyer syndrome?\",\"o\":[\"46,XY karyotype\",\"Short blind-ending vagina with absent uterus\",\"A uterus and upper vagina are usually present\",\"Absent or incomplete spontaneous pubertal development\",\"Streak gonads with increased gonadoblastoma risk\"],\"a\":1,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"46,XY is the characteristic karyotype in pure gonadal dysgenesis due to defective testicular development.\",\"A short blind-ending vagina with absent uterus is characteristic of androgen insensitivity or Müllerian agenesis contexts, not Swyer syndrome.\",\"Because functioning testes never produced adequate anti-Müllerian hormone, Müllerian structures are retained, including a uterus.\",\"Failure of gonadal sex-steroid production causes poor spontaneous pubertal development and primary amenorrhea.\",\"Streak gonads containing Y-chromosome material carry malignant germ-cell risk, so prophylactic gonadectomy is generally recommended.\"],\"e\":[\"Swyer syndrome is 46,XY complete gonadal dysgenesis, producing a phenotypic female with nonfunctional streak gonads.\",\"The least compatible finding is an absent uterus with a blind-ending vagina, because absent testicular anti-Müllerian hormone permits Müllerian development.\",\"Failure of testicular differentiation means little AMH or testosterone is produced, so female internal and external differentiation proceeds while gonadal steroid failure causes hypergonadotropic hypogonadism.\"],\"tk\":\"Recognize Swyer syndrome by a 46,XY karyotype, streak gonads, retained Müllerian structures, and absent normal gonadal puberty.\",\"pr\":\"Y-chromosome material in dysgenetic gonads increases the risk of gonadoblastoma and dysgerminoma, making gonadectomy an important preventive step.\",\"tp\":\"In primary amenorrhea with poor breast development, obtain gonadotropins, pelvic imaging, and karyotype when gonadal dysgenesis is suspected.\",\"cn\":\"Swyer syndrome has a uterus because no anti-Mullerian hormone (AMH) is made; an absent uterus suggests androgen insensitivity or Mullerian agenesis.\",\"sl\":\"Swyer syndrome: 46,XY, testes fail to develop so no testosterone or anti-Mullerian hormone (AMH) is produced; female external genitalia, uterus and fallopian tubes present, absent pubertal development and a 30% risk of gonadal malignancy (dysgerminoma).\",\"s\":[[\"L28_0\",[17]],[\"L29_0\",[6]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F344\"},{\"id\":\"F345\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":6,\"L\":28,\"ty\":[\"Complication\",\"Cause\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"21-hydroxylase deficiency and virilization\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"An untreated prepubertal girl with androgen excess from 21-hydroxylase deficiency is most likely to develop which manifestation?\",\"o\":[\"Infertility as the first childhood manifestation\",\"Primary amenorrhea before pubertal age\",\"Delayed puberty from isolated estrogen deficiency\",\"Peripheral precocious puberty with rapid growth and virilization\",\"Hypertension from excess mineralocorticoid activity\"],\"a\":3,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Infertility may become relevant later in life but is not the typical early manifestation of childhood androgen excess.\",\"Primary amenorrhea cannot be established before the expected age of menarche and does not capture the early androgenic presentation.\",\"Untreated androgen excess accelerates rather than delays androgen-dependent pubertal features.\",\"Excess adrenal androgens produce peripheral precocious puberty, rapid linear growth, advanced bone age, pubic hair, and virilization.\",\"Classic 21-hydroxylase deficiency lowers aldosterone synthesis and may cause salt wasting; hypertension suggests 11β-hydroxylase or 17α-hydroxylase deficiency.\"],\"e\":[\"21-hydroxylase deficiency diverts adrenal steroid precursors into androgen synthesis because cortisol production is impaired.\",\"In an untreated prepubertal girl, excess androgens cause rapid growth, advanced bone age, pubic hair, and virilization, a gonadotropin-independent precocious-puberty pattern.\",\"Reduced cortisol removes pituitary negative feedback, raising ACTH and driving adrenal hyperplasia with accumulation of 17-hydroxyprogesterone and androgen precursors.\"],\"tk\":\"Link 21-hydroxylase deficiency to cortisol deficiency, elevated 17-hydroxyprogesterone, and androgen-driven virilization or precocious pseudopuberty.\",\"pr\":\"Unlike 11β-hydroxylase deficiency, classic 21-hydroxylase deficiency does not cause deoxycorticosterone-mediated hypertension and may instead produce salt wasting.\",\"tp\":\"Assess electrolytes and hemodynamic status promptly in suspected classic CAH because mineralocorticoid deficiency can cause neonatal or childhood adrenal crisis.\",\"cn\":\"Untreated 21-hydroxylase deficiency causes androgen-driven peripheral (gonadotropin-independent) precocious puberty with rapid growth and virilization.\",\"sl\":\"Peripheral precocious puberty (pseudopuberty) is 20% of cases and always pathological. Adult-onset congenital adrenal hyperplasia (CAH) from 21-hydroxylase deficiency is a cause of virilism.\",\"s\":[[\"L28_0\",[6]],[\"L31_0\",[6]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F345\"},{\"id\":\"F346\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":7,\"L\":30,\"ty\":[\"Pharm\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Clomiphene citrate mechanism\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which mechanism best explains ovulation induction by clomiphene citrate?\",\"o\":[\"Exogenous recombinant follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH) replacement\",\"Hypothalamic estrogen-receptor antagonism that increases gonadotrophin-releasing hormone (GnRH), FSH, and luteinising hormone (LH) release\",\"Continuous pituitary GnRH-receptor agonism\",\"Aromatase inhibition within ovarian granulosa cells\",\"Direct stimulation of ovarian LH receptors\"],\"a\":1,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Exogenous FSH directly recruits ovarian follicles and is used in gonadotropin stimulation protocols rather than describing clomiphene pharmacology.\",\"Clomiphene acts as a selective estrogen receptor modulator with antiestrogenic effects at the hypothalamus, reducing negative feedback and increasing GnRH, FSH, and LH.\",\"Continuous GnRH agonism eventually downregulates pituitary GnRH receptors and suppresses gonadotropins rather than inducing ovulation.\",\"Aromatase inhibition is the mechanism of letrozole, which lowers estrogen synthesis to increase gonadotropin drive.\",\"Direct LH-receptor stimulation is the action of hCG-like agents, not clomiphene.\"],\"e\":[\"Clomiphene citrate is a selective estrogen receptor modulator used to induce ovulation in patients with an intact hypothalamic-pituitary-ovarian axis.\",\"Its dominant ovulation-induction action is blocking estrogen negative feedback at the hypothalamus, which raises endogenous GnRH and subsequently FSH and LH secretion.\",\"Increased gonadotropin drive promotes follicular recruitment and maturation, followed by an endogenous LH surge capable of triggering ovulation.\"],\"tk\":\"Explain clomiphene ovulation induction through hypothalamic estrogen antagonism and increased endogenous gonadotropin secretion.\",\"pr\":\"Hacker & Moore notes a multiple-gestation rate of roughly 6–8% with clomiphene, lower than that associated with gonadotropin stimulation.\",\"tp\":\"Before prescribing an ovulation-induction agent, document the cause of anovulation and evaluate semen and tubal factors when indicated so treatment addresses the couple rather than ovarian function alone.\",\"cn\":\"Clomiphene citrate is an anti-oestrogen that blocks hypothalamic oestrogen feedback, raising gonadotrophin-releasing hormone (GnRH), follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH) and luteinising hormone (LH).\",\"sl\":\"Ovulation induction: anti-oestrogens, e.g. clomiphene citrate, with 75% ovulating within 6 months; aromatase inhibitors for clomiphene resistance.\",\"s\":[[\"L30_0\",[19]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F346\"},{\"id\":\"F347\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":1,\"L\":27,\"ty\":[\"Dx\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Ruptured ectopic pregnancy\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A woman approximately 7 weeks from her last menstrual period has a positive pregnancy test, severe abdominal pain, and hemodynamic instability. What is the most likely diagnosis?\",\"o\":[\"Acute cystitis\",\"Uncomplicated corpus luteum cyst\",\"Threatened miscarriage\",\"Ruptured ectopic pregnancy\",\"Hyperemesis gravidarum\"],\"a\":3,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Acute cystitis causes dysuria, frequency, and suprapubic discomfort without hemoperitoneum or shock.\",\"A corpus luteum cyst is common in early pregnancy and is usually self-limited; uncomplicated cysts do not produce profound hemodynamic instability.\",\"Threatened miscarriage causes vaginal bleeding with a closed cervix and a viable intrauterine pregnancy; shock with severe abdominal pain requires another emergency diagnosis.\",\"A ruptured ectopic pregnancy can cause hemoperitoneum, abdominal pain, and hypovolemic shock in a patient with an early positive pregnancy test.\",\"Hyperemesis gravidarum causes persistent vomiting, dehydration, and weight loss rather than acute intraperitoneal bleeding.\"],\"e\":[\"Ectopic pregnancy must be considered in any early pregnancy with pain or bleeding until an intrauterine pregnancy is established.\",\"Severe abdominal pain plus hemodynamic instability at about 7 weeks is highly concerning for tubal rupture with intra-abdominal hemorrhage.\",\"Trophoblastic invasion of a fallopian tube can erode maternal vessels; once the tube ruptures, rapid hemoperitoneum causes falling preload and shock, making operative resuscitation urgent.\"],\"tk\":\"Treat hemodynamic instability with suspected ectopic pregnancy as a ruptured ectopic until proven otherwise.\",\"pr\":\"Shoulder-tip pain or syncope can reflect hemoperitoneal irritation and blood loss even when vaginal bleeding is minimal.\",\"tp\":\"Obtain rapid intravenous (IV) access, blood for crossmatch, focused ultrasound, and immediate gynecologic surgical involvement rather than delaying definitive care for serial beta-hCG testing.\",\"cn\":\"Positive pregnancy test, severe abdominal pain and haemodynamic instability in early pregnancy indicate ruptured ectopic pregnancy.\",\"sl\":\"Empty uterus, adnexal mass and fluid in the pouch of Douglas (POD) indicates ruptured ectopic: admit for assessment and surgery. Ectopic pregnancy can lead to massive haemorrhage and death; clinical triad is pain, amenorrhoea and vaginal bleeding.\",\"s\":[[\"L27_0\",[5,26]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F347\"},{\"id\":\"F348\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":0,\"L\":0,\"ty\":[\"Criteria\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Gestational age from LMP\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A pregnant woman's last menstrual period began on 14 August 2025. On 21 May 2026, what is her gestational age by menstrual dating?\",\"o\":[\"41 weeks\",\"39 weeks\",\"38 weeks\",\"37 weeks\",\"40 weeks\"],\"a\":4,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"41 weeks equals 287 days and would require another 7 days beyond 21 May 2026.\",\"39 weeks equals 273 days and remains one week short of the elapsed 280 days.\",\"38 weeks equals 266 days and underestimates the interval from 14 August 2025 to 21 May 2026.\",\"37 weeks would correspond to 259 days from the first day of the LMP, shorter than the elapsed interval here.\",\"The interval is 280 days, which equals exactly 40 completed weeks of gestation.\"],\"e\":[\"Gestational age by menstrual dating is counted from the first day of the last menstrual period, not from estimated conception.\",\"From 14 August 2025 to 21 May 2026 is 280 days, corresponding to 40 weeks exactly.\",\"Naegele-style dating uses a 280-day or 40-week pregnancy interval from the LMP because ovulation and fertilization usually occur about 2 weeks after menstrual dating begins.\"],\"tk\":\"Calculate menstrual gestational age from the first day of the LMP using a 280-day reference pregnancy.\",\"pr\":\"First-trimester crown-rump length is generally more accurate than menstrual dating when the LMP is uncertain or cycles are irregular.\",\"tp\":\"When LMP and early ultrasound dating differ beyond the accepted threshold, use the locally adopted redating criteria and document the final estimated due date once.\",\"cn\":\"Gestational age is counted from the first day of the last menstrual period (LMP); the due date is 280 days (40 weeks).\",\"sl\":\"LMP (last menstrual period) to EDD (estimated date of delivery) is 280 days / 40 weeks / 9 months and 7 days, giving the estimated gestational age (EGA).\",\"s\":[[\"L00_0\",[7]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F348\"},{\"id\":\"F349\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":2,\"L\":1,\"ty\":[\"Pharm\",\"Complication\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Magnesium sulfate toxicity\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which findings and management are most appropriate for magnesium sulfate toxicity during treatment for preeclampsia?\",\"o\":[\"Hyporeflexia and respiratory depression; stop magnesium and give intravenous calcium gluconate\",\"Hypertension and tremor; repeat the magnesium loading dose\",\"Isolated bradycardia; give oral calcium carbonate\",\"Polyuria and agitation; administer intravenous potassium\",\"Hyperreflexia and tachypnea; continue the infusion\"],\"a\":0,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Loss of deep-tendon reflexes and respiratory depression indicate magnesium toxicity; the infusion should be stopped and IV calcium gluconate administered as an antagonist.\",\"An additional magnesium loading dose would worsen toxicity and is not treatment for hypertension or tremor.\",\"Oral calcium carbonate acts too slowly for clinically important magnesium toxicity and is not the emergency antidote.\",\"Polyuria and agitation are not the classic toxicity pattern, and potassium does not reverse magnesium-induced neuromuscular depression.\",\"Hyperreflexia is more typical of severe preeclamptic neuromuscular irritability; magnesium toxicity suppresses reflexes and respiration.\"],\"e\":[\"Magnesium sulfate prevents and treats eclamptic seizures but can depress neuromuscular and respiratory function at toxic concentrations.\",\"The combination of hyporeflexia and respiratory depression requires stopping magnesium and giving intravenous calcium gluconate while supporting airway and breathing.\",\"Magnesium reduces acetylcholine release and membrane excitability; calcium antagonizes magnesium at neuromuscular and cardiac sites, helping reverse life-threatening toxicity.\"],\"tk\":\"Recognize loss of reflexes and respiratory depression as magnesium toxicity requiring immediate cessation and intravenous calcium gluconate.\",\"pr\":\"Renal impairment increases toxicity risk because magnesium is cleared primarily by the kidneys; oliguria should prompt dose and level review.\",\"tp\":\"During magnesium therapy, chart respiratory rate, patellar reflexes, urine output, and renal function, and keep calcium gluconate immediately accessible.\",\"cn\":\"Magnesium sulphate toxicity causes loss of reflexes and respiratory depression; stop the infusion and give 1 g intravenous (IV) calcium gluconate.\",\"sl\":\"Monitor hourly for toxicity: loss of patellar reflexes (8-10 mEq/L), respiratory depression or arrest (12 mEq/L), mental status changes then ECG (electrocardiogram) changes. If toxicity develops stop magnesium sulphate, check vital signs and levels, and give 1 g calcium gluconate intravenously (IV) over 3 minutes.\",\"s\":[[\"L01_0\",[47]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":2,\"rs\":\"F349\"},{\"id\":\"F350\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":0,\"L\":27,\"ty\":[\"Physio\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Early ultrasound cardiac activity\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"When can embryonic cardiac activity usually first be detected by transvaginal ultrasound?\",\"o\":[\"4 weeks of gestation\",\"7–8 weeks of gestation\",\"5–6 weeks of gestation\",\"10–12 weeks of gestation\",\"14 weeks of gestation\"],\"a\":2,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"At 4 weeks, a gestational sac may just become visible, but an embryo with detectable cardiac activity is generally not yet seen.\",\"By 7–8 weeks, cardiac activity should usually be evident in a normally developing pregnancy, but this is later than the earliest typical detection.\",\"With high-resolution transvaginal ultrasound, embryonic cardiac activity is commonly detectable around 5.5–6 weeks once the embryo is visible.\",\"10–12 weeks is well beyond the usual transvaginal sonographic detection window and closer to routine Doppler auscultation timing.\",\"At 14 weeks, sonographic cardiac activity has been visible for many weeks in a normal pregnancy.\"],\"e\":[\"Early transvaginal ultrasound can visualize embryonic cardiac activity soon after the embryo becomes identifiable.\",\"Cardiac motion is commonly seen at approximately 5.5–6 weeks of gestation, although exact visibility depends on dating accuracy and embryo size.\",\"Early cardiac development begins before reliable imaging; increasing crown-rump length and transvaginal resolution make motion detectable before transabdominal or handheld Doppler methods are dependable.\"],\"tk\":\"Expect transvaginal detection of embryonic cardiac activity at roughly 5.5–6 weeks when dating and visualization are adequate.\",\"pr\":\"Absence of cardiac activity in a very small embryo should not trigger a miscarriage diagnosis unless validated ultrasound size and repeat-scan criteria are met.\",\"tp\":\"Record mean sac diameter or crown-rump length and scan route when viability is uncertain so the follow-up interval can be chosen safely.\",\"cn\":\"Embryonic cardiac activity is first seen on transvaginal ultrasound at about 6 weeks (5-6 weeks).\",\"sl\":\"Fetal pole at gestational sac 18 mm; fetal heart (FH) at 6 weeks (crown-rump length (CRL) 4-10 mm).\",\"s\":[[\"L27_0\",[4]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F350\"},{\"id\":\"F351\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":2,\"L\":1,\"ty\":[\"Mx\"],\"lv\":3,\"ti\":\"Severe preeclampsia at 35 weeks\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A woman at 35+2 weeks has persistent blood pressure of 165/115 mmHg and right-upper-quadrant pain. What is the most appropriate management?\",\"o\":[\"Maternal stabilization followed by planned delivery\",\"Tocolysis to prolong pregnancy to 39 weeks\",\"Discharge with outpatient blood-pressure review in 1 week\",\"Expectant management without antihypertensive treatment until 37 weeks\",\"Oral iron therapy and routine antenatal follow-up\"],\"a\":0,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"At 35+2 weeks with severe hypertension and right-upper-quadrant pain, urgent stabilization with antihypertensive therapy and seizure prophylaxis as indicated should be followed by delivery planning.\",\"Tocolysis has no role in prolonging pregnancy when severe hypertensive disease threatens maternal safety.\",\"Outpatient review is unsafe with severe-range hypertension and upper-abdominal pain because maternal stroke, eclampsia, and hepatic complications can evolve rapidly.\",\"Untreated severe-range hypertension cannot be observed expectantly without active maternal therapy and senior obstetric assessment.\",\"Iron therapy does not address severe hypertension, seizure risk, hepatic involvement, or the placental disease driving this presentation.\"],\"e\":[\"Severe preeclampsia requires immediate maternal assessment and stabilization, especially when severe-range hypertension is accompanied by symptoms of end-organ involvement.\",\"Blood pressure of 165/115 mmHg with right-upper-quadrant pain at 35+2 weeks indicates severe disease, making delivery appropriate once acute maternal risks are controlled.\",\"Placental dysfunction drives systemic endothelial injury; delivery removes the placenta, while rapid blood-pressure control and magnesium when indicated reduce stroke and eclampsia risk before birth.\"],\"tk\":\"Manage severe preeclampsia after 34 weeks with maternal stabilization and timely delivery when severe features are present.\",\"pr\":\"Right-upper-quadrant or epigastric pain can reflect hepatic swelling or ischemia and may precede worsening transaminases, thrombocytopenia, or subcapsular hematoma.\",\"tp\":\"Repeat severe blood pressure promptly, obtain complete blood count (CBC), creatinine and liver enzymes, assess fetal status, treat persistent severe hypertension, and involve senior obstetric staff immediately.\",\"cn\":\"Pre-eclampsia with severe features (blood pressure 160/110 mmHg or more, right upper quadrant pain) needs maternal stabilisation followed by urgent delivery.\",\"sl\":\"Severe features include persistent blood pressure of 160 systolic or 110 diastolic and persistent epigastric or right upper quadrant pain. Management of pre-eclampsia with severe features: urgent delivery after maternal stabilisation (intravenous antihypertensive, +/- magnesium sulphate, corticosteroids that should not delay delivery).\",\"s\":[[\"L01_0\",[36,37,43]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":2,\"rs\":\"F351\"},{\"id\":\"F352\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":3,\"L\":23,\"ty\":[\"Dx\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Twin-to-twin transfusion syndrome\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A monochorionic diamniotic twin pregnancy develops oligohydramnios around one fetus and polyhydramnios around the other. What is the most likely diagnosis?\",\"o\":[\"Twin reversed arterial perfusion sequence\",\"Twin-to-twin transfusion syndrome\",\"Dichorionic discordant growth\",\"Selective fetal growth restriction without fluid discordance\",\"Twin anemia-polycythemia sequence\"],\"a\":1,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"TRAP sequence involves an acardiac or severely malformed perfused twin supplied retrogradely by a pump twin, a different monochorionic complication.\",\"Twin-to-twin transfusion syndrome results from unbalanced placental vascular anastomoses and classically produces a donor oligohydramnios sac and recipient polyhydramnios sac.\",\"Dichorionic twins lack shared placental vascular anastomoses and therefore do not develop classic TTTS.\",\"Selective fetal growth restriction can coexist with fluid abnormalities but is primarily defined by growth and placental-share discordance rather than the characteristic donor-recipient fluid sequence.\",\"Twin anemia-polycythemia sequence is defined by marked intertwin hemoglobin discordance, often with abnormal middle cerebral artery (MCA) Dopplers, and may occur without the classic oligo-polyhydramnios sequence.\"],\"e\":[\"Twin-to-twin transfusion syndrome occurs only in monochorionic pregnancies with shared placental vascular connections.\",\"The characteristic ultrasound pattern is oligohydramnios in the donor sac and polyhydramnios in the recipient sac, reflecting chronic net transfusion between twins.\",\"Intertwin arteriovenous anastomoses create volume imbalance: the donor develops hypovolemia and reduced urine output, while recipient hypervolemia increases renal perfusion and urine production.\"],\"tk\":\"Recognize oligohydramnios-polyhydramnios sequence in monochorionic twins as classic twin-to-twin transfusion syndrome.\",\"pr\":\"Society for Maternal-Fetal Medicine (SMFM) recommends beginning ultrasound surveillance for TTTS in monochorionic diamniotic twins at 16 weeks and at least every 2 weeks thereafter.\",\"tp\":\"Once TTTS is suspected, document deepest vertical pockets, donor bladder visibility, Dopplers, fetal growth, and Quintero stage and refer promptly to a fetal-therapy center when criteria are met.\",\"cn\":\"Oligohydramnios in one sac and polyhydramnios in the other in monochorionic diamniotic twins is twin-to-twin transfusion syndrome (TTTS).\",\"sl\":\"Twin-to-twin transfusion syndrome (TTTS): donor deepest vertical pocket (DVP) 2 cm or less and recipient DVP 8 cm or more up to 20 weeks (10 cm or more after 20 weeks); Quintero stage I is the polyhydramnios-oligohydramnios sequence.\",\"s\":[[\"L23_0\",[35,36]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":2,\"rs\":\"F352\"},{\"id\":\"F353\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":4,\"L\":9,\"ty\":[\"Pharm\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Oxytocin prophylaxis at cesarean birth\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"In the UK/National Institute for Health and Care Excellence (NICE) active-management regimen at cesarean birth, which uterotonic dose is given immediately after delivery to reduce postpartum hemorrhage risk?\",\"o\":[\"Oxytocin 5 IU by intravenous injection\",\"Methotrexate 50 mg/m² intramuscularly\",\"Misoprostol 50 micrograms orally\",\"Magnesium sulfate 4 g intravenously\",\"Oxytocin 1 IU by intramuscular injection\"],\"a\":0,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"In the UK/National Institute for Health and Care Excellence (NICE) cesarean regimen, oxytocin 5 IU IV is used immediately after birth to encourage uterine contraction and reduce blood loss.\",\"Methotrexate is an antimetabolite used in selected ectopic pregnancy and gestational trophoblastic disease contexts, not uterine atony prophylaxis.\",\"Misoprostol 50 micrograms is not the standard cesarean prophylactic uterotonic dose and this amount is far below common PPH-prevention regimens.\",\"Magnesium sulfate relaxes smooth muscle and is used for eclampsia seizure prophylaxis or fetal neuroprotection, not prevention of uterine atony.\",\"Oxytocin 1 IU IM is below the standard prophylactic dose used in the cited UK active-management regimen.\"],\"e\":[\"Active management after cesarean birth includes a prophylactic uterotonic to promote prompt uterine contraction.\",\"Under the UK/NICE regimen specified here, 5 IU intravenous oxytocin is administered immediately after delivery; this framing avoids conflating the UK regimen with World Health Organization (WHO)'s broader 10-IU recommendation for all births.\",\"Oxytocin activates myometrial Gq-coupled receptors, increasing intracellular calcium and coordinated uterine contraction that compresses placental-bed vessels.\"],\"tk\":\"Use 5 IU IV oxytocin for the specified UK/NICE cesarean active-management regimen while following local protocols for dose and infusion strategy.\",\"pr\":\"WHO's 2025 consolidated PPH guidance recommends oxytocin 10 IU IM/IV for prevention of PPH for all births, illustrating legitimate protocol variation across authorities.\",\"tp\":\"Confirm the institutional uterotonic protocol before cesarean delivery and modify second-line agents for contraindications such as hypertension, asthma, or cardiac disease.\",\"cn\":\"For postpartum haemorrhage (PPH) prevention at caesarean section, oxytocin 5 IU is given intravenously (IV).\",\"sl\":\"Uterotonics for prevention of postpartum haemorrhage (PPH): 10 IU oxytocin intramuscularly (IM) after vaginal delivery, 5 IU oxytocin intravenously (IV) after caesarean section (CS); carbetocin 100 micrograms IV over 1 minute can be used at CS.\",\"s\":[[\"L09_0\",[6]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":2,\"rs\":\"F353\"},{\"id\":\"F354\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":6,\"L\":33,\"ty\":[\"Physio\",\"Criteria\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Timing of ovulation after LH surge\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Ovulation usually occurs approximately how long after the onset of the luteinizing hormone surge?\",\"o\":[\"6–8 hours\",\"34–36 hours\",\"12–18 hours\",\"5–7 days\",\"14 days\"],\"a\":1,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"6–8 hours is generally too early for follicular rupture after the LH surge begins.\",\"Ovulation usually occurs about 34–36 hours after the onset of the LH surge and roughly 10–12 hours after its peak.\",\"12–18 hours is closer to the interval after the LH peak than to the interval from the beginning of the surge.\",\"5–7 days after the surge is well into the luteal phase, after corpus luteum formation.\",\"About 14 days approximates the duration from ovulation to the next menstrual period in many cycles, not the surge-to-ovulation interval.\"],\"e\":[\"The LH surge initiates final oocyte maturation, cumulus expansion, luteinization, and follicular rupture.\",\"Ovulation generally follows the onset of the LH surge by about 34–36 hours, with a shorter interval from the LH peak.\",\"LH stimulates proteolytic enzymes and prostaglandins in the dominant follicle; these processes weaken the follicular wall until oocyte release occurs.\"],\"tk\":\"Remember ovulation at roughly 34–36 hours after LH-surge onset and about 10–12 hours after its peak.\",\"pr\":\"Urinary LH predictor kits identify the preovulatory surge and therefore provide a practical fertile-window marker before actual follicular rupture.\",\"tp\":\"When counseling for timed intercourse or insemination, explain that an LH-positive test predicts imminent ovulation rather than proving that ovulation has already occurred.\",\"cn\":\"Ovulation occurs 34-36 hours after the onset of the luteinizing hormone (LH) surge.\",\"sl\":\"The onset of the luteinizing hormone (LH) surge occurs 34-36 hours before ovulation and reliably indicates the timing of ovulation.\",\"s\":[[\"L33_0\",[9]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F354\"},{\"id\":\"F355\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":1,\"L\":27,\"ty\":[\"Mx\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Methotrexate follow-up for ectopic pregnancy\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"After single-dose methotrexate for a stable ectopic pregnancy, which beta-hCG pattern indicates an appropriate early treatment response?\",\"o\":[\"A mandatory decline from day 1 to day 4\",\"A fall of less than 5% from day 4 to day 7\",\"An initial plateau or rise by day 4 followed by at least a 15% fall from day 4 to day 7\",\"Immediate normalization within 24 hours\",\"A rise through day 7 with no subsequent fall\"],\"a\":2,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"A fall before day 4 can occur, but a day-1 to day-4 decline is not required for the standard single-dose response assessment.\",\"A less than 5% decline from day 4 to day 7 is inadequate and should trigger protocol-directed reassessment.\",\"Beta-hCG may initially plateau or rise; an at least 15% fall from day 4 to day 7 is the usual response threshold in the single-dose protocol.\",\"Immediate beta-hCG normalization is not expected because trophoblastic tissue takes time to regress after methotrexate.\",\"A continued rise through day 7 without an adequate subsequent decline suggests treatment failure or need for additional assessment rather than expected response.\"],\"e\":[\"Methotrexate destroys proliferating trophoblast gradually, so beta-hCG does not need to fall immediately after treatment.\",\"In the single-dose protocol, a plateau or rise can occur by day 4, but an at least 15% decline between days 4 and 7 indicates an adequate early response.\",\"Folate antagonism arrests trophoblastic DNA synthesis; as viable trophoblast decreases, beta-hCG falls over days to weeks, requiring continued serial monitoring until resolution.\"],\"tk\":\"Assess single-dose methotrexate response using the day-4 to day-7 beta-hCG decline, with at least 15% fall as the usual adequacy threshold.\",\"pr\":\"Tubal rupture can occur despite falling beta-hCG, so biochemical improvement does not replace clinical assessment of new pain, dizziness, or hemodynamic change.\",\"tp\":\"Give written rupture precautions and arrange every scheduled beta-hCG measurement before the patient leaves the treatment area.\",\"cn\":\"After single-dose methotrexate, a beta human chorionic gonadotrophin (beta-hCG) fall of at least 15% between day 4 and day 7 indicates response.\",\"sl\":\"\",\"s\":[],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F355\"},{\"id\":\"F356\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":1,\"L\":27,\"ty\":[\"Ix\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Antiphospholipid testing in recurrent miscarriage\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A couple presents after recurrent miscarriages. Which laboratory investigation should be included early because it identifies an important treatable cause of recurrent pregnancy loss?\",\"o\":[\"Broad inherited thrombophilia panel in every patient\",\"Antiphospholipid antibody testing\",\"Serial serum cancer antigen 125 (CA-125)\",\"Parental karyotyping as the only initial investigation\",\"Routine TORCH serology\"],\"a\":1,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Universal inherited thrombophilia screening is not recommended for recurrent miscarriage in the absence of additional indications.\",\"Testing for lupus anticoagulant and anticardiolipin antibodies identifies antiphospholipid syndrome, an established and treatable cause of recurrent pregnancy loss.\",\"cancer antigen 125 (CA-125) is an adnexal-mass biomarker and has no role in routine recurrent-miscarriage evaluation.\",\"Parental karyotyping is useful selectively, especially after an unbalanced fetal chromosomal result or relevant family history, but it is not the sole initial test.\",\"Routine TORCH serology is not recommended as a standard explanation-seeking panel for recurrent miscarriage without specific infectious evidence.\"],\"e\":[\"Recurrent miscarriage evaluation prioritizes causes that are evidence-based and potentially actionable, including antiphospholipid syndrome and uterine or selected genetic factors.\",\"Antiphospholipid antibody testing is an important early laboratory investigation because persistent antibodies plus qualifying pregnancy morbidity can establish antiphospholipid syndrome (APS).\",\"Antiphospholipid antibodies promote thrombosis and placental dysfunction; when obstetric APS is confirmed, aspirin plus heparin can improve live-birth outcomes in appropriate patients.\"],\"tk\":\"Include antiphospholipid antibody testing in evidence-based evaluation of recurrent miscarriage.\",\"pr\":\"APS laboratory criteria require persistent positivity on 2 occasions at least 12 weeks apart, so a single positive test does not establish the diagnosis.\",\"tp\":\"Order the defined APS panel rather than indiscriminate thrombophilia testing, and plan repeat confirmation at the correct interval if the first result is positive.\",\"cn\":\"Antiphospholipid antibody testing (anticardiolipin and lupus anticoagulant) is a key early investigation in recurrent miscarriage because the syndrome is treatable.\",\"sl\":\"Antiphospholipid antibody syndrome accounts for 15% of recurrent miscarriage; screen with anticardiolipin antibodies and lupus anticoagulant; treat with low-molecular-weight heparin (LMWH) and low-dose aspirin (live birth rate 70% vs 10% untreated).\",\"s\":[[\"L27_0\",[17,18,23]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F356\"},{\"id\":\"F357\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":0,\"L\":1,\"ty\":[\"Screening\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Antenatal proteinuria screening\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which test is recommended for routine screening for proteinuria at face-to-face antenatal visits?\",\"o\":[\"Spot urine protein-to-creatinine ratio for every asymptomatic visit\",\"Serum creatinine\",\"24-hour urine protein collection\",\"Renal ultrasound\",\"Urine dipstick testing\"],\"a\":4,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"A spot protein-to-creatinine ratio is used to quantify significant proteinuria after a positive screen or clinical concern rather than as the universal first screen.\",\"Serum creatinine assesses renal filtration and may be indicated in hypertensive disease, but it does not screen urine for protein.\",\"A 24-hour urine protein collection can quantify protein loss but is cumbersome and is not used as the routine screening test at every antenatal visit.\",\"Renal ultrasound evaluates anatomy and obstruction and cannot serve as a routine biochemical proteinuria screen.\",\"Urine dipstick testing is recommended at routine face-to-face antenatal appointments to screen for proteinuria.\"],\"e\":[\"Proteinuria screening is part of routine antenatal surveillance because new protein loss may signal preeclampsia or renal disease.\",\"National Institute for Health and Care Excellence (NICE) recommends urine dipstick testing at every routine face-to-face antenatal appointment; a positive result can then be quantified using a validated protein-to-creatinine or albumin-to-creatinine strategy.\",\"Glomerular endothelial injury in preeclampsia increases urinary protein permeability, so a simple screening test helps identify patients who need blood-pressure review and diagnostic assessment.\"],\"tk\":\"Use urine dipstick testing for routine antenatal proteinuria screening and quantify significant results rather than relying on dipstick alone for diagnosis.\",\"pr\":\"Proteinuria is not required for the diagnosis of preeclampsia when hypertension is accompanied by qualifying maternal organ dysfunction or uteroplacental dysfunction.\",\"tp\":\"Whenever a dipstick is positive, check the blood pressure and symptoms at the same encounter and arrange quantitative testing according to the hypertensive-disorders pathway.\",\"cn\":\"Routine proteinuria screening in pregnancy uses urine dipstick (reagent strip); a positive result is quantified by protein:creatinine ratio.\",\"sl\":\"Use an automated reagent-strip reading device for dipstick screening for proteinuria; if positive (1+ or more), use spot protein:creatinine or albumin:creatinine ratio; gold standard to quantify is 24-hour urine collection.\",\"s\":[[\"L01_0\",[29]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F357\"},{\"id\":\"F358\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":4,\"L\":20,\"ty\":[\"Criteria\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Bishop score\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which finding is not included in the Bishop score used to assess cervical favorability before induction of labor?\",\"o\":[\"Cervical dilatation\",\"Cervical effacement or length\",\"Fetal station\",\"Fetal presentation\",\"Cervical consistency\"],\"a\":3,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Cervical dilatation reflects mechanical opening of the cervix and contributes directly to formal cervical-favorability scoring before induction.\",\"Cervical effacement or length is scored because progressive shortening indicates cervical ripening.\",\"Fetal station is scored relative to the ischial spines and reflects descent of the presenting part.\",\"Fetal presentation is documented before induction because malpresentation alters delivery planning, but it is not one of the five Bishop score variables.\",\"A soft cervix indicates advancing collagen remodeling and receives a more favorable score before induction.\"],\"e\":[\"The Bishop score estimates readiness for induction using cervical dilatation, effacement or length, consistency, position, and fetal station.\",\"Fetal presentation is assessed separately; a cephalic fetus can still have an unfavorable cervix and a breech fetus can have a high Bishop score.\",\"Cervical ripening involves collagen degradation and increased tissue hydration, while descent changes station, so these variables predict how readily induction may establish labor.\"],\"tk\":\"Bishop score assessment should separate cervical favorability from fetal presentation and other prerequisites for induction.\",\"pr\":\"A modified Bishop score may substitute cervical length for effacement, but presentation still remains outside the score.\",\"tp\":\"Before inducing labor, document presentation, placental location, membrane status, and fetal wellbeing in addition to the Bishop score.\",\"cn\":\"The Bishop score includes cervical dilatation, effacement, consistency, position and fetal station, but not fetal presentation.\",\"sl\":\"The Bishop score is based on the dilation, effacement (or length), position and consistency of the cervix and the station of the head with respect to the ischial spines.\",\"s\":[[\"L20_0\",[20]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":2,\"rs\":\"F358\"},{\"id\":\"F359\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":6,\"L\":33,\"ty\":[\"Physio\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Dominant follicle selection\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which statement best describes selection of the dominant follicle during the follicular phase?\",\"o\":[\"Progesterone is the dominant ovarian steroid throughout the early follicular phase\",\"The corpus luteum secretes peak progesterone before ovulation\",\"The dominant follicle has increased follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH) responsiveness and aromatase activity\",\"Estradiol suppresses luteinising hormone (LH) throughout the entire follicular phase\",\"Inhibin A is the principal inhibin produced by small growing follicles\"],\"a\":2,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Progesterone is low during the follicular phase and rises after ovulation when the corpus luteum forms.\",\"The corpus luteum exists only after follicular rupture, so its peak progesterone secretion occurs during the mid-luteal phase.\",\"The selected follicle develops greater FSH responsiveness and granulosa-cell aromatase activity, allowing continued estradiol production as circulating FSH declines.\",\"Sustained high estradiol near mid-cycle switches to positive feedback and promotes the luteinising hormone (LH) surge rather than suppressing LH continuously.\",\"Growing follicles predominantly produce inhibin B; inhibin A becomes more prominent from the corpus luteum.\"],\"e\":[\"Early follicular FSH recruits a cohort of follicles, and one becomes dominant through greater gonadotropin sensitivity and estradiol-producing capacity.\",\"The dominant follicle can continue growing while FSH concentrations fall because its granulosa cells express more FSH receptors and efficient aromatase.\",\"Rising estradiol and inhibin B reduce pituitary FSH, causing less competitive follicles to undergo atresia while the dominant follicle persists.\"],\"tk\":\"Dominant follicle selection depends on increasing FSH sensitivity rather than persistently high circulating FSH.\",\"pr\":\"Granulosa-cell aromatase converts theca-derived androstenedione to estradiol under FSH stimulation.\",\"tp\":\"When interpreting cycle-day hormone tests, relate results to the follicular versus luteal phase rather than applying one reference range across the cycle.\",\"cn\":\"The dominant follicle is the one most sensitive to follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH), producing the most estradiol and inhibin B.\",\"sl\":\"Only the follicles most sensitive to follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH) undergo further development; the most FSH-sensitive follicle continues to develop and produces a large amount of estradiol and inhibin B, while the rest (99%) become atretic.\",\"s\":[[\"L33_0\",[7]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F359\"},{\"id\":\"F360\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":2,\"L\":3,\"ty\":[\"Pharm\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Enzyme-inducing antiseizure drugs\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which antiseizure medication is a clinically important hepatic enzyme inducer that can reduce the effectiveness of some hormonal contraceptives?\",\"o\":[\"Carbamazepine\",\"Levetiracetam\",\"Sodium valproate\",\"Lamotrigine\",\"Gabapentin\"],\"a\":0,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Carbamazepine induces CYP3A4 and other hepatic enzymes, accelerating metabolism of contraceptive steroids and increasing contraceptive-failure risk.\",\"Levetiracetam is not a clinically important hepatic enzyme inducer and has relatively few pharmacokinetic interactions with hormonal contraception.\",\"Valproate is highly teratogenic but does not cause the classic contraceptive interaction through hepatic enzyme induction.\",\"Lamotrigine is not an enzyme inducer; instead, estrogen-containing contraception can increase lamotrigine clearance and lower its concentration.\",\"Gabapentin is renally eliminated and does not significantly induce hepatic enzymes that metabolize contraceptive steroids.\"],\"e\":[\"Antiseizure drugs differ importantly in enzyme-inducing potential, teratogenicity, and interactions with contraception.\",\"Carbamazepine accelerates hepatic metabolism of estrogen and progestogen, so standard oral contraceptive exposure may be reduced.\",\"Induction of cytochrome P450 enzymes increases steroid clearance; contraceptive planning therefore favors methods not compromised by enzyme induction when appropriate.\"],\"tk\":\"Enzyme-inducing antiseizure therapy must be considered explicitly when choosing contraception before and after pregnancy.\",\"pr\":\"Teratogenicity and enzyme induction are separate properties: valproate is strongly teratogenic without being the classic enzyme-inducing contraceptive-interaction drug.\",\"tp\":\"At medication review, document the exact antiseizure drug and check contraceptive interactions and preconception folate planning before changing therapy.\",\"cn\":\"Carbamazepine, phenytoin and phenobarbital are hepatic enzyme-inducing antiepileptic drugs (AEDs) that reduce hormonal contraceptive efficacy.\",\"sl\":\"Enzyme-inducing antiepileptic drugs (AEDs): phenobarbital, phenytoin, carbamazepine; non-enzyme-inducing: valproate, lamotrigine, gabapentin, ethosuximide, clonazepam. Enzyme inducers reduce the efficacy of the combined oral contraceptive pill (COCP), minipill and Depo-Provera; Mirena is ideal.\",\"s\":[[\"L03_0\",[9,14]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F360\"},{\"id\":\"F361\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":8,\"L\":14,\"ty\":[\"Dx\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Vulvovaginal candidiasis\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A woman has intense vulvar pruritus and thick white vaginal discharge. Which additional finding would be least consistent with vulvovaginal candidiasis?\",\"o\":[\"Vulvar erythema and edema\",\"Vaginal pH of 4.2\",\"Excoriations from scratching\",\"Budding yeast or pseudohyphae on microscopy\",\"Frothy green discharge with a strawberry cervix\"],\"a\":4,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Vulvar erythema and edema are common inflammatory findings in symptomatic Candida vaginitis.\",\"Candida vaginitis usually preserves an acidic vaginal pH below 4.5, so pH 4.2 fits the pruritic thick-discharge presentation.\",\"Severe Candida-associated pruritus can cause excoriations and fissuring from scratching.\",\"Microscopy may demonstrate budding yeast or pseudohyphae, providing direct evidence of Candida.\",\"Frothy green discharge and a strawberry cervix are associated with trichomoniasis, not the thick pruritic Candida syndrome described.\"],\"e\":[\"Vulvovaginal candidiasis commonly causes pruritus, vulvar inflammation, and thick white discharge while vaginal pH remains in the normal acidic range.\",\"A frothy green discharge with strawberry cervix instead describes inflammatory trichomonal vaginitis.\",\"Candida overgrowth produces local mucosal inflammation without the characteristic pH elevation caused by organisms associated with bacterial vaginosis or trichomoniasis.\"],\"tk\":\"Vaginitis differentiation uses discharge character, pH, microscopy, and cervical findings rather than discharge color alone.\",\"pr\":\"A vaginal pH above 4.5 should prompt consideration of bacterial vaginosis or trichomoniasis rather than uncomplicated Candida.\",\"tp\":\"Before empiric recurrent-yeast treatment, obtain focused examination and testing when symptoms are atypical or repeatedly recur.\",\"cn\":\"Frothy discharge with a strawberry cervix indicates trichomoniasis, not vulvovaginal candidiasis.\",\"sl\":\"Candidiasis: thick white curd-like non-offensive discharge, vulval itching, erythema, fissuring, oedema. Trichomoniasis: very foul-smelling discharge (thin and scanty to yellow, profuse and thick), itching, dysuria; classic strawberry cervix.\",\"s\":[[\"L14_0\",[8,12,14]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F361\"},{\"id\":\"F362\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":3,\"L\":24,\"ty\":[\"Dx\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Asymmetrical fetal growth restriction\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Ultrasound shows a fetus with preserved head circumference but a disproportionately reduced abdominal circumference. Which growth pattern best explains these findings?\",\"o\":[\"Asymmetrical fetal growth restriction\",\"Symmetrical fetal growth restriction\",\"Fetal macrosomia\",\"Constitutionally large fetus\",\"Isolated macrocephaly\"],\"a\":0,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Asymmetrical FGR commonly preserves head growth while reducing abdominal circumference because placental insufficiency limits hepatic glycogen deposition and subcutaneous fat.\",\"Symmetrical FGR reduces head and abdominal measurements proportionally and is more typical of early intrinsic fetal insults.\",\"Macrosomia generally enlarges abdominal circumference, especially with fetal hyperinsulinemia, rather than producing a small abdomen with preserved head size.\",\"A constitutionally large fetus tracks high growth centiles across measurements instead of showing disproportionate abdominal restriction.\",\"Macrocephaly requires an enlarged head circumference; the head measurement here is normal while the abdomen is small.\"],\"e\":[\"Late placental insufficiency often produces asymmetrical fetal growth restriction with reduced abdominal growth before head growth is affected.\",\"Preserved head circumference with low abdominal circumference reflects the classic head-sparing pattern.\",\"Chronic hypoxemia redistributes cardiac output toward the brain and heart while reduced hepatic perfusion and nutrient storage shrink the abdomen.\"],\"tk\":\"Head-sparing FGR reflects fetal adaptation to placental insufficiency and does not imply that cerebral risk is absent.\",\"pr\":\"The abdominal circumference is particularly sensitive to impaired fetal nutrition because liver size and subcutaneous fat change early.\",\"tp\":\"Plot serial biometry and combine growth velocity with umbilical artery Doppler and fetal surveillance rather than judging FGR from a single scan.\",\"cn\":\"Preserved head circumference with a disproportionately small abdominal circumference indicates asymmetrical fetal growth restriction (FGR).\",\"sl\":\"Asymmetric (about 70 to 80%): abdominal circumference (AC) falls first (reduced liver glycogen) while the head is spared by blood-flow redistribution; typical of placental insufficiency.\",\"s\":[[\"L24_0\",[12]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F362\"},{\"id\":\"F363\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":2,\"L\":3,\"ty\":[\"Complication\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Antiseizure medication teratogenicity\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which finding is a minor dysmorphic feature rather than a major structural congenital malformation associated with in-utero exposure to teratogenic antiseizure medication?\",\"o\":[\"Neural tube defect\",\"Cleft lip or palate\",\"Congenital cardiac defect\",\"Hypertelorism\",\"Hypospadias\"],\"a\":3,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"A neural tube defect is a major structural malformation, with valproate exposure carrying a particularly important association.\",\"Orofacial clefting is a major congenital malformation reported with several older antiseizure medications.\",\"A congenital cardiac defect is a major organ malformation included among recognized antiseizure-medication teratogenic outcomes.\",\"Hypertelorism is a craniofacial dysmorphic feature; it is not itself a major structural organ malformation.\",\"Hypospadias is a structural urogenital malformation and therefore differs from a minor facial dysmorphic feature.\"],\"e\":[\"Prenatal exposure to some antiseizure medicines can produce both major congenital malformations and minor craniofacial dysmorphism.\",\"Hypertelorism alters facial spacing but does not represent a major structural defect such as neural-tube, cardiac, or urogenital malformation.\",\"Teratogenic risk varies by drug and dose because interference with folate-dependent development, oxidative pathways, and embryonic signaling differs among antiseizure agents.\"],\"tk\":\"Preconception antiseizure-medication review should minimize fetal teratogenic risk while maintaining maternal seizure control.\",\"pr\":\"Valproate carries a particularly high risk of major congenital malformations and adverse neurodevelopmental outcomes.\",\"tp\":\"Do not abruptly stop antiseizure therapy in pregnancy; coordinate medication changes with neurology and maternal-fetal medicine before conception when possible.\",\"cn\":\"Hypertelorism is a minor feature of fetal anticonvulsant syndrome; neural tube defects, cleft lip/palate and heart defects are major abnormalities.\",\"sl\":\"Fetal anticonvulsant syndrome: major abnormalities are microcephaly, cleft lip and palate, neural tube defect (NTD), congenital heart defects, intrauterine growth restriction (IUGR) and developmental delay; minor abnormalities are hypertelorism, distal digital and nail hypoplasia, flat nasal bridge, low-set ears, epicanthic fold and long philtrum.\",\"s\":[[\"L03_0\",[6]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F363\"},{\"id\":\"F364\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":3,\"L\":24,\"ty\":[\"Cause\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Symmetrical fetal growth restriction\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which condition is classically associated with symmetrical fetal growth restriction?\",\"o\":[\"Late-onset placental insufficiency\",\"Maternal chronic hypertension developing near term\",\"Post-term placental aging\",\"Fetal chromosomal abnormality\",\"Isolated uterine artery vasoconstriction in late pregnancy\"],\"a\":3,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Late placental insufficiency more often produces asymmetrical restriction because blood flow is redistributed to preserve cerebral growth.\",\"Near-term maternal hypertension commonly restricts growth through placental insufficiency, which tends to produce a head-sparing pattern.\",\"Post-term placental aging is a late gestational process associated with oligohydramnios and wasting rather than early global proportional restriction.\",\"An early chromosomal abnormality can impair cell proliferation throughout the fetus, producing proportionate restriction of head, abdomen, and long-bone growth.\",\"A late uteroplacental perfusion defect primarily limits nutrient delivery after much organogenesis has occurred, favoring disproportionate abdominal restriction.\"],\"e\":[\"Symmetrical FGR reflects proportionate reduction across fetal biometric parameters and often begins early in gestation.\",\"A fetal chromosomal abnormality can impair growth from early development, unlike later placental disease that preferentially reduces abdominal size.\",\"Early disruption of cell number and organ development affects the entire fetus before adaptive head-sparing redistribution becomes dominant.\"],\"tk\":\"Early symmetrical FGR should trigger consideration of aneuploidy, congenital infection, and major fetal anomalies.\",\"pr\":\"Symmetry alone does not establish etiology; gestational age at onset and structural ultrasound findings refine the differential.\",\"tp\":\"When early symmetrical restriction is confirmed, review dating, perform a detailed anomaly scan, and discuss genetic evaluation when clinically indicated.\",\"cn\":\"Symmetrical fetal growth restriction (FGR) is classically caused by early fetal insults such as chromosomal abnormality, syndromes or congenital infection.\",\"sl\":\"Symmetric (about 20 to 30%): all parameters small from early on; think aneuploidy, syndromes, infection (cytomegalovirus, CMV), drugs, or a constitutionally small fetus.\",\"s\":[[\"L24_0\",[12,18]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F364\"},{\"id\":\"F365\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":3,\"L\":24,\"ty\":[\"Physio\",\"Cause\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Fetal brain-sparing redistribution\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"In placental insufficiency causing asymmetrical fetal growth restriction, which organ is relatively spared by adaptive blood-flow redistribution?\",\"o\":[\"Liver\",\"Subcutaneous tissue\",\"Brain\",\"Abdominal viscera\",\"Skeletal muscle\"],\"a\":2,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"The liver receives less nutrient-rich umbilical venous flow during placental insufficiency, reducing glycogen stores and abdominal circumference.\",\"Subcutaneous fat is depleted as nutrient supply falls, contributing to the thin appearance of growth-restricted neonates.\",\"Fetal redistribution preferentially preserves cerebral perfusion, producing relative brain sparing despite reduced somatic and abdominal growth.\",\"Reduced splanchnic perfusion limits abdominal-organ growth and is reflected by a falling abdominal circumference.\",\"Skeletal muscle mass falls when chronic nutrient and oxygen delivery are limited, reducing lean tissue growth.\"],\"e\":[\"Placental insufficiency can trigger centralization of fetal circulation toward organs essential for immediate survival.\",\"The brain is relatively spared while hepatic, adipose, and muscle growth fall, explaining preserved head size with a smaller abdomen.\",\"Hypoxemia lowers cerebrovascular resistance, producing a reduced middle cerebral artery pulsatility index as part of the compensatory redistribution response.\"],\"tk\":\"Brain-sparing physiology is an adaptive marker of fetal compromise, not proof of normal neurologic prognosis.\",\"pr\":\"A low cerebroplacental ratio can indicate redistribution even when umbilical artery Doppler abnormalities are modest.\",\"tp\":\"Interpret cerebral Doppler findings together with gestational age, umbilical Doppler, growth velocity, and the complete fetal surveillance picture.\",\"cn\":\"In placental insufficiency, fetal blood flow is redistributed to protect the brain (brain sparing), heart and adrenals.\",\"sl\":\"Circulatory redistribution (brain sparing): flow is protected to the brain (middle cerebral artery (MCA) vasodilation), heart and adrenals, and sacrificed in kidneys, liver and gut.\",\"s\":[[\"L24_0\",[23]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F365\"},{\"id\":\"F366\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":8,\"L\":36,\"ty\":[\"Pharm\",\"Mx\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Pharmacotherapy for overactive bladder\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A 34-year-old woman with overactive bladder has persistent urgency and frequency despite an adequate bladder-training program. She has no cognitive impairment, glaucoma, or significant anticholinergic burden. Which medication is an appropriate first pharmacologic option?\",\"o\":[\"Duloxetine\",\"Immediate-release oxybutynin\",\"Desmopressin\",\"Vaginal estrogen alone\",\"Intravesical botulinum toxin A as first treatment\"],\"a\":1,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Duloxetine increases urethral sphincter activity and has a limited role in stress urinary incontinence rather than urgency-predominant overactive bladder.\",\"Oxybutynin is an antimuscarinic that reduces detrusor activity; in a younger woman without anticholinergic vulnerability it is a reasonable low-cost option after behavioral therapy.\",\"Desmopressin reduces nocturnal urine production and may help selected nocturia, but it does not directly treat the detrusor overactivity driving daytime urgency.\",\"Vaginal estrogen can improve genitourinary syndrome of menopause and associated urinary symptoms, but this 34-year-old lacks menopausal atrophy.\",\"Botulinum toxin A is considered after behavioral and oral drug therapy have not worked or are not tolerated.\"],\"e\":[\"Behavioral therapy is first-line for overactive bladder, followed by an appropriate bladder medicine when symptoms remain troublesome.\",\"In a young woman without anticholinergic risk factors, oxybutynin can reduce urgency and frequency after unsuccessful bladder training.\",\"Antimuscarinic blockade reduces M3-mediated detrusor contraction during filling, increasing functional bladder capacity and decreasing urgency episodes.\"],\"tk\":\"Overactive-bladder pharmacotherapy should follow bladder training and account for cognitive risk, comorbidity, and total anticholinergic burden.\",\"pr\":\"NICE advises against immediate-release oxybutynin in older women at higher risk of sudden physical or mental deterioration.\",\"tp\":\"Review response and adverse effects about 4 weeks after starting an overactive-bladder medicine and switch agents if benefit is inadequate or toxicity is troublesome.\",\"cn\":\"After failed bladder training, immediate-release oxybutynin is the first-line drug for overactive bladder (OAB).\",\"sl\":\"National Institute for Health and Care Excellence (NICE): bladder retraining for a minimum of 6 weeks; if no benefit, antimuscarinics; first-line drug is immediate-release oxybutynin; if not tolerated, darifenacin, solifenacin, tolterodine, trospium or extended-release or transdermal oxybutynin.\",\"s\":[[\"L36_0\",[84]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F366\"},{\"id\":\"F367\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":4,\"L\":9,\"ty\":[\"Mx\",\"Criteria\"],\"lv\":3,\"ti\":\"Major postpartum hemorrhage resuscitation\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"During resuscitation for major postpartum hemorrhage with coagulopathy, which laboratory target is inappropriate?\",\"o\":[\"Platelet count above 50 × 10^9/L\",\"Prothrombin time less than 1.5 times normal\",\"Fibrinogen concentration at least 2 g/L\",\"Activated partial thromboplastin time greater than 1.5 times normal\",\"Activated partial thromboplastin time less than 1.5 times normal\"],\"a\":3,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"A platelet count above 50 × 10^9/L supports clot formation during active major obstetric hemorrhage.\",\"Keeping PT below 1.5 times normal reflects adequate correction of major coagulation-factor depletion.\",\"Obstetric hemorrhage can rapidly consume fibrinogen, and maintaining approximately 2 g/L or higher is an important hemostatic target.\",\"A prolonged aPTT above 1.5 times normal indicates persisting coagulation-factor deficiency; resuscitation aims to correct rather than maintain this abnormality.\",\"An aPTT below 1.5 times normal is compatible with correction of significant coagulopathy during hemorrhage resuscitation.\"],\"e\":[\"Major postpartum hemorrhage requires simultaneous control of bleeding and correction of shock, anemia, hypothermia, acidosis, and coagulopathy.\",\"An aPTT greater than 1.5 times normal is evidence of inadequate hemostatic correction, not a therapeutic goal.\",\"Continued hemorrhage consumes and dilutes clotting factors, platelets, and especially fibrinogen, so goal-directed blood-component replacement is used alongside uterotonic and procedural control.\"],\"tk\":\"Hemostatic resuscitation in PPH aims to correct prolonged clotting times and preserve platelets and fibrinogen while definitive bleeding control proceeds.\",\"pr\":\"Fibrinogen may fall early in severe obstetric hemorrhage; a low fibrinogen concentration predicts progression to severe PPH.\",\"tp\":\"Activate the local major-obstetric-hemorrhage protocol early, warm the patient and blood products, and repeat coagulation studies during ongoing bleeding.\",\"cn\":\"In major postpartum haemorrhage (PPH), activated partial thromboplastin time (APTT) should be kept below 1.5 times normal.\",\"sl\":\"Therapeutic goals: haemoglobin (Hb) above 8 g/dL, platelet count above 50 x 10^9/L, prothrombin time (PT) less than 1.5 times normal, activated partial thromboplastin time (APTT) less than 1.5 times normal, fibrinogen above 2 g/L.\",\"s\":[[\"L09_0\",[12]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F367\"},{\"id\":\"F368\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":2,\"L\":4,\"ty\":[\"Dx\",\"Criteria\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Hyperemesis gravidarum\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which laboratory abnormality is least typical of hyperemesis gravidarum caused by prolonged vomiting?\",\"o\":[\"Hypokalemia\",\"Hypernatremia\",\"Ketonuria\",\"Hypochloremic metabolic alkalosis\",\"Elevated hematocrit from dehydration\"],\"a\":1,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Vomiting and secondary renal potassium losses commonly produce hypokalemia during substantial volume depletion.\",\"Hypernatremia is not the typical electrolyte pattern in hyperemesis; prolonged vomiting more often causes volume depletion with hypokalemia and may produce hyponatremia, although severe free-water loss can occasionally raise sodium.\",\"Reduced oral intake can produce starvation ketosis with urinary ketones; ketonuria is not required for diagnosis or severity grading.\",\"Loss of gastric hydrochloric acid can cause hypochloremia and metabolic alkalosis, particularly with persistent vomiting.\",\"Intravascular volume contraction can cause hemoconcentration, including a relatively elevated hematocrit.\"],\"e\":[\"Hyperemesis gravidarum is severe persistent nausea and vomiting that can cause dehydration, electrolyte disturbance, weight loss, and nutritional deficiency.\",\"Prolonged vomiting typically produces potassium and chloride depletion rather than hypernatremia as a characteristic finding.\",\"Gastric hydrogen and chloride loss promotes metabolic alkalosis, while poor caloric intake causes lipolysis and ketosis; severe prolonged disease can also lead to thiamine deficiency.\"],\"tk\":\"Hyperemesis assessment should focus on hydration, electrolyte disturbance, nutrition, and alternative causes rather than requiring ketonuria for diagnosis.\",\"pr\":\"Give thiamine before dextrose in women with prolonged vomiting or poor nutritional intake to reduce the risk of Wernicke encephalopathy.\",\"tp\":\"Check weight, orthostatic status, electrolytes, renal function, and ability to tolerate oral intake before deciding on outpatient versus inpatient treatment.\",\"cn\":\"Hyperemesis gravidarum causes hyponatraemia, hypokalaemia, hypochloraemic alkalosis, ketonuria and raised haematocrit, not hypernatraemia.\",\"sl\":\"Hyperemesis gravidarum: intractable vomiting, dehydration, alkalosis, hypokalaemia, hyponatraemia and weight loss usually more than 5% of pre-pregnant weight, plasma volume depletion with elevated haematocrit, and ketonuria.\",\"s\":[[\"L04_0\",[4]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":2,\"rs\":\"F368\"},{\"id\":\"F369\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":2,\"L\":10,\"ty\":[\"Complication\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Diabetes in pregnancy and neonatal glucose\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which statement about maternal diabetes and the fetus or newborn is false?\",\"o\":[\"Maternal hyperglycemia can cause fetal hyperglycemia\",\"Neonate born to a diabetic woman typically develops hyperglycemia immediately after birth\",\"Poor glycemic control around conception increases congenital-malformation risk\",\"Insulin requirements often increase in the second half of pregnancy\",\"Diabetic retinopathy alone is not an absolute contraindication to vaginal birth\"],\"a\":1,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Maternal glucose crosses the placenta, so maternal hyperglycemia produces fetal hyperglycemia and stimulates fetal pancreatic insulin secretion.\",\"After delivery, maternal glucose supply stops while fetal hyperinsulinemia persists, so the characteristic early neonatal problem is hypoglycemia rather than hyperglycemia.\",\"Pre-existing diabetes with poor periconceptional glycemic control increases major congenital-malformation risk during organogenesis.\",\"Placental hormones generate progressive insulin resistance, so insulin requirements often rise during later pregnancy in pre-existing diabetes.\",\"Diabetic retinopathy requires ophthalmic surveillance during pregnancy, but it is not by itself an absolute indication for cesarean delivery.\"],\"e\":[\"Maternal diabetes affects pregnancy through placental glucose transfer, fetal insulin secretion, and maternal vascular disease.\",\"At birth, interruption of maternal glucose while neonatal insulin remains elevated makes neonatal hypoglycemia a characteristic early complication.\",\"Chronic fetal hyperinsulinemia promotes growth and suppresses endogenous glucose after cord clamping; poor early-pregnancy maternal control can independently disrupt organogenesis.\"],\"tk\":\"Neonatal glucose surveillance is required after diabetic pregnancy because persistent fetal hyperinsulinemia can cause early hypoglycemia.\",\"pr\":\"Congenital-malformation risk is tied most strongly to glycemic control during organogenesis in pre-existing diabetes, not to gestational diabetes first diagnosed later.\",\"tp\":\"Arrange early neonatal feeding and glucose monitoring after birth and document maternal diabetes type, treatment, and late-pregnancy control for the neonatal team.\",\"cn\":\"Infants of diabetic mothers develop neonatal hypoglycaemia, not hyperglycaemia, because fetal hyperinsulinaemia persists after cord clamping.\",\"sl\":\"Fetal complications of diabetes include macrosomia, polyhydramnios, shoulder dystocia, neonatal hypoglycaemia, polycythaemia and jaundice. Maternal then fetal hyperglycaemia causes fetal hyperinsulinaemia; diabetic retinopathy should not be considered a contraindication to vaginal birth.\",\"s\":[[\"L10_0\",[7,16,22,26]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F369\"},{\"id\":\"F370\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":6,\"L\":33,\"ty\":[\"Dx\",\"Criteria\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Core premenstrual disorder\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A woman has recurrent irritability, breast tenderness, bloating, and low mood confined to the luteal phase. Symptoms resolve shortly after menstruation starts, and she is symptom-free during the follicular phase. What is the best classification?\",\"o\":[\"Premenstrual exacerbation of a baseline psychiatric disorder\",\"Primary dysmenorrhea\",\"Generalized anxiety disorder\",\"Core premenstrual disorder\",\"Major depressive disorder\"],\"a\":3,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Premenstrual exacerbation describes a baseline condition present throughout the cycle that predictably worsens premenstrually; this patient is symptom-free between episodes.\",\"Primary dysmenorrhea is prostaglandin-mediated cramping pain around the onset of menstruation rather than a luteal cluster of mood and somatic symptoms.\",\"Generalized anxiety disorder causes persistent excessive anxiety across months and is not restricted to the luteal phase.\",\"Core premenstrual disorder has cyclical symptoms confined to the luteal phase with a symptom-free interval after menstruation and is not simply worsening of another chronic disorder.\",\"Major depressive disorder produces sustained depressive symptoms and functional impairment rather than complete resolution with menstruation.\"],\"e\":[\"Premenstrual disorders are defined by cyclic timing, not by one particular symptom.\",\"Luteal-phase symptoms that resolve with menstruation and leave a symptom-free follicular interval fit core premenstrual disorder.\",\"Sensitivity to normal fluctuations in ovarian steroids and serotonergic signaling appears more important than abnormal absolute hormone concentrations.\"],\"tk\":\"Prospective daily symptom recording across at least two cycles helps confirm that symptoms are truly cyclical.\",\"pr\":\"Premenstrual exacerbation differs because the underlying psychiatric or medical disorder remains present outside the premenstrual window.\",\"tp\":\"Ask the patient to chart symptoms and functional impairment prospectively before committing to long-term pharmacologic treatment.\",\"cn\":\"Cyclical luteal-phase symptoms with a symptom-free follicular week and no underlying disorder define core premenstrual disorder (PMD).\",\"sl\":\"Core premenstrual disorder (PMD) per the International Society for Premenstrual Disorders (ISPMD): cyclical, resolving during or shortly after menstruation with a symptom-free week, prospectively rated, not an exacerbation of an underlying disorder, and impairing daily life. Premenstrual exacerbation is a variant PMD.\",\"s\":[[\"L33_0\",[34,36,37]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F370\"},{\"id\":\"F371\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":6,\"L\":34,\"ty\":[\"Mx\",\"Pharm\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Menopausal hormone therapy with intact uterus\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A postmenopausal woman with bothersome vasomotor symptoms has an intact uterus and no contraindication to menopausal hormone therapy. Which systemic regimen protects the endometrium while treating her symptoms?\",\"o\":[\"Estrogen combined with an adequate progestogen\",\"Estrogen alone\",\"Progestogen alone as standard first-choice systemic replacement\",\"Testosterone alone\",\"Aromatase inhibitor therapy\"],\"a\":0,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Systemic estrogen plus progestogen treats vasomotor symptoms while the progestogen opposes estrogen-driven endometrial proliferation in a woman with a uterus.\",\"Unopposed systemic estrogen increases endometrial hyperplasia and cancer risk when the uterus is present.\",\"A progestogen-only regimen is not the standard replacement strategy for vasomotor symptoms when estrogen can be used.\",\"Testosterone is not a substitute for systemic menopausal estrogen-progestogen therapy and has no role in routine endometrial protection.\",\"An aromatase inhibitor lowers estrogen and is used in selected hormone-sensitive malignancy settings, not menopausal symptom replacement.\"],\"e\":[\"Systemic menopausal hormone therapy must account for whether the endometrium is present.\",\"A woman with an intact uterus generally needs progestogen with systemic estrogen to prevent unopposed endometrial stimulation.\",\"Estrogen promotes endometrial proliferation through nuclear estrogen receptors; periodic or continuous progestogenic opposition induces secretory change and limits hyperplasia.\"],\"tk\":\"Endometrial protection is mandatory when systemic estrogen is prescribed to most women with an intact uterus.\",\"pr\":\"After total hysterectomy, estrogen-only therapy may be appropriate when there is no separate indication for a progestogen.\",\"tp\":\"Before prescribing systemic hormone therapy, review bleeding history, thromboembolic and cancer risk, blood pressure, and whether the uterus is present.\",\"cn\":\"Women with a uterus on systemic hormone replacement therapy (HRT) need a progestogen with estrogen to prevent endometrial hyperplasia and cancer.\",\"sl\":\"Intact uterus: combined hormone replacement therapy (HRT), cyclical in perimenopause and continuous combined in postmenopause; no uterus: oestrogen only. Unopposed oestrogen increases the risk of endometrial cancer.\",\"s\":[[\"L34_1\",[11,31]],[\"L34_0\",[31]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F371\"},{\"id\":\"F372\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":6,\"L\":33,\"ty\":[\"Dx\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Primary dysmenorrhea\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A young woman has recurrent cramping lower abdominal pain beginning just before menstrual bleeding and lasting 8–72 hours. Pelvic examination is normal. What is the most likely diagnosis?\",\"o\":[\"Endometriosis\",\"Pelvic inflammatory disease\",\"Adenomyosis\",\"Primary dysmenorrhea\",\"Mittelschmerz\"],\"a\":3,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Endometriosis can cause secondary dysmenorrhea, dyspareunia, and infertility, often with pain that becomes progressive or extends beyond the first menstrual days.\",\"Pelvic inflammatory disease causes pelvic pain with findings such as cervical motion tenderness, discharge, fever, or sexually transmitted infection (STI) risk rather than predictable isolated menstrual cramps.\",\"Adenomyosis more often causes secondary dysmenorrhea and heavy bleeding in an older reproductive-age patient, often with a bulky tender uterus.\",\"Primary dysmenorrhea causes cyclic cramping around the onset of menses without pelvic pathology, usually beginning in adolescence after ovulatory cycles become established.\",\"Mittelschmerz is brief unilateral mid-cycle ovulatory pain, not 8–72 hours of cramping centered on menstruation.\"],\"e\":[\"Primary dysmenorrhea is painful menstruation without identifiable pelvic disease and classically begins shortly before or with bleeding.\",\"The predictable 8–72-hour cramping pattern with a normal pelvic examination fits primary rather than secondary dysmenorrhea.\",\"Progesterone withdrawal increases endometrial prostaglandin F2α and E2, causing uterine hypercontractility, vasoconstriction, and ischemic pain.\"],\"tk\":\"Primary dysmenorrhea is treated first with non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs) and/or hormonal suppression when no secondary cause is suspected.\",\"pr\":\"NSAIDs work best when started just before or at onset of menses because prostaglandin production drives the pain.\",\"tp\":\"Investigate for secondary causes when pain begins after years of painless cycles, progressively worsens, or accompanies dyspareunia, infertility, abnormal bleeding, or examination findings.\",\"cn\":\"Cramping lower abdominal pain starting just before menses, lasting 8-72 hours, with a normal pelvic exam is primary dysmenorrhea.\",\"sl\":\"Primary dysmenorrhoea presents 6-12 months after menarche with cramping lower abdominal or pelvic pain that may commence before bleeding and usually lasts 8-72 hours.\",\"s\":[[\"L33_0\",[23,25]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F372\"},{\"id\":\"F373\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":4,\"L\":20,\"ty\":[\"Complication\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Postmaturity and oligohydramnios\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which finding is associated with fetal postmaturity in a prolonged pregnancy?\",\"o\":[\"Polyhydramnios\",\"Generalized fetal hydrops\",\"Placenta previa\",\"Diffuse fetal edema from heart failure\",\"Oligohydramnios\"],\"a\":4,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Polyhydramnios is associated with diabetes, impaired fetal swallowing, and selected anomalies rather than the usual post-term fluid pattern.\",\"Hydrops fetalis causes abnormal fluid accumulation in fetal compartments and is not a characteristic consequence of uncomplicated prolonged gestation.\",\"Placenta previa is a low placental implantation disorder that causes painless bleeding and is unrelated to postmaturity physiology.\",\"Fetal edema from heart failure belongs to hydrops physiology rather than placental aging in a post-term pregnancy.\",\"Oligohydramnios becomes more common after term as placental function declines and fetal urine output may decrease.\"],\"e\":[\"Prolonged pregnancy increases exposure to placental senescence, oligohydramnios, meconium passage, and postmaturity features.\",\"Reduced amniotic fluid is a recognized accompaniment of post-term placental insufficiency.\",\"Diminished placental reserve can reduce fetal renal perfusion and urine production, lowering fluid volume and increasing the risk of cord compression during labor.\"],\"tk\":\"Post-term surveillance should consider both fetal wellbeing and amniotic fluid because oligohydramnios can signal reduced placental reserve.\",\"pr\":\"Postmaturity syndrome classically includes dry peeling skin, long nails, reduced subcutaneous fat, and a wasted appearance.\",\"tp\":\"Confirm pregnancy dating before labeling a pregnancy post-term, then follow local policy for surveillance and timing of induction.\",\"cn\":\"Oligohydramnios from placental insufficiency is a feature of postmaturity syndrome in prolonged pregnancy.\",\"sl\":\"Postmaturity syndrome: intrauterine growth restriction (IUGR), meconium-stained amniotic fluid, oligohydramnios, fetal distress, loss of subcutaneous fat, and dry cracked skin reflecting placental insufficiency.\",\"s\":[[\"L20_0\",[5]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F373\"},{\"id\":\"F374\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":0,\"L\":22,\"ty\":[\"Physio\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Early pregnancy hCG secretion\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"In early pregnancy, which tissue is the principal source of human chorionic gonadotropin?\",\"o\":[\"Corpus luteum\",\"Syncytiotrophoblast\",\"Cytotrophoblast\",\"Maternal anterior pituitary\",\"Decidual stromal cells\"],\"a\":1,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"The corpus luteum responds to hCG through the luteinising hormone (LH) receptor and supplies progesterone that supports the early gestation.\",\"The syncytiotrophoblast secretes hCG soon after implantation, maintaining corpus-luteum steroidogenesis until placental hormone production is established.\",\"Cytotrophoblast provides proliferative trophoblastic cells, whereas the fused syncytial layer is the main endocrine source of hCG.\",\"The maternal anterior pituitary secretes follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH) and LH outside pregnancy but does not provide the placental hCG signal.\",\"Decidual cells support implantation and produce local mediators, but they are not the major source of hCG.\"],\"e\":[\"After implantation, placental trophoblast rapidly produces human chorionic gonadotropin.\",\"The endocrine syncytiotrophoblast is the principal source of hCG detected in serum and urine.\",\"hCG binds the LH receptor on luteal cells, rescuing the corpus luteum so progesterone secretion continues until placental steroidogenesis is sufficient.\"],\"tk\":\"Syncytiotrophoblastic hCG production is the hormonal link that maintains the corpus luteum in early gestation.\",\"pr\":\"Serum hCG reflects trophoblastic activity, so an abnormal trajectory can aid assessment of early pregnancy but is not interpreted in isolation.\",\"tp\":\"When evaluating pain or bleeding in early pregnancy, interpret serial hCG together with transvaginal ultrasound and the patient's hemodynamic status.\",\"cn\":\"Human chorionic gonadotrophin (hCG) is produced by the syncytiotrophoblast and rescues the corpus luteum.\",\"sl\":\"Human chorionic gonadotrophin (hCG) source: syncytiotrophoblast; detectable about 8-10 days after conception; doubles every about 48 h and peaks at 8-10 weeks; rescues the corpus luteum until the luteo-placental shift (7-9 weeks).\",\"s\":[[\"L22_0\",[6]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F374\"},{\"id\":\"F375\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":9,\"L\":39,\"ty\":[\"Criteria\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"FIGO cervical cancer stage IA2\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A cervical carcinoma is diagnosed only microscopically and is confined to the cervix. Stromal invasion measures more than 3 mm but no more than 5 mm in depth; horizontal spread is 6 mm. What is the International Federation of Gynecology and Obstetrics (FIGO) stage?\",\"o\":[\"Stage IA1\",\"Stage IB1\",\"Stage IA2\",\"Stage IB2\",\"Stage IIA1\"],\"a\":2,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Stage IA1 is limited to stromal invasion of 3 mm or less, which is shallower than the depth described.\",\"Stage IB1 applies to invasive carcinoma confined to the cervix that is deeper than stage IA and less than 2 cm in greatest dimension.\",\"FIGO stage IA2 is microscopic invasive carcinoma with stromal invasion greater than 3 mm and no more than 5 mm; current FIGO staging no longer uses horizontal extent to subdivide stage IA.\",\"Stage IB2 describes a cervical tumor at least 2 cm but less than 4 cm, not microscopic invasion limited to 5 mm depth.\",\"Stage IIA1 requires extension beyond the cervix to the upper two-thirds of the vagina without parametrial involvement.\"],\"e\":[\"FIGO stage IA is microscopically diagnosed cervical carcinoma with stromal invasion no deeper than 5 mm.\",\"Invasion greater than 3 mm and no more than 5 mm is stage IA2, even when horizontal spread measures 6 mm.\",\"The 2018 FIGO revision removed the previous 7-mm horizontal-width limit, making depth of stromal invasion the defining IA1-versus-IA2 measurement.\"],\"tk\":\"Cervical microinvasive staging depends on measured stromal depth, with IA1 at 3 mm or less and IA2 above 3 mm through 5 mm.\",\"pr\":\"Visible lesions are not classified as stage IA even if they appear small; stage IA requires microscopic diagnosis.\",\"tp\":\"Record pathology depth, tumor size, lymphovascular-space invasion, and imaging findings separately because staging and treatment planning use different risk features.\",\"cn\":\"International Federation of Gynecology and Obstetrics (FIGO) stage IA2 cervical cancer is microscopic invasion over 3 mm to 5 mm deep.\",\"sl\":\"IA2: confined to the cervix, diagnosed by microscopy with invasion of more than 3 mm and less than 5 mm with lateral spread less than 7 mm. IA1 is less than 3 mm deep.\",\"s\":[[\"L39_0\",[27]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F375\"},{\"id\":\"F376\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":6,\"L\":33,\"ty\":[\"Physio\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Inhibin B secretion\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which ovarian cell type is the principal source of inhibin B during the follicular phase?\",\"o\":[\"Granulosa cells\",\"Theca interna cells\",\"Ovarian stromal fibroblasts\",\"Corpus luteum macrophages\",\"Oocytes\"],\"a\":0,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Developing follicular granulosa cells secrete inhibin B, which selectively suppresses pituitary follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH) secretion.\",\"Theca interna cells respond to luteinising hormone (LH) and synthesize androgen precursors for follicular estrogen production.\",\"Ovarian stromal fibroblasts provide structural support and are not the main source of follicular inhibin B.\",\"Luteal macrophages participate in immune regulation and remodeling, not the dominant secretion of inhibin B.\",\"The oocyte directs follicular development through local signaling factors but does not supply the circulating inhibin B feedback signal.\"],\"e\":[\"Inhibin B is produced chiefly by granulosa cells of developing follicles during the follicular phase.\",\"Its rise reflects activity of the growing follicular cohort and contributes to selective FSH suppression.\",\"Inhibin B acts at the anterior pituitary to reduce FSH synthesis and secretion without directly reproducing estradiol's broader hypothalamic-pituitary feedback effects.\"],\"tk\":\"Granulosa-cell inhibin B provides follicular-phase negative feedback specifically on FSH.\",\"pr\":\"Inhibin B is generally more prominent in the follicular phase, whereas inhibin A rises after ovulation from luteinized granulosa cells.\",\"tp\":\"In fertility assessment, interpret ovarian-reserve markers together with age, menstrual history, and antral follicle count rather than using one hormone alone.\",\"cn\":\"Granulosa cells of the growing follicles are the main source of inhibin B in the follicular phase.\",\"sl\":\"The follicular phase shows increasing levels of estradiol and inhibin B; the dominant follicle produces a large amount of estradiol and inhibin B.\",\"s\":[[\"L33_0\",[5,7]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F376\"},{\"id\":\"F377\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":6,\"L\":34,\"ty\":[\"Physio\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Menopausal gonadotropin changes\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which endocrine pattern is expected after natural menopause?\",\"o\":[\"Suppressed serum follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH)\",\"Sustained high luteal progesterone\",\"Increased serum FSH\",\"Increased inhibin B\",\"Increased estradiol from active follicles\"],\"a\":2,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"A suppressed FSH suggests central hypogonadism or exogenous negative feedback rather than ovarian follicle depletion.\",\"Without ovulation or a functional corpus luteum, progesterone remains low after menopause.\",\"Loss of ovarian follicles reduces estradiol and inhibin feedback, so pituitary FSH rises substantially after menopause.\",\"Follicular depletion causes inhibin B to fall, removing an important restraint on FSH secretion.\",\"Natural menopause is characterized by loss of follicular estradiol production, not increased ovarian estradiol.\"],\"e\":[\"Natural menopause follows depletion of the ovarian follicle pool and loss of cyclic estradiol, progesterone, and inhibin production.\",\"Reduced ovarian negative feedback produces a marked rise in FSH, with LH also increasing.\",\"FSH rises particularly prominently because loss of inhibin B removes a selective inhibitory signal in addition to falling estrogen feedback.\"],\"tk\":\"Postmenopausal hypergonadotropic hypogonadism reflects primary ovarian follicle depletion rather than pituitary failure.\",\"pr\":\"Menopause in women aged 45 or older with typical symptoms is usually a clinical diagnosis after 12 months of amenorrhea, without routine FSH testing.\",\"tp\":\"When symptoms or amenorrhea occur at an atypically young age, assess for pregnancy and alternative endocrine causes before labeling menopause.\",\"cn\":\"Follicle depletion at menopause lowers estradiol and inhibin B, so follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH) rises markedly.\",\"sl\":\"Falling follicles reduce estradiol (E2) and inhibin B, raising follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH); in decompensated failure FSH is 10-20 times higher, luteinizing hormone (LH) 3 times higher and E2 drops.\",\"s\":[[\"L34_0\",[9]],[\"L34_1\",[2]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F377\"},{\"id\":\"F378\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":7,\"L\":32,\"ty\":[\"Ix\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Biochemical confirmation of ovulation\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which biochemical test is most useful for confirming that ovulation has occurred in a woman undergoing subfertility evaluation?\",\"o\":[\"Random serum prolactin\",\"Early-follicular testosterone\",\"Mid-luteal serum progesterone\",\"Day-3 luteinising hormone (LH) concentration alone\",\"Serum anti-Müllerian hormone\"],\"a\":2,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Prolactin testing can identify hyperprolactinemia as a cause of ovulatory dysfunction but does not confirm that ovulation occurred in the sampled cycle.\",\"Testosterone helps assess hyperandrogenism but is not a luteal marker of ovulation.\",\"A mid-luteal progesterone level reflects corpus-luteum activity after follicular rupture and is a standard biochemical method for confirming recent ovulation.\",\"A basal luteinising hormone (LH) concentration can vary and cannot demonstrate that follicular rupture and luteinization occurred.\",\"anti-Müllerian hormone (AMH) estimates ovarian follicle quantity and expected ovarian response but does not confirm ovulation in a particular cycle.\"],\"e\":[\"After ovulation, the corpus luteum secretes progesterone, creating a measurable luteal-phase rise.\",\"Sampling serum progesterone in the mid-luteal phase, approximately 7 days before the expected period, provides biochemical evidence of ovulation.\",\"LH-triggered luteinization converts granulosa cells into progesterone-secreting l");